      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>SIX DOCTORS AT AN INQUEST. Tuberculosis or Injury ?</head>
        <p>SIX DOCTORS AT AN INQUEST. Tuberculosis or Injury ?</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIERY CONTROVERSY.</p>
        <p>There was a great array of medical talent at the Huntsman, Haydock, on Monday,when Mr. Brighouse Tesumed the inquest on William Cunliffe, of Clips ley- lane, who died on January 31, and was alleged to have received an injury at one of Messrs. R. Evans and Co.'s pits last, year. Mr. H. L. Riley represented the widow, Mr. T. Glover, J.P., the Miners' Federation, and Mr. E. Peace the colliery company. Six doctors were also present. The Coroner wanted to know why the colliery com- pany allowed the certificate to pass,and did not at once deny and contest the statement that the man had met with an accident. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Peace said that the certificate would go to Mr. George first, but the c idliery people never had any notice of an accident until weeks later. The Coroner said he understood that the accident was alleged to have taken place in April, but the owners contended that they had no notice until some months afterwards,and that placed them in a very awkward posi- tion, because they had no proper opportunity of testing the statements as to the accident. Mr. Riley said the man was paid sick money by Mr. George, the colliery company's secretary, and Mr. George was also secretary of the Coalowners' Insurance Society, so that Mr. George must have known about the man being injured. The Coroner said he had commented on this position of affairs before and he could not help thinking that when an official who occupied a dual position got notice of an occurrence such as this, he could be held to have had notice or knowledge of the matter from the point of view of the Compensation Act. John Jones said he worked near deceased. The accident happened on the last day that Cunliffe worked at the pit. Cunliffe was walking along in front of him when he fell. The Coroner was now handed Dr. Dowling's certificate and said that the only evidence before the jury was that on April 21 deceased was certified to be suffering frotna sprained ankle, and he never worked afterwards. Unless the contrary was proved that was strong presumptive evidence that the man had been hurt. Mr. Peace said Dr. Hayward would tell them that the man's condition was due to general tuberculosis.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said they would have to see if the tuber- culosis originated in the lungs or was first set up in the ankle.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said he did not know how Mr. Peace was going to combat the evidence. _ .</p>
        <p>Mi. Peace said he would call the fireman and others, who would deny conversations attributed to them by the witness.</p>
        <p>- Mr. George gave evidence and said that nnder the sick club rule a man received payment even though he might be hurt in the street or away from work. The Coroner suggested that a printed form should be used which would give the firm a future reference.</p>
        <p>Paul Twist, the fireman, denied any knowledge of the accident, or that it was reported to him. William Smith, a collier, who worked with the deceased, also denied hearing of the accident. Enoch Darwen, hooker-on, said the deceased did not leave the pit until the usual time. Mrs. eunliffe, who had said her husband cams home at dinner time, was recalled and said she could not say the exact time. - - - -</p>
        <p>Dr. Unsworth, who had made a post-mortem in the presence of Drs. Haywooe, Dowling, Jackson, and J. S. Fox, desoribed the condition of the body. In his opin- ion the whole tronblehad arisen from deceased suffering from consumption of the lungs. He thought that at some time deceased had received an injury to the ankle, but it was in no way connected with the death, which was from natural causes. _ _</p>
        <p>Dr. Dowling, surgeon for the New Miners' Permanent Relief Society, entirely disagreed with Dr. Unsworth as to the condition of the body. Witness had treated</p>
        <p>deceased for sprained ankle, which he said was sprained in the pit on the day he first attended him—April 21st. From the post-mortem he had no doubt that deceased met with an accident. There was no" advanced state" of tuberculosis.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: This is a severe blow to the medical profession. The jury and myself will not have so much faith in them after this. Witness said he believed the man died primarily from injury to the left ankle. Dr. Thompson, of Haydock, said that he attended deceased on May 4th, when he told him his ankle had given way several times. There was then no signs of injury to the ankle, and deceased made no reference to an injury in the mine. The jury eventually returned a verdict of "Accidental death," being agreed that he met with an injury on April 21st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>I. 1</head>
        <p>I. 1</p>
        <p>2 FOR DRAMS 17 AvCA BE S McDOUGALL'S Pateo SELF-RAISING FLOUP. The Examiner Saturday, February 13, 1904.</p>
        <p>larger number of branches, in the course of each year, than any society. During the past year they had estab- lished about 90 adult tents and 100 juvenile tents. Sobriety, continued the speaker, meant national progress and as long as the British working man allowed drink to be the chief factor in his life, wretchedness, misery and depression would continue to exist. With the. exception of two out of 130 districts,each district inthe Order had a surplus on the funeral fund. They could also say what no other Order could say, that for every pound they had promised to pay they had a pound to pay it with. (Applause.) Brother Churchill gave other facts'to prove the soundness of the Order and asked the members to exert themselves to increase its strenght. (Applause.) An enjoyable musical programme was submitted. Songs were creditably rendered by Miss Fairhurst and Bro. G. H. Gough, and Miss Bouchier skilfully played violin solos. Bro. Johnson (Heywood) created great merriment with his humorous songs. Miss A. Hill, A.V.C.M. accompanied. During the evening a Past chief ruler's framed emblem was presented by Bro. Churchill to Bro. Donald Reid, of Charity 'I ent.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A very pleasant gathering was the annual congrega- tional tea party in connection with this church which took place in the schoolroom, John-street, on Thursday evening. About 130 persons sat down to a capital tea, the following ladies presiding at the tables : Mesdames Milne, Rees, Neil, lodge, Ditchfleld,Bennett, and Walker, Misses Naylor and Brown.</p>
        <p>The subsr;qiient ent4t-ainment was presided over by I the pastor, the Rev. J. L. Milne, M.A., 8.D., who expressed regret at the absence, through illness, of ; Councillor Dodds who had consented to take the chair. The Rev. John Yonge was also unable to attend, owing to pressure of engagements. An address was delivered by the Rev. W. Dunn, of _Risley, who heartily congratulated the pastor upon haying such a large gathering. Meetings of th it des- cription did a great deal of good as they brought minister and people together and increased their mutual interest. He asked the congregation to work more enthusiastically for the advancement of the church and addressed words of advice to the younger members. In order to succeed they must go forward with the sole aim of working for good and studying to improve them- selves. He urged them to follow *the example of the Saviour, to be courteous, loving and kind to all around them and to help any who chanced to ho in distress. The Rev. J. L. MILNE spoke on the Church motto for the year—" Go forward." -Thera was, he said, work for every church in Warrington to combat the many evils which existed. To"Go forward"was the reason for the Churches existence, and worship without work was imperfect. They must not be overcome by difficulties, but be prepared to meet and overcome those difficulties, An enjoyable programme was submitted. The Misses Chesworth gave instrumental selections in good style, and Miss e. Chesworth rendered a song. Miss Furness and Miss Spencer were responsible for recitations, and Mrsarown and Mr. Potts contributed songs. Mr. Spencer was an efficient accompanist. The usual votes of thanks concluded a successful gathering. ST. THOMAS'S CHURCH, STOCKTON HEATH. ; A goodly company assembled fer the annual tZaparty in connection with thi:: church on Monday evening and the gathering was a distinct success. Tea was served to over 250 persons, and a very interesting and varied entertainment, arranged by Mrs.W. B. Roberts, followed. Mrs. C. Stone sweetly sang "The Toilers" and "Vocal Waltz" and had the honour of a recall. Miss Annie Sutton was heard to advantage in "Angus Mac- donald" and a trio in character from "The silver slipper " was admirably• rendered by the Misses Francomb, Willett, and Whittle. Miss Bouchier contributed a pleasing violin solo. and Mr. F. Pearson creditably sang "Jolly Jenkins." Messrs. E. Reddish, Potts. J. Croughton and F. Pearson gave a quartette " Call John." The entertainment was brought to a close with a farce entitled" Dearest Mamma," by members of the Warrington Dramatic Society. All sustained their parts successfully and the audience was highly amused at the troubles caused in the home of a newly-married couple by the interfer- ence of the much-abused mother-in-law. Atm. James Fairclough was thoroughly at home in the character of Mrs. Breezely-Fussell and she was ably supported by the following :—Harry Clinton, Mr. Geoffrey Dodds: Nettle Crofter, Mr. H. G. Mosley ; Uncle Browser,Mr. Sidney Wallhead ; Jones,Mr. Victor Roberts ; Edith Clinton, Miss Marraret Pierpoint ; and Mrs. Honeywood, Miss N. Fairclough. On the propo- sition of the vicar, the Rev. H. V. Pigot, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the artistes and Mrs. W. B. 'Roberts, for arranging the programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</head>
        <p>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</p>
        <p>UNFURLING OF A NEW BANNER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Visit of Bro. Alderman Dimond and Bro. C. W. Churchill.</p>
        <p>Saturday was a red-letter day in the history of local ilechabitism. For a considerable time the Warring- ton district of this ancient and flourishing Order has felt the need of a banner to he used on the occasion of public demonstrations. At last, thanks to the efforts of an able committee, well backed-up by the body of members, a large, handsome, silk banner has been purchased, and on Saturday it was unfurled at Brick- street mission in the presence of a large assembly. The vitality of the Warrington district is evidenced by the fact that the total adult membership is over 1,240, with funds of upwards of ,E 4,000. In addition there are over 780 juvenile members, and the funds in this section amount to over £3OO.</p>
        <p>Saturday was also the cccasion of the annual dis- trict business meeting which was hel.d in the mission in the afternoon.—Bro. B. Walker, D.D.R., called the Council to order shortly after 3 o'clock, and the roll being called the whole of the officers and repre- sentatives from the eighteen branches, with the excep tion of the D.C.8., responded. There was also a large number of visitors, including 'Bro. C. W. Churchill, H.D.R., Bro. D. Main, H.S.J.T., and Alderman J. Dimond, of Southport, P.Fl.C.R.—After the opening ceremony had been concluded, Bro. Walker, S.D.B. called upon the 11.D.R., to preside.—Bro. Churchill, in responding to the invitation, said he was glad -to be present, and gave a brief and encouraging address to the officers and workers on the position of the Order. Bro Main and Bro. J. Dimond also addressed the meeting. Bro. Winstanley, D. S., submitted the report of the Executive which showed that progress had been made during the year and the funds and membership of the District continued to increase. The numerical strength of the district was 1248 benefit members, being an in- crease of 84 members. The sick expenditure had been exceptionally heavy during the year and after meeting all liabilities there was a gain of £lO6 Os. Od. making the Sick Fund £1,933 2s ld.There had been fiveclaims on: the Funeral Fund, by 3 members and 2 wiVes to the amount of £4O, there being a gain on this fund of £162 17s making the Funeral Fund £1457 lie 71-d; Management Funds £lB5 lie 54t; Special Funds £B9 12s Od. The balance account showed that the funds of the district invested, in savings banks were £335 4s sd, Wigan propertyl79s 6s lid ; Earlestown property £2,349 8s id ; loans on property £3OO, sick and funeral funds of tents over £4OO, cash in Treasurer's hand £24 is 11d; total funds £4,204 is 4d. •The Executive Committee reported that efforts were being made to open several adult branches in the district. The balance sheet and auditor's report, which were considered most satisfactory,were adopted. Bro. W. Wright, D.S.J.T., presented his annual report on the work dose by the Juvenile Section which showed that splendid progress had been made by the juveniles in their different Tents and the district was in a good position both numerically and financially. One new Tent has been opened at Padgate, with good results. The increase for the year, after transferring seven members to the adult section and one by clearance, was 192 making the membership of the district 781, and juvenile funds up to date just over £3OO. The Juvenile Executive reported that they had decided to accept the offer of the Board of Directors to hold the Juvenile Provincial Conference (No. 1) in Warrington about the middle of May next. The officers were elected as follows :—Bros. B. Walker, D.C.R. ; W. Antrobus, D.D.C.R. ; R. Harrison, Levite ; J. Hill, guardian ; T. Massey, treasurer; T. Win- stanley, D.S. ; W. Wright, D.S.J.T. ; T. Hall, J. Shaw and E. Walkden, trustees. The meeting concluded, an excellent tea was served to about 250 members and friends. A public meeting wasaftervrards held, Bro. the Rev. John Yonge presid- ing over a very large attendance. The event of the evening was the unfurling of the banner by Bro. Alder- man Dimond.</p>
        <p>In the course of his opening address the Chairman expressed his gratification at the large attendance which, he hoped, augured well for the coming days of the Order in what was sometimes called "The good old town of Warrington." He was not quite sure whether, from a temperance point of view, Warrington deserved to be spoken of as good. If it was good, the sooner it was better the better. (Hear, hear). He hoped all those present who were believers in the Principles and practices of temperance would do more than ever to promote these principles and practices among their fellow-townsmen. (Applause.) The rev, gentleman drew attention to the fact that in the attractive souvenir of that gathering which had been prepared,the photograph of the Bishop of Liverpool, who is an honorary member of the Order, as well as a portrait of himself (Mr. Yonge) appeared. He did not think he(the speaker)had eves be- fore had the honour of appearing in the same pages with a Bishop of the Church of England. ' It was a matter for congratulation that a minister of Christ, holding that very conspicuous position in the diocese of Liverpool, should have identified himself with the Order. He trusted that his clergy would follow his example. (Hear, hear.) It was not always that the clergy did follow the example of their bishops, or perhaps some of them would not be found walking along paths, in which, in the opinion of some observers, a few of them were erring and straying. He (the speaker) also claimed to be a bishop, and he was rather of opinion that his claim was as valid, according to the New Testament, as that of the Lord Bishop of Liverpool. In the pages of the New Testament he did not think anyone could discover a' church officer of the order of a diocesan bishop, but they could discover many a church officer of the order of a congregational bishop—that was to say, a bishop of a congregation of Christian believers, seeking to teach them in the truths of Christ and to lead them in the ways of God. He believed there were learned bishops of the Church of England who were willing to admit that the congregational idea of a bishop was nearer to the primitive idea than the one which they themselves professed. He hoped that gathering would serve to give a fresh impetus, greater enthusiasm and inspiration to all the members of the order in Warring- ton. The OrGer dated back to 1835. He was informed that in 1861 the membership for the whole of the king- dom was upwards of 6,000. Up to 1903 it had reached the line figure of 186,8.31. (Applause.) He was glad to hear of the growth of the juvenile tents, because the strength of the temper- , ance movement lay in the young people, and it was of the supremest importance that the senior societies should foster and cherish the junior organiza- tions, so that boys and girls could be trained in the principles of total a.bstinence from childhood. There i was certainly plenty of room in Warrington for the (propagation of temperance. He hoped they would see a larger and more earnest spirit infused into the work. He rejoiced in the resuscitation of the Temperance !Federation, and he hoped that under its new manage- ment its history might be brighter and more prosper- ous than it had been hitherto. He trusted that the various temperance societies would always Ibe diligent agitators for all kinds of temper- ance reform and watchful sentinels against all the plottings of the trade which, like a certain ; person should be nameless, was always going about seeking whom it might devour. He was very pleased to see that at the Licensing Sessions on Friday the magis- trates refused a licence which was sought for in the Lovely-lane district. They ought to have refused another licence for that neighbourhood a year ago, but they did not. He hoped they would maintain the stand they had taken u? in regard to the new application. (Applause.) "Before unfurling the batmer, Bro. Alderman Dimond delivered an interesting address. It had, he said, been his privilege on many occasions to visit Warrington but that, he believed, would be a red letter day to all present. When the juvenile movement was started in Warrington he came over, at the request of the brethren and initiated a few juveniles. It was then that juvenile Reehabitism took root in this town, and now it had be- come a very great power in the district. There was no doubt that juvenile rechabitism lent itself to the kindly sympathy of every grown up person, whether he was a teetotaler or a drinker. They strongly be- lieved that the temperance work 'among the children was going to elevate society and to give us a grand, noble, and sober England. (Applause). If only the working classes would expend the money now spent in drink, in useful commodities, it would go a long way towards making the country prosperous without having to "dump on other people's premises." (Applause.) In conclusion Bro. Dimond expressed the hope that the temperance movement would gwell,so that reform in • legislation could be brought about. (Applause.) The banner was then unfurled amid loud applause. and was much admired. It is of beautiful texture and workmanship, and bears the arms of the Order. Brother CHURCHILL also addressed the gathering and • traced the early history of the Order. Since 1861, he said, it had never looked back, and ,they had to thank men like Bro. Dimond for the splendid position it now held. He hosed the new banner would lead the War- I rington distria on to greater victories in the light , which had not yet reached its worst stage, but in which ' Rechabites must take a foremost and important part. They were proud of their great numbers because every member initiated meant another opponent of the drink traffic. That was why he gloried in the magnificent Progress of the juvenile tents where children were instructed in total abstinence principles. The adult financial membership of the Order was well over 200,000 nd the juvenile membership was close upon I 30,000. For fourteen years in succession the Orde ad been able to claim the honour of having opened r '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
