      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SOUVENIR.</head>
        <p>A SOUVENIR.</p>
        <p>Each visitor received a neatly-arranged souvenir which had been printed at the "Examiner "office. It contained excellent photographs of the Bishop of Liverpool (Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse), Bro. Alderman Dimond, P.H.C.R.. P.H.S.J.T., Bro. C. W.lChurchill, P.H.S.J.T.. Bro. the Rev. John Yonge, and Bro. Richardson Campbell (high secretary of the Order), all of whom are esteemed members of the Order. There were also sketches of the Old Friars' Green Chapel,the-early home of total abstinence in Warrington where the first local branch of the Order (Charity Tent No 11) was instituted in May, 1836, and the Brick-street Mission. The booklet also supplied an abundance of information respecting the pozition of the order generally and of the Warrington district in par- ticular. In an address of welcome the Banner Committee wrote :—" We entreat you to take a deeper interest in the future in all the details connected with your tents,to make them into sources of activity in temperance work so that from them my radiate an influence for good, that many may be reclaimed,our young men made strong against the enticements so thickly around them,and our children nurtured in the principles which we have proved to be so beneficial ourselves." It is the earnest wish of the committee, whose efforts have resulted in the purchase of the banner that every tent in the dis- trict will, as opportunity arises, make use of it, and that, as from time to time it is unfurled,and holds aloft its silent witness, it may be a means of spreading forth those principles of the Order, and may do itsishare in bringing many recruits into the ranks of Rechabitism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL TEA PARTIES.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL TEA PARTIES.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL,S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Parr Hall was the scene of a bright and happy gather- ing on Monday evening, when the annual congrega- tional tea party and entertainment in connection with St. Paul's Church were held. The function will rank as one of the most enjoyable of its kind in the history of the Church—a fact that speaks well for the good work of those who were responsible for the preparations. In this connection, a special word of praise is due to Mr. A. H. Burgess, who was an indefatigable hon. secretary. Tea was served to a company of over 300, lady members of the congregation presiding at the tables. Before the entertainment commenced, several speeches on the past year's work were delivered. Alderman Sutton presided, and he was supported by the Vicar, the Rev. W. Bracecamp, the Rev. T. W. Barber, curate, and Messrs. R. J. Cherley and J. H. Smethurst (wardens). The CHAIRMAN, who commented upon the fact that he was perhaps the oldest member of the congregation with the exception of Mr. Geddes, said he had known every incumbent of St. Paul's since the church was built, but not one of them had surpassed Mr. Bracecamp in his desire to benefit the congregation. His heart and soul were in his work, and the church was undoubtedly progressing under his rule and guidance to an extent which had never been previously exceeded, and that was owing to his desire to promote the moral and spiritual welfare of his flock. (Applause). Alderman Sutton also paid a tribute to the work of the wardens (Messrs. R. J. Morley and J. H. Smetherst),the Revs. T.W.Barber and C. J. Edwards, and Mrs. Bracecamp. Mr. J. H. SMETHURST said they would be interested to know that the finances of the church for the past three years had established a record. For•the past ten months the offertories had reached the sum of £354 2s. Bd. Some people would no doubt think they were "rolling in money," but they were never harder up than now, and yet they were never better off. (Laughter.) The £354 had gone and more beside, simply beeause they had acted on the principle of looking ahead. The "church expenses account" had taken up a good sum. They had started a restoration fund, and they would have to decorate the church if they could not rebuild it ; and they had to their credit £55 is. sd. Then there was the endowment fund. Since 1839, when the movement was started they had succeeded in increasing the endowment of the living from £BO to S:110, and if the work went on in the same proportion they would some day be able to say that St. Paul's was free and open. (Applause). They had subscribed liberally to outside objects—to "waifs and strays" £l4 Os. Id-, £3l lOs Easter offertory to the Vicar; £26 14s. 6d. for the Infirmary; Additional Curates Society, £6 10s. 8d; Lifeboat Institution, £8 18s. 3d. [xi conclusion Mr. Smethurst said that as long as the Vicar continued to work as he was doing now and was backed up by the people, so long would St. Paul's continue to flourish. (Applause.) The VICA R briefly addressed the meeting, and said that as long as such a good feeling existed between pastor and people they could not but expect God's blessing to test upon their work. The way the con- gregation had supported the efforts of the clergy and wardens in regard to raising the endowment fund ought to be an encouragement to all Church people, as it showed what a congregation could do when they all worked together- in unity. (Applause) Since their last annual gathering an addition had been made to their clerical staff, in the person of the Rev. T. W. Barber, who was not unknown in Warrington. They heartily welcomed Mr. and Mrs. Barber, and trusted that their work would be greatly blessed. (Applause,) In conclusion Mr. Bracecamp cordially thanked the wardens, sidesrnen, lady workers, and the congregation generally for their support. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Burgess for his services as hon. secretary, on the proposition of the Rev.IIT. W. BARBER, seconded by Alderman SMETHURST. The programme was an exceptionally interesting one, the services of some well-known local artistes having been secured. All acquitted themselves creditably and the audience gavel expression to their appreciation by heartily applauding. Sprightly music was given in fine style by a ladies' orchestra consisting of the Misses V. K. and J. M. Finney, M. Leigh,M. Chesworth,L. Birchall, E. Greenough, Mary Chesworth and M. Parsonage. Miss Arline Harrison sang "The Swallows" very sweetly, and so delighted was the 'audience that an encore was accorded. Miss Mabel Henshall gave piquant render- ings of a "Japanese love song " and " If I could steal thy wings "and she, too, was recalled. Mr. J. Rabbit was in excellent voice and interpreted " Mona " so expressively that a demand for" more 'had to be com- plied with. Not less successful was Mr. Fred Field- house who gave" A soldier's song" with spirit. Mr. R. H. Pearson played a violin solo with his usual taste and skill. The choir contributed a creditable part song. Two items which gained ' ' special approval were the Chinese character songs "Chin Chin Chinaman" ("Geisha"), and "Chinee sojer man" ("San Toy ") by Masters F. Knowles. T. Bow- cock, C. Short, W. Ankland, T. Smith and C. Warburton who gave the same aperforma,nce with great success at People's College Concert. Their humorous antics created general merriment. Mr. White completely mystified his audience by remarkable "Sleight of hand." Mr. H. M. Ashton gave as a humorous recital Artemus Ward's "Lecture on the Mormons" and was as enter- taining as ever. Mr. P. N. Avery scored a success with his humorous songs. The concluding item was a quartette "When the bedding bloom of May.' in which the voices of the Misses Arline Harrison and Mabel Henshall and Messrs. A. H. Burgess and F. Fieldhonae blended effectively. The duties of accompanist were discharged by Mrs. Avery, Miss Finney and Messrs. E. Wilkes and W. H. Payton. A largely attended dance in connection with the parish was held in the Parr Hall-on Tuesday evening, the music being et-Tolled by Mr. Anderton's band. Messrs. W. T. Flood, G. Freeman. A. C. Binks, ' J. Houghton, J. Bate. W. Thompson, H. B. Houghton and G. H. Shaw acted as M.C.'s. ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>I The annual congregational tea party in connection I with this church was held in the schoolroom on Monday I evening, over 200 persons partaking of tea. Afterwards lan entertainment was held, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton presiding. The Chairman expressed pleasure that Miss Bee (headmistress of the Liverpool-road, school) was now recovering from her serious illness, and he hoped . she would soon be able to be amongst them as usual. ' (Applause.) A capital progranitne was submitted in- chiding instrumental selections by the Misses Pooley I and Warren,gongs by Miss Longdin, Miss Ball and Mr. I Sutcliffe, anda recitation by Miss Beesley. Mr. Hignett contributed humorous songs and amusing dialogues entitled " Thor Tutor" and "Box and Cox." were well given by chers and friends. Mr. Percy Randall ' iweonmanied.</p>
      </div>
