      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Two papers, of more than usual interest, were read before the members of the Literary and Philosophical Society on Manz:ay evening. The first was read by Mr. Charles White, whose subject was " The First University." He referred to Salerno and its early school of medicine. This university was established in 1150 and was famous in the Middle Ages. Mr. White proceeded to speak of the Monastery of Monte Cassino, the original cloister of the Benedictines, founded in 528. Mr. White was cordially thanked for his paper on the proposition of Mr. May. In referring to the valuable medical library at the Infirmary, Mr. May said he had the privilege of seeing some of those beautiful old books, and heaped , that some day Mr. White would take the opportunitir of showing them to that society, as they were the material for a very interesting paper. they was some talk of turning the books into money—as the Infirmary needed it, and he would suggest to the Town Council to make the Infirmary a good substantial offer for them and prevent such a valuable collection going away from the town. Mr. J. R. Young secondpd the motion, and commented upon the reference in the paper to the admission of lady mmb!rs in the medical university. According to the paper, he said, they had justified their admission. He was glad to see that there were signs of progress, and that there was same endeavour to•bring our English universities up to'a modern state.—Mr. G. Carruthers (hon. secretary), Alderman Bennett and Mr. A. J. Jolley also took patt in the , discussion.—An interesting lecture on " Podium" was afterwards delivered- by Mr. It. H. Albery, ,B. Sc., physical 'master at the Secondary Scholl. The lecture was illustrated by numerous experiments, charts and lantern slides, :Ina the properties of radium were fully described. Samples of radium were exhibited and inspected with interest. The President (Mr. rtuddock) remarked that Mr. Alhery's lecture that evening was but the. introduction to •a much longer lecture they hoped to haye;at some future date. -He would like to thank Mr. J: Rymer Yonng for obtaining the samples of radium, and providing the opportuaity for showing them, to a Warrington :audience. Thanks were also due to Mr. Frith for manipulating the lantern. Mr. Albery. was thanked .for his paper, a compliment which Mr Albery acknowledged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RHEUMATISM</head>
        <p>RHEUMATISM</p>
        <p>Was lon,, thought to be a disease of the bones. Modern researcehas, however,proved such a thing to be errone- ous. In reality rheumatism is the outcome of an impure state of the blood. Countless sufferers who have for I years, borne untold misery through rheumatism, have found this out to their cost. Mr. limper, 12, Upper Chapel Street, Tividale, Tipton, writes :—" You will be interested to know that my mother—who is 74 years of age—has recently been restored to health in a most marvellous manner. It was impossible for her to walk even a few steps, so full of rheumatism was she. Having myself proved the value of Dr. Morso's Indian Root Pills—for my blood had been rid of many bad humours by them—l advised her to try some. Her pains left her like magic, and to-day she is full of gratitude for all the good Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills have done." They are a per- fect blood purifier, curing liver and kidney complaints, biliousness, indigestioin, constipation piles, dyspepsia. female ailments, eczema, pimples, and blotches. Sold by leading chemists and stores, price Is. lid. per bottle, or on receipt of price from the W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd.. 21, Farringdon-aveune, London, E.C. FREE SAMPLE. Mention this paper and enclose id. stamp for postage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>,_. WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL</head>
        <p>,_. WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of Widnes Town Council, on Tuesday, the Mayor (Alderman S. Quinn) presiding, on the recommendation of the Electricity Committee, it was unanimously decided to apply to Parliament for an extension of the Widnes Electric Light Order of 1901 with special provision for transfer. It was also decided to delegate the power to manufacture electricity to the Mersey Electric Supply Company under conditions which were described as possibly the best obtainable, and which, it was pointed out by Alderman Timmis, still left it open to the Council or a company to introduce electric trams. The proposed extension to the water pumping plant at Stocks Well to meet growing demands was sanctioned at a cost of R14,000.—0n the recommendation of the General Pur- poses Committee, by-laws relatinr, to street betting,the sale of racing tips, loitering, music near buildings, and hawking were adopted.—A portrait of his Majesty the King, by Mr. Luke Fildes, R.A., having been presented by Aldermln Gessage, the thanks of the Council were given to that gentleman. The Education Committee presented an estimate by which 2d in the 0£ will be added to the local rates.</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total nuyaber of failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Feb. sth, was 178, viz.:—New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 89; deeds of arrangement registered, 89. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 88 ; deeds of arrangement, 84— total 172; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 24, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1901, to date, of 70. •</p>
      </div>
