      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Improvad Funeral Arrangemmts.</p>
        <p>special meeting of the Warrington Board of Guar- dians was held on Wednesday morning. Mr. A. Brooks presided, and there were also present : Mrs. Clay, Rev. Father Wilson. Councillor Ashcroft, Messrs. G. Mather, S. Davies, J. Payton, William Houghton, G. Pilling, A. J. Jolley, T. Ritchie, J. Turton, A. Jolley, and J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master) and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate schools).</p>
        <p>CONGESTED STATE OF THE HOUSE. PAUPER FUNERAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The House Committee, at a meeting on January 29th, resolved that in consideration of the long and faithful services rendered to the Guardians by Mr. S.Greenway, tailor, his weekly wage of 28s. be continued to him during his illness and the Master was anth9rised to re- ceive his son's signature for the receipt of the same, the tailor being unable to sign.—It was resolved that the outfit applied for Hannah Roberts by the Home of Epileptics, Maghull. be supplied, and that Mrs. Clay be appointed to visit the home and see Hannah Roberts.— The Master drew attention to the congested state of the House, especially the male hospital and it was decided to issue special notices for the matter to be dealt with. At the same meeting it was resolved to review the funeral arrangements at the House with a view to their improvement. At a meeting of the Com- mittee on February sth the Master reported upon the existing funeral arrangements and letters urging reform were read from the Revs. W. Bracecamp and Father Ryan. Estimates for carrying out improvements were received from Messrs. Greenough, Connor, and Maginn, and the committee unanimously decided to accept their terms and abolish the arrangements tken in existence. The terms quoted were to provide two horses and a hearse for 10s. per journey to Warrington Cemetery and to provide four suitable bearers at 2s. 6d, each, each of the above firms to have the work quarterly. It was resolved to increase the weekly wage of Walter T. Sampson, electrical engineer, from 345. to 38s. With respect to the overcrowded state of the House, a com- mittee was appointed consisting of. Messrs. Brooks, Pilling, Mather, A. J. Jolley, Ashcroft, and Father Wilson, to go minutely into the matter and report. Mr. G. PILLING, in moving the confirma- tion of the minutes, referred to the proposed improved burial arrangements. He was pleased, he said, that the committee had taken the matter in the right light. The practice observed by the Guardians for many years past had become obsolete. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the Board imanintously approved of the sug- gested change. Mrs. Clay was asked to visit Hannah Roberts at the home for epileptics at Maghull, and on his (the Chairman's) own initiative he had asked the Matron to accompany her. Mrs. Clay was very glad'of the matron's company. With referencelto the:congestedistate of the Workhouse hospital, some of the male patients, he said were sleeping in a portion of the buildings that was really not allowed under the Act. However the Guardians had tojmake the best of the situation;and the special committee would go into the matter thoroughly at an early date. Some different arrangement would have to be made but he hoped it would not involve any large expense. (Hear, hear.) Rev. FATHER WILSON seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. DAVIES said he was very glad the Board was doing away with the undesirable conveyance in which they had taken the dead from the Workhouse to the Cemetery. Paupers ought to have as decent a burial as any other person in the town. Councillor ASHCROFT thought Mr. Pilling ought not to have invited the Matron to accompany Mrs. Clay. This was a matter which should have been left to the House Committee to decide.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON: What are you going to do with the old hearse?</p>
        <p>Mr. S. bAv-lEs : Chop it up. Mr. G. MATHER : The best plan is to send it to Russia.</p>
        <p>Mr. RITCHIE spoke in support of the action of Mr. Pilling. Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON moved that the minutes pass with the addition of the Matron's name to the deputation at Maghull. Mr. A. JOLLEY seconded and this was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LEGAL POINT.</head>
        <p>A LEGAL POINT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK stated that the House Committee had re- quested him to advise upon [a recent decision of the Court of Appeal in whichit was held that a Workhouse came within the term "public building," and that the engine house was therefore a factory within the mean- ing of the Factory and Workshop Act 1901. The Guardians of the Mile End Old Town were summoned for keeping a certain part of the Workhouse, being an engine shed and machinery for the purpose of supplying electric light and power to the Workhouse, not in conformity with the Factory and Workshop Act, 1901. The actual offence was that a certain steam engine was not securely fenced. The judges appeared to have had some difficulty inerrivinfr'at their decision, and each expressed doubts thereon. Yet their decision was that a Workhouse came within the term "public building." Although the litigation did not arise out of any liability under the Employers Liability Act in respect of a claim for compensation, but as explained upon a complaint of insufficient fencing, the effect of the decision was 0 being the Workhouse within the meaning of the Factory Act. It, was necessary for the Guardians to see that the fencing of their engines Scc., was secure. The prudence of including any officer in the insurance against claims for compensation was a matter for the Guardians.</p>
        <p>The matter WA S left with the House Committee. THE COUNTY RATE PRECEPT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK was givell-futh-o'iitliO-1F,;1.1e the usual notices upon the assistant overseers as soon as the Ccunty Rate precept was received. This was all the business.</p>
        <p>DECREASE OF CRIME AT Sr HELENS. The annual report of Chief Constable Wood. of St. Helens, shows the town is becoming more free from crime each year. The table of indictable offences is almost clear of any record of serious crimes. For minor indictable offences, 179 person were arrested and two summoned. Only eleven were committed for trial, and 168 were dealt with summarily. The number of persons proceeded against for other criminal offences was 2,730, a decrease of 114 on the previous year, and on the quasi-criminal offences (597) there is a decrease of 32. There are 138 public, 105 beerhouses, and nine grocers' licences in the town. The public analyst dealt with 150 samples of food and drugs, and in three cases proceedings were taken, and convictions followed for adulteration.</p>
        <p>A MIGHTY BELL.—One of the sights of the Shwe Dagone pagoda, in Burtnah, is a gigantic bell of bronze., weighing 421 tons, and said to be the third largest hell in the world, the largest. being at Moscow, and the next i largest in Mingin, also n Burmah. After conquering Burmah, the British undertook to carry the great Ran- goon bell to Calcutta as a tro hy, but dropped it over- board in the Rangoon river, where it defied all efforts of the engineers to raise it. Some years later the Burmese, who had not ceased to mourn its loss, begged . to be allowed to recover Their petition was granted, and by attaching to it an incredible number of bamboo floats, the unwieldy mass:- of metal was finally lifted from its muddy bed, and triumphantly restored to its. place.</p>
        <p>In the Making of Millionaires Porridge has played a very important part. The majority of them were reared in humble circumstances, and had good brain and muscle - building Porridge for their daily fare. It was that that gave them their strength and manliness and money - getting power What higher testimony could there be to the-valuc of ' Provost ' Oats Porridge as an article of diet? Most people know that Scotch Oats are the finest in the world,</p>
        <p>and as ' Provost' Oats are the</p>
        <p>pick of Scotch Oats they naturally represent an excellence of quality that is unequalled. They are far superior to American Oat. R. ROBINSON &amp; SONS, ANNAN, N. B.</p>
        <p>Try our "Blue Bell" Flaked I.ic2, it makes dainty puddings•in five minutL!-;. Simply drench the flakes in hHi water and cook in an oven for five minu:c..i. 3d. one pound packet.</p>
      </div>
