      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ROYAL MUMMING.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL MUMMING.</p>
        <p>Apart from the Coronation, there has not been such a gay or important royal function for many years as the marriage of the Prin- cess Alice of Albany to Prince Alexander of Teck, in St. George's Chapel, Windsor, which took place on Wednesday. It was not only •-•ery popular in the royal family itself, but 0.50 with the public, as it was a thoroughly English wedding, and, *hat is of necessity rare amongst royalty, a love-match. Princess Alice, since her father's death when she was quite a baby, has been brought up in the neighbourhood of Esher, where the Duchess of Albany and she are very popular; and Prince Alexander of Teck, the youngest favourite son of the late Duchess, was educated by his patriotic mother as a thorough Englishman. As he is brother to the Princess of Wales, there was a double connection in the wedding, and whilst the King and Queen were enter- taining at Windsor Castle on behalf of the bride, the Prince and P)incess of Wales at Frogmore represented the bridegroom's family, and acted as hosts to him and the near relations on his side. The King, acting on behalf, of his brother, the 'late Prince Leopold, gave the bride away, and was supported by her brother, the young Duke of Saxe-Coburg Gotha. Princess Alice (now of Teck) was followed to the altar by five royal brides- maids, the little Princess Victoria of Wales, the Duke of Connaught's two pretty daugh- ters, the Princesses Margaret and Patricia, Princess Mary of Teck, and the little Princess of Waldeck. The bridegroom was attended by his brother as best man. The ceremony was performed by the Archbishop of Canter- bury, supported by several other Bishops and well-known clergy. The King himself, who is very inuch attached to the young bride, had personally arranged all the details of the ceremony, and it is notable that only one wedding has taken place in St. George's Chapel, Windsor, since that of the uchess of</p>
        <p>H.M. KING OF WURTEMBURG, WHOSE ILLNESS PRE- VENTED HIS HEING PRESENT AT THE WEDDING. Albany herself. Four processions preceded the ceremony, each heralded by a fanfare of trumpets. The first consisted of the royal guests and their suites ; the second of the members of the royal family; the third of the bridegroom and his best man;. and the fourth of the young bride on the King's arm, with her supporters and bridesmaids. After the ceremony the King and Queen gave a luncheon-party in the state dining-room and the Waterloo Chamber; and in the evening a family dinner-party was held in the state dining-room. The bride and bridegroom left Windsor by special train late in the afternoon for Hatfield, where, at Brockett Hall, Lord Mount Stephen's lovPly Tilace. they are spend- ing the first part of the honeymoon. bates on they proceed to the Riviera, where the rest of the honeymoon will be passed at the Duchess of Albany's beautiful place, the Villa Nevada. The presents numbered over five hundred, and were truly magnificent and most valuable—not only did they consist of a wonderful array of jewellery and plate, but also of handsome pieces of furniture, carriages, patntings and other useful articles. The royal guests from abroad included the Queen of Wurtemburg, the Queen Dowager of Holland, the Prince and Princess, of Wied, the Prince and Princess of Waldeck-Pyrmont, Princess Alexandra of Shaumburg - Lippe, Princess Helene, and the Prince and Princess of Ben- tham- S t einf ur th.</p>
        <p>• ' ‘1•. • ' • 4.14' 4 ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>.423,000 LOCK.</head>
        <p>A .423,000 LOCK.</p>
        <p>J_Tp-river steamer trippers will hear with pleasure that a new lock is now being com- pleted at Teddington, which will make the long delay experienced there for years a:thing of the past. The new lock; which will be the largest on the Thames, and will cost over -23.000, is to be devoted to the barge traffic, leaving the old lock exclusively for the pleasure steamers. wing 650 feet in length, it is nearly five times longer than the existing one, which has been in use since 1857, when it replaced the first lock, constructed in 1776. Teddington has always been .the confusion point on the Thames, owing to the fact that the traffic is so worked that the greater part of it arrives there at high water, when much</p>
        <p>cv</p>
        <p>THE OLD LOCK AND THE</p>
        <p>WESTF.RN HALF OF THE NEW. time ,is lost, as the old lock can only take one barge at . a time. The new lock will carry, at one operation, what is known as• a full team ; that ,is, a tug and six barges. The lock has, however, been divided into two compartments, making it possible to get short teams through with greater dispatch than if the whole length were used. It is estimated that more traffic will in future be passed in a quarter of an hour than the lock at present takes 45 minutes to negotiate. The work was begun last spring, and con- siderable. difficulty has been. met with in the constriction; water in the foundations being a particularly troublesome cause of delay, to say nothing of the heavy floods. I3nt if they can get the lock finished before the summer pleasure traffic begins, the Thames' Conser- vancy will be satisfied. It is tipped to verform the opening ceremony in April</p>
