      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>_ -</p>
        <p>THE COTTON CRISIS</p>
        <p>The gamble in cotton on the other side o. the Atlantic, with all its baneful influence on one of the world's greatest industries, has brought into prominence the name of Daniel J. Sully, who now dominates the situation as regards American supplies of cotton. Rum- ours have been in circulation that he has now retired, after having amassed a fortune of 3,000,000 dols., but such reports have generally been contra- dicted, and it is difficult to ob- tain reliable in- formation. For the uninitiated, it may be here explained that the figures given daily in the papers regard- ing the fluctua- tions in the price per lb. are decimal points</p>
        <p>J. suLn</p>
        <p>of cents, and some confusion is caused by the quotation occasionally being conver-ted into • pence. The existing price in America ranges between 17 and 18 cents per lb., or 81d. to 9d., and no such values as these have been known since the year 1874, when quotations were still under the influence of the American war. Conditions ' since those days, however, have very materially changed, for whereas the crop then was about 4,000,000 bales, last year the crop was considered small at 10,750,000 bales. In 1874, too, English spinners accounted for about i million bales, while America took only a little over if millions. Now the English consumption has risen to over 2i million bales, while American mills require more than 4./ million bales. The American planter thus finds the largest market at home. Mr. Sully and his colleagues are reputed to be in possession at present of contracts .for the whole of the visible supply of cotton. namely, about three million bales, and the effect of this is naturally seen in the ex treordinary rise in the value of the commodity attendant upon the endeavours of the sellers to Mr. Sully to get clear of their liabilities, as there is no Possibility of their obtaining supplies for delivery, and the terms on which they will be allowed to buy back their contracts will doubtless be onerous in the extreme.</p>
        <p>./. 1 )1 ' _ . , i \ \ \\ --, . MR. DANIEL \,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>OUR NEWEST BATTLESHIP.</head>
        <p>OUR NEWEST BATTLESHIP.</p>
        <p>Last week a fresh addition to the British Navy was launched at Portsmouth before an unusually distinguished gathering of specta- tors. The man-o'-war in question is a first- class battleship of what is known as the "King Edward VII." class, and is called after the colony of New Zealaud. Appropriately, therefore, it was the Countess of Onslow who, accompanied by her husband, christened the IaCW ship with a bottle of colonial wine; and it must have brought back many pleasant re- collections to both of the happy days from 1898-1902 when the Earl was Governor of the colony, which liked him as well as he liked it. The Duke of Connaught honoured</p>
        <p>p"</p>
        <p>-- _ , - 1,1 . - - - `kr • - ="-r- -_ - ""*"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>H.M.S. NEW ZEALAND</head>
        <p>H.M.S. NEW ZEALAND</p>
        <p>the occasion with his presence, and amongst the other notable people in the famous dock- yard were Admiral Sir Nowell Salmon, the new "Father of the Navy " ; Admiral Sir John Fisher, commander -in - chief at Ports- mouth, fresh from the War Office Committee; Admiral Sir Edmund Fremantle; Maj.-Gen. Montgomery, commanding the Southern Dis- trict ; and, last but not least, the Agent- General for New Zealand. The new battle- ship took the water with an easy grace amidst loud cheering and the strains of the National Anthem, flying the royal standard, the New Zealand flag, and the white ensign. Imme- diately after the launch the keel plate of another similar .vessel, to be named the Bri- tannia, was laid. H.M.S. New Zealand is 425 feet long and 78 feet in beam, and has a displacement of 16,350 tons. She will have four 12-,inch and four 9.2-inch guns, with a secondary armament of ten 6-inch quick-firing guns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>:Ail. J. P. WILLIAM'S SUDDEN END</head>
        <p>:Ail. J. P. WILLIAM'S SUDDEN END</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Powell Williams, M.P., who died in the Westminster Hospital on Sunday evening, after being seized with a slight apoplectic fit in the House of Commons, was noted for the success of his candidatures. He was born in 1840, and was th f b late Mr. Joseph Williams, of Worcester. He was educated at Edgbaston Pro- prietary School. For several years he was a member, of the Birmingham Town Councii, and for five years was chair-, man of the Finance Com- mittee of that body. He was in the Sur- veyor's Depart- ment of the Post Office under Anthony Trollope. In 1.885 he first POWELL stood as Parlia- WILLIAMS, M.P. men tary candidate for South Birmingham, and was returned by a large majority. Becoming a Unionist, he was again returned in the General Election in 1886, and continued in the representation of the division down to the time of his death. At the General Elec- tion of 1895 he secured a majority of 3,573 over his Liberal opponent, and in 1900 he was returned unopposed. In 1895 the late .Lord Salisbury appointed him Financial Secretary to the War Office—a position which he held until the reconstruction of his Cabinet by Lord Salisbury after the last General Election.</p>
        <p>tie son o. the ~,,,„..—..........,....... / \\,.. ••,,,\z \\ ' c---D! ....) / •, . i. . 1 "ff /,// •' THE LATE MR. J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
