      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 13, 1904.</p>
        <p>WHAT TURKISIT RULE</p>
        <p>Eel;g ous bigotry is less universal in Turkny than is often believed. The attitude of the Mussulman is less one of intense animosity and I,reselytising fervour than of contempt. The Christian suljects of the Sultan are grouped into Millets, or ,Churches, each of which has a recognised head, and through whom all -communications r.ss. In ordinary times religi3us fanaticism for the most part slumbers ; but when the Turk is aroused he is liable to F_erce gusts of religions passion, and the militant fervol:-: of earlier ages renews itself within him. Bigotry is not the sole cause of any outbreak of cruelty on the part of the Turk; but it quickly reinforces the other evil pascions, and gives to the struggle some- thing of the character of a religious crusade. These, then, are the principal reasons why there can be no finality in any political arrangement which leaves a Christian popula- tion under the - rule of the. Turk. Every revolt has 'substantially the same causes; and if it ends in any other way than in the emancipation of the sufferers it very speedily breaks out again. History shows us that there is only one solution. To refuse to recognise it, and to press it on the other Powers is to perpetuate the misery and bloodshed of Mace- donia, to postpone the inevitable step of securing for that unhappy province the freedom which is now enjoyed hy the other states of the Balkan penine&amp;a.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MEANS.</head>
        <p>MEANS.</p>
        <p>BY G. P. 000011 *(JOINT AUTHOR OF "THE HEART OP EMPIRE " ).</p>
        <p>It cannot be too clearly understood that the crisis in Macedonia arises from the 'normal and permanent abuses of the Turkish regime. One Sultan may be better than another, and one Governor lass corrupt than his predecessor, but the system which they administer has remained practically unchanged for centuries. The causes of this immobility lie deep in the psychology of the Turk. Though he realises that in one or two departments of life, notably! in business, the European is his superior, he has always entertained, and entertains to-day, a brutal, unreasoning con- tempt for the Christian, the contempt of a Mussulman for the unbeliever, ths contempt of a warrior race for men whom they have defeated and whom they hold down by force. Under stress of necessity Sultans have issued charters affirming the equality of all their subjects; but there is scarcely a Turk who believes in any such equality, .and no system of law built in ' its recognition has the slightest chance of practical application. It has been truly said that it is impassible to demand a reform which has not long figured in the Statute-Book. The complete failure pf Sultan Malunnd in the early part of last century, and of Midhat and the young Turks in the later part, to introduce even the more elementary of what may be called European ideas, is a proof that reform from within is not to be looked for. That laws and treaties and charters in no way affect the realities of Turkish rule is a lesson that many European diplomatists have not even yet learnt; but it is the first step in understanding the Eastern Question. _ _</p>
        <p>S.nce the Crimean War Turkey has been recognised as a - member of the, comity of nations; but her ideas and practice remain substantially the same as when the followers of Othman roamed over the plains of Asia Minor. The Turks are an army of 'occupation in Europe, and there is no reason to believe they will ever be anything else. We know from history that the rule of the Turk has proved so intolerable that every Christian people that has over passed under his sway has made repeated efforts to throw ,off the yoke. But let us 'look a little more closely at the system, and inquire what it is from which the Christian more particularly suffers. The fountain of most other evils is the utter disorganisation in which the finances of the Empire permanently find themselves. Though the Sultan rules over. populous countries and fertile land, the revenue is scarcely more than i:20,0D0,000; the customs are earmarked for the payment of interest on the enormous and ever-accumulating foreign debt; and, whatever else suffers, the army must be paid, and new artillery and ammuni- tion must be bought. Of the remainder, a very large part slips away on its journey to and from the Treasury. The Turkish peasant is at least as honest as his Christian neigh- bour; hut the corruption of the Turkish officials is practically universal. They would. indeed be saints if they lived otherwise, con-. sidering the system of which they form part: The Empire is 'divided into yilayets or provinces, over each of which is a Governog. These Governors are supposed to receive handsome ialaries ; but as • a matter of fact a Turkish official has, in most cases' to find his own salary, for he receives none. He has very probably paid a considerable sum to obtain the post, and he must begin by recouping himself. He then finds that the calls made from Constantinople on the local trbasury,ltre -frequent and urgent; and it is only natural that he should believe that his chances of future promotion depend in large measure on the readiness with which he responds to the claims that are made on him. He has to squeeze for himself and he has to squeeze for his master; and the example that is set in high places is followed in all the lower ranks of the official hierarchy. Every one is forced to procure money from, somewhere, and it is the peasant who .pays. The difference between theory and practice is as great in regard to the ordinary expenses of administration as in the case of the salary of officials. The taxes are, on paper, the lightest in Europe; but they are farmed out, and the collectors make their percentage before they hand over. the required sum to the authorities. Moreover, there is no proteetion against .abuse in regard to the time and method of collection, and no safeguard against a demand proffered a second time; the peasant is, at the mercy of the concessionaire.</p>
        <p>This state of things is not hold in check by the law courts. The plunderer fears nothing, and the victim hopes nothing from them. It is an immemorial and unchanging principle that the word of a Christian is not to weigh against the word of a Mussulman. The law court can be relied on only to confirm and approve the outrage of which copplaint is brought. Mohammedan law is based on the Koran, and the distinction that is familiar to us between canon and secular law is unknown. Sultan Mahmud made a erallant effort to introduce a better legal system some seventy years age; but no part of his work met with. less success. justice is only to be -obtained when the law of the land recognises the equality of the various sections of its inhabit-- ants; and Mohammedan law'is in every part the expression of the superiority of the Blussulman.</p>
        <p>A third evil from which the Christian popu- lation invariably suffers is the absence of public 'orde. Not that Turkey is witl.o it police. She Is well supplied with them; but their activity is often more oppressive than that of the malefactors whom. they are sup- Posed to hold in check. Like other officials, they are as a rule unpaid, or, at any rate, only partially paid, and they live .on the inhabit- ants in consequence. It is even to-day scarcely realised in England th4t no Mace- donian Christian is secure from day to day against the theft of one or other item of his small stock of belongings. In no country is the general economic position of the peasantry lower, and it, is only by unremittin,, and laborious toil that a family can live. unremitting the whole or part of the fruits of this labour is stolen, either with or without a pretext, it is not surprising that deeds of desperation cnsue.</p>
        <p>, A fourth evil of the Christian popu- lation in every country directly governed by Turkish officials and garrison3d by Turkish troops is that the honour of Christian women is never safe. It,is a most ordinary occurrence for.the wife or (laughter to be seized when the bead,of the family is away at work, outraged, and returned en the following day. It is use- less to take the matter into court, for insult would merely be added to injury. Thole aro only two alternatives—to take no notice or to join in ally revolt that is taking place. If we once realise that no woman of comely appear- ance is safe from outrage we shall better understand the Macedonian revolt and all other revolts, past and future, of the Christian population against the Turkish rule. No Breakfast Table complete without</p>
      </div>
