      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>rilisß appearance of that unique little "Miary and Com- panion " issued by GRO. G. BUSSBY OS: CO., for the use of Cricketers (which may be obtained from Sports Dealers, Booksellers, or Railway Bookstalls for 6d.), reminds us of the coming season, in anticipation of which GEO. 4. BUINEY &amp; Co.' have prepared a " record" stock of Bate. We are told that the timber they hold for Bat making alone exameds 1,000 tons. " Glorious Cricket follows the British Flag everywhere." (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>HUNSLET v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington journeyed to Hunslet on Saturday,and ful- filled expectations by getting well beaten. It was a great disappointment to the Hunslet football enthus- iasts that miserable weather and turf conditions spoiled the return encounter with Warrington at Parkside. Hun.let were minus the services of the brothers Whiteley, Jackson, and Hannah. Albert Goldthorpe and Burnell filled the centre three-quarter positions and Burnett went to the half back pot. Warrington were without Hockenhall, his place being taken by Kenyon.</p>
        <p>When Dickenson started the game for Warrington there would be about 2,000 persons present. The Huns- let forwards at once asserted a distinct superi ority,and by working their way up the field in dogged fashion compelled their opponents to resort to purely defensive tactics. Warrington had a very warm time of it, and on one occasion the line was only saved by the clever defence of Mahoney. Again the visiting line had a narrow escape, as from a cross kick by Everson, Walter Goldthorpe almost got over. He should have gone straight on, but preferred to pass, and there being no one near enough to take the ball the opportunity was lost. Kenyon relieved with a clever run and punt to centre. Albert Goldthorpe next made a good though unsuccessful effort to lower the goal from a penalty given against Warrington for off-side play. Hunslet tried hard to break through the defence, but were kept out by the floe play of the visiting backs. Thus at half-time nothing had been scored. The game was restarted by Albert Goldthorpe, a grand rush of the home forwards being the first item of note. After about five minutes' play a scrummage was formed near the Warrington line, and from here Albert Gold- thorpe passed to Burnell. This player forced his way over with several men on his back, finally grounding the ball in the right hand corner. Albert Goldthorpe lowered the goal with a magnificent shot from this difficult position. Fish made a brilliant single-handed effort for Warrington, but failed to get through. The Hunslet captain provided another opportunity for a score t ree minutes later. Play was taking place at the Warrington " 25 " when A. Goldthorpe kicked across, and there being some laxity in the defence, Burnett shot up and dropped on the ball between the posts, Albert Goldthorpe having no difficulty in adding the goal points. Warrington now wakened up, but were unable to break through the Hunslet defence, and when the whistle sounded for "no side," the Parkside team had won by 2 goals 2 tries (10 points), to nil. Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>Hunslet—H. Price, back; W. Goldthorpe, A. E. Goldthorpe, A. E. Price, and A. Burnell, threequarter backs; E. Burnett and A. Everson. half backs; J. Shooter, H. Wilson, J. W. Clew, T. Walsh, W. Brooke, J. Barraclough, J. liamae, and W. Wray, forwards. Warrington—Hallam. back; Fish, Isherwood. Dick- enson,and Kenyon,threequarter backs ;'Bate and Mahoney, half backs • Morrison; Boardman, 'Swift, Cook, Thomas Jolley, Fielding. and Lunt, forwards. Referee, Mr. E. H. Smirk, Wigan. Touch judges, Messrs. F. T. Rowlands (Dewsbury) and T. Davis (Warrington). HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Played at Hull. Runcorn won the toss' .tnd a start was made by Starks. For a time the home team looked dangerous, when from a scrummage. near the Rovers' line the visiting forwards obtaining possession the ball was passed to Jolley, Richardson, and then Butterwortk who crossed over under the posts, and Farmer placed an easy goal. This was the half-time score. The Rovers re-started but they failed to break down a stubborn defence, and their play was altogether disappointing. When Runcorn werepenalised for, obstruction, Gordon had no difficulty in kicking a goal, but this was their only score. On the other hand, the visitors added another try after a passing movement by Jolley, Richard- son and Butterworth. Warder finally getting possession and crossing 'easily. Farmer failed to convert. Result: —Runcorn 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points), Kingston Hovers 1 goal (2 points). Teams : Hull Kingston Rovers—Carmichael, back; West, Gordon. S. Reed and Harrison, three-quarter backs; Barry and J. W. Reed, half backs; Fletcher, Starks Ellis, Gorman, Reed, Wiltshire, Whitcombe, and Townsley, forwards. R uncorn —Harrison, back ; Jones Bu tterworth, Warder, and Dutton, three-quarter backs;l Jolley and Richardson, half backs; Walker, Farmer, -Moulton, A. Richardson, Washburn, Tomlinson, Robson, and C. Houghton, forwards. •4P "Referee, Mr. .1. H. Smith, Widnas. Touch judges, Messrs. J. W. Mellor, Goole, and H. Sewell, Leeds. WIDNES v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 1,500 spectators. Leigh 'at once attacked, and a (rood chance was spoiled by Burgess knocking on. Glnley and Bennett played smartly, but again a knock-on saved the Widnes line. A fine dribble by Wailing and Barber took play into the visitors' half, but it was not for long, and Leigh returned to the attack. Widnes were penalised, but Johnson's kick went wide. A very strong forward mpvement by Widnes was checked by Ganley, and at half-time there was no score.</p>
        <p>The second half opened in favour of Widnes. Cockmm got possession, and ran over, but the ball was brought back for a knock-on. Mercer intercepted a pass, and kicked to the Widnes line, but Tilley put the ball in touch. For a time the Widnes defence was sorely taxed, and then Ike Taylor, with a clever run, cleared to the centre. Bennett had his kick charged down, and Wail- ing following up was baulked by Pearson kicking dead, and•the match eventually ended in a pAntless draw. teams :</p>
        <p>Widnes—Tilley, back; Wailing Taylor, Peacock, and Barber, three-quarter backs; Lally and Douglas, half backs; S. Aspey, G. Aspey, Cockram, Curtis, McCabe, McDonald, Webster, and Rigby, forwards. Leigh—Pearson, back; Ganley, Messer, Bennett, and Underwood, three-quarter backs; Burgess and Johnson, half-backs; Boardman, Roberts, Blackburn, Kelly, Clare, Silcock, Berry, and O'Neil, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Kennedy (Broughton). Touch judges, Messrs. J. Jones - (Warrington), and F. Little (St. Helens).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SWINTON! v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>SWINTON! v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>At Swintom,l3efore 10,000 spectators. Lomas opened the scoring with a fine try, but the same player failed at pal. In the second half Salford completely over played their opponents. Heath scored a try from a good effort by Bedford. Then Lomas kicked a penalty goal. Bedford also ran in a try for the visitors, but, Lomas failed. Result—Salford, 11 points ; Swinton, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS. Mrs. BILTAIN, ths great nnthority on ()Adam's Diseasyg. strongly re 30 urnends VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. ADA S. BALLIN, 5, Agar-street, London, Editor of Won imAJoi, writes :—" VENO's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is an exceedingly successful remedy. It is very pleasant to take, and the relief it gives is rapid. The preparation is perfectly safe for children." Mrs. HAGUE, Ratcliffe Crescent. Lockwood, near Huddersfield, writes My little boy suffered from hoarsAness and bronchitis. We all thought he never would be cured or speak plain again. After taking VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE he was able to speak plain. I have never seen anything to act so quickly." Ask for VENo's LIGHTNING Col:G if CURE at Chemists and Drip, Stores. Price 'PA., Is. IW., and 2s. 9d. Sold everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
