      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>. ,</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Broughton, in wet weather, before 5,000 people. Davies started for Batley, who had to defend, and Wilson soon scored, W. James landing a goal from the place. In the second half the Rangers had again rather the best of matters, and Widdeson and Oram scored. Hodgson scored for Batley. Result—Broughton Rangers, 1 goal 3 tries (11 points) ; Batley, 1 try (3 points). _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>HALIFAX V. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX V. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>At Hanson-lane. The visitors kicked off. After very even play Gledhill scored an unconverted try for the homesters. On restarting Wigan attacked, and Latham dropped a goal. Play was very interesting until the close. Result—Halifax, 1 try (3 points) ; Wigan,l goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>OLDHAM v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>At Oldham, before 10,000 spectators. The start was exciting. Leeds were given a penalty kick, and Little- wood kicked a goal in the first minute. A quarter of an hour later Ferguson equalised. Nothing further was scored before the interval, though play was very excit- ing. Ferguson struck the upright with a splendid kick. A keen game was fought without further score. Result —Oldham, 1 goal; Leeds, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>KEIGHLEY v. HULL.</head>
        <p>KEIGHLEY v. HULL.</p>
        <p>At Keighley, before 3000 spectators, in gloomy weather. Playing downhill Keighley pressed frequently, often looking like scoring. Walker's goal-kicking was faulty, but in the first half he landed a penalty. In tbe second half Keighley made most of the play, and showed great determination. Stacey scored a smart try, and Walker converted. He also dropped a goal. Result— Keighley, 2 goals 1 try (9 points) ; Hull, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>VUDDERSFIELD v. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>VUDDERSFIELD v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>At Huddersfield,in foggy weather,before 5000 people. In the first half Mosby and Dechan scored tries for Bradford, Mobby converting one, and Deere scored a try for Huddersfield. In the second half Mosby and Upton scored tries. Mosby placed all the Bradford goals. Result—Bradford, 4 goals 4 tries ; Hudders- field, 1 try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WA.IIIIINGTON " A " V. ROCHDALE RANcEns.—Owing to the wretched weather there was only a small attend- ance at Wilderspool on Saturday to witness this match. Harris re-appeared in the home ranks' for the first time since he met with the accident at Bradford on Novem- ber 28th. The Rochdale men early attacked, and a presistent attack was eventually rewarded by cnie of the half-backs scoring near She posts. the try being improved upon. Following this reverse the Warringtonians took up a strong position and clever runs by Hockenhall and Harris were checked dangerously near the line. Penalty kicks eased the pressure slightly until a round of pass- ing between Evans, Duckworth and Short resulted in the last-named punting over the Rochdale backs, and following up he missed scoring by inches. However, a a score was not long in forthcoming, for another bout of passing resulted in the ball travelling to Harris who rushed over at the corner. The same player again got over just before the interval, at which period the score read :—Warrington, two tries (six points) ; Rochdale Rangers, one goal one try (five points.) During the second half the Warringtonians were the aggressors throughout. Harris crossed on one occasion, but the try was not allowed. Final: Warrington, two tries (six points); Rochdale Rangers, one goal one try (five points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL, The Unemployed Question. LABOUR BUREAU NOT WANTED. Corporation Officials' Salaries.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL, The Unemployed Question. LABOUR BUREAU NOT WANTED. Corporation Officials' Salaries.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held inthe Bold Street Wesleyan school on Tuesday evening, Mr. Joseph Podmare,junior, presiding.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED LABOUR BUREAU. The CHAIRMAN explaiaed that at the last meeting a resolution was passed requesting the Cprporation to provide work for the unemployed. The question of a labour bureau was adjourned for a month. Mr. RA.NDLES said he was disappointed at the failure to carry the part of the resolution relating to a labour bureau at the last meeting. The opinion of his branch was still the same and he moved that the Warzington Town Council be requested to provide a bureau for the use of the unemployed. Bureaux were at work in other towns ad had proved very useful. Mr. ROWLANDS seconded. He alluded to the details recently furnished to the Local Government Board by Mr. J. C. Sutton. clerk to the Board of Guardians, who stated that there was a marked increase in the number seeking relief from the Guardians. It was high time that the Town Council established a bureau which he believed would find employment for a great many of those out of work.</p>
        <p>Mr. BUCKLEY said the joiners' society had instructed him to vote against the formation of a bureau. Mr. Smrrti objected most stronglg to the resolution on the ground that it was playing far too much into the employers ' hands. He would "kick against" the proposal as long as he had a vote on the Council. Mr. ROWLA.NDS said that if the bureau were managed properly, it ought to be of great benefit. If the manage- ment was not satisfactory they would only have them- selves to blame. Mr. KENDIUCK said he had been instructed by his branch to oppose the resolution. When people regis- tered their names at a bureau there was no stipulation at to the hours of labour and rate of wages if they found employment. That was directly opposed to trade union principles. and he considered that a bureau would be detrimental to trade unionism in Warrington. At the same time something should be done to relieve th• distress. .</p>
        <p>Mr. MAssEv contended that the Trades Council should be able to deal with the unemployed question. When skilled labour was wanted, the employers wrote to the secretaries of trade unions requesting them to provide men.</p>
        <p>Mr. P LINSTON thought the formation of a bureau would be practically the half-way house to non-union- ism. He agreed with MT. Massey that employers wrote to the secretaries of labour organizations when they required men. If working men would only adhere more strongly to the principles of trade unionism there would not he the slightest necessity for a labour bureau in Warrington, as the trade unions would be in a posi- tion to do the work which a bureau would do.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY (Councillor Stott) said he was once infavour of a bureau, but after receiving more inform- ation he was strongly opposed to it. He was only in favour of labour bureaux in towns where the local Councils were willing to provide work for all or the greater number of the men who registered their names. The opening of a bureau on the ordinary lines would place employers in such a position that they would be able to hold a sword over the heads of their workmen by telling them that if they did not do this or that they would be discharged and their places taken by men from the labour bureau. In Manchester 2,000 men had registered their names at the bureau, but employment : had been obtained for only about 50. On the other hand, in Bradford out of 800 registered, 500 had been engaged, many of them by the City Council. If the Corporation was willing to provide work for those who registered, a bureau would be a great advantage. Mr. RANDLES pointed out that secretaries of trade unions could only deal with their own Members, but a great number of the unemployed were non-unionists. He desired that they should be helped by the opening of a bureau. As to the contention that it would IL playing into the masters' hands, if employers wanted to take the line indicated by Mr. Stott, they could do so at the present time by merely visiting the works gates -where every morning they would find from thirty to, ' forty men seeking employment. The CHAIRMAN while agreeirg with Mr. Randles as to the prevalance of distress suggested that he should bring the question of alleviating it before the Council in another form. A bureau would serve a useful par- pose in ascertaining the extent of the distress. On a vote being taken the resolution was lost by a 1 large majority. 1 The SECRETARY said the General Purposes Com- mittee had appointed a committee to consider the</p>
        <p>question of pro. iding work for the ti-ne-n-ipl-o-ye-d:. - The Secretaries of the various branches were re- questedqo furnish Councillor Stott with any informa- tion in their possession relative to the lack of employ- ment in their particular trades. THE PRICE OF GAS. REDUCTION WANTED FOR USERS OF SLOT METERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. BULLOCK .had given notice of his intention to</p>
        <p>introduce the subject-of a reduction in the price of gas to users of slot meters. He was unable to attend, but wrote complaining that it was an injustice to charge users of slot meters 7d. per 1,000 feet more than ordin- ary consumers. The SECRETARY read the report of the Gas Engineer presented at the last Town Council meeting. He con- I tend4l that the Trades Council was quite justified in complaining of the price. He always understood that slot meters were introduced in order to encourage the poorer people to use gas in preference to oil lamps and low-flash oil. To charge them more than the ordinary consumers was a very peculiar method of encouragement. _</p>
        <p>Along discussion -mis_necl,Alr,Buckley moving ihat the</p>
        <p>Secreta-iy be instructed to push the in-atter foiward and</p>
        <p>do everything in his power to obtain a reduction. Mr. JOLLEY_ suggested that the Council should recom-</p>
        <p>mend the abolitiOn of meter rents, and Mr. Buckley agreed to add the recommendation to his resolution.</p>
        <p>NERVE PAINS, WINTER ILLS, CHILBLAINS, CUTS AND SORES, BRUISES, BURNS, Etc.</p>
        <p>\Mr. HANDLES was in favour of a public demonstration against the unjust way in which the users of slot meters were treated. Mr. KENDRICK said that although the ga s engineer stated that there was a loss on slot meters, every in- ducement was extended to him (Mr. Kendrick) by the i officials at the gas works to use one. 1 It was decided to defer the question of the abolition of meter leer, the first part of the resolution being carried unanimously. RESIGNATION OF THE TREASURER. The SECRETARY j announced with regret that Mr. Malone, the treasurer, had resigned from the Council. It was decided to fill the office at the next meeting. MUNICIPAL REFORM. The SECRETARY reported the receipt of a notice of the National Municipal Reform Conference to be held in Manchester on March 12th. Councillor Dodds wil preside at the afternoon meeting, and in the evenin Mr. S. Norbury Williams will speak. Mr. PLINSTON, who had taken the chair as Mr. Pod more had to leave the meeting, considered they had quite sufficient to talk about with reference to local trade union matters without incurring the expenditure of sending a delegate to a conference of that descrip- tion. He noticed the speakers included one or two gentlemen who had been endeavouring to put Warring- ton right in municipal work. 'Whether they had done so or ever would do so, was it question for people to settle for themselves. Personally he did not see why the Council should take part in the conference. It was decided not to send a delegate. THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE AND "FAIR CONTRACTS!' PROTEST AGAINST INCREASES OF OFFICIALS' SALARIES. Mr. BUCKLEY said he did not know ,whether the Education Committee recognized the fair wages clause drawn up by the T 0 %VAL Council, and he would ask Mr. Plinston to keep the matter in mind, especially in regard to building contracts. The committee had already given out work to a firm which they did not recognize as ",fair." He also wished to protest against the extravagant waste of money on ' Corporation officials' salaries. Their eyes had been opened of late to,the way in which their representatives wasted money in this direction. If they were only as lenient with the workmen and displayed the same willingness to advance their wages, he would havea great deal more confidence in them. He was very pleased at the stand Councillor Stott took on the matter. He moved that the Council should strongly protest against "the waste of money in raising officials' salaries, and ask the Corporation to take the workmen's wages as much into consideration as the officials." Mr. ROWLANDS seconded. The SECRETARY said that as increases of salaries of over £2 a week were only considered in February it would probably be much more effective if the Council passed a resolution next January. Mr. PLINSTON said a resolution would be introduced at the next meeting of the Education Committee that the Director's salary 'be advanced. Mr. Bucgley's pro- position, if carried, would be of some weight to him in the stand he would take on that question. He would be quite as strong in opposing that advance as any member of the Town Council was at the last meeting. He might not have any better results, but that would not be his fault. In regard to the "fair wage" clause, he introduced the question in regard to a certain con- tract and the Director said the firm had agreed to the usual clause as to the "recognized rate of wages." fl The SECRETARY said he was trying to compile a list of "fair and unfair shops" doing any class of work that the Corporation required. He would be pleased to receive information from any branches. The resolution was agreed to.</p>
        <p>Toilers,</p>
        <p>Mental and Manual, will derive great benefit from the regular daily use of an</p>
        <p>ABSOLUTELY PURE COCOA.</p>
