      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY 13th 19C4.</p>
        <p>THE DUKE of DEVON- THE GOVERNMENT SHIRE'S speech at the THROWING OVER Guildhall on Monday, MR. CHAMBERLAIN. a n d Mr. MORLEY'S almost simultaneous challenge to the Government had a remarkable effect on the Treasury Bench. Mr. GERALD BALFOUR, coached no doubt by his brother, took a line which was designed to ,keep every Unionist Free Trader in the Ministerial lobby, even at the risk of offending Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S snpporters. For this remarkable Government has veered right away from the great compact between Mr. CHAM- BERLAIN and the Prime Minister. Mr. ARTHUR BALFOUR openly avowed his sympathy with the policy of preferential tariffs, that is to say with Protection undisguised; and yet Mr. GERALD BALFOUR was put up to repudiate Protection in the most unequivocal terms. He admitted that the Cabinet ware divided on the subject,but for himself he declared that Protection was not a wise policy for the country that it means unfair burdens on the consumer for the benefit of the producer; and that if he had to choose between Protection and the existing system he should uphold free imports. That is the choice offered to Mr. GERALD BALFOUR by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, with whose policy free imports are incompatible. But the Prime, Minister's brother is in a singular state of mind for he is in favour of granting a preference to the Colonies on some specified plan quite different from Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S. Perhaps it will take the form of a bounty to the Colonial producers. How that could be adopted without taxing the consumers in the United Kingdom and thus establishing Protection in another and more insidious shape we have not the smallest idea. This, however, is merely incidental. The upshot of the debate is that, in their des- perate extremity, the Government have come very near to breaking with Mr. 011AMBERLAIN al- together. Probably this is because,as Mr. iNIoRLEY. said, they are a set of party managers wirepullers clutching at any, means of safety that may present itself. No man understood this process better than Mr. CHAMBERLAIN himself, and therefore he may not be disposed to press too hardly on his old colleagues. But tne sum of their pleas should make him smile. Mr. GERALD BALFJUR explained that the Prime Minister's policy was not Protec tion at all, but simply a convenient arrangement by which countervailing duties, with the consent of Parliament in every case, would be levied on the goods of a country which persisted in treating our trade with outrageous unfairness. These countervailing duties are not to be protective du- ties. Now at ;, Sheffield and Bristol the Prime Minister, with the support of Sir MICHAEL HICKS BEACH, declared for a deep and genuine change in our fiscal system, and especially for the abandon- ment of the principle of taxation for revenue cnly. Everybody understood this to mean that taxes which were not levied for revenues would be levied for the purpose of protecting our industries. But, according to Mr. GERALD BALFOUR, a countervailing 'duty will do neither the one thing nor the other. It will intimidate the foreigner without increasing the revenue or protecting the home car. When asked for I</p>
        <p>lan illustration of this singular process, Mr. BALFOUR declined to go into details before the general election. It was a prudent resoltr:,ion. How a countervailing duty on ii,nerican produce for example, is to protect nobody and lay no burden on the consumer we shall probably never learn. Mr. GERALD Ilar.Foua will certainly not unfold this marvel to his constituents, and after the general election there will be no curiosity to know. For the moment it is indispensable for the Cabinet to have it understood that their fiscal policy, whatever it may be, is not protective. This may serve as a device for a party division in the House of Commons, but in the country it can only excite disrespectful mirth.</p>
        <p>If any of our readers would like information about the weather for any of the ensuing ten months we shall be pleased to supply a forecast lacking nothing as regards definiteness. The accuracy of the information depends , upon the honourable observance by the weather of the nicely varied programme laid down for it. Mr. B. G.. Jankins,F.R.A.S., has sent us a copy of his "British Weather Chart, 1904," which has a most formidable prediction for each month based on such convincing data as telluric curves, low and high bark periods. Mr. Jenkins gives these for what they are worth. So do we. Our readers will probably be most interested in summer prospects, if • there are any. Apparently there arc a few. For instance we are to have snow in May. Then follow June, July and August with weather described as"unsettled." The temperature is going to be in a similar state, and taking the chart as a whole, we cannot extract very much comfort from it. As a prophet, how- ever, Mr. Jenkins probably does not expect to be honoured in his own country. We give him credit for having done his best with a difficult subject, and we can only hope that the weather, with customary perversity, will upset his decidedly pessimistic predictions.</p>
        <p>This is a country of anomalies of all sorts. Some are merely amusing, others distinctly awkward and irritating. Under the latter heading we have one close at hand, it-lithe shape of the toll-charges for crossing the old Warburton bridge over a disused section of the river. Pedestrians and cyclists have long been mulcted in the modest sum of one half-penny, but the Ship Canal Co., who took over the Bridge and the roadway on the completion of their undertaking, have recently made the restriction much more obnoxious. The half-penny toll has been doubled,while for motor- cyclists and motor-car owners, the privilege of crossing the bridge has been made absurdly expensive. Very curiously the old Warburton Bridge Trust of 1863 contained a clause so comprehensive aa to include such modern inventions as motor vehicles. The clause in particular specifies "traction engines and machines propelled by steam or other power," so that there is no escape for the already severely- restricted motor-car. The new charges are as follow : cyclist id., motor-cyclists 6d., to carry two persons is., and motor-cars 23. 6d. Natur- ally the motor-men don't like having to disburse silver when they cross a decayed old bridge like the one under notice. The high-level Warburton Bridge over the Ship Canal really acts as a decoy for travellers, who come blithely up the road oblivious of the nice little trap that awaits them. There is naturally very much dis- satisfaction at this state of affairs and the Warrington Rural District Council through its Surveyor, has already taken the matter up with a view to removing the griev- ance. An endeavour is being made to pur- suade the Lancashire and Cheshire County Councils to take the road over as a main road and abolish the obnoxious tolls. This should not be a very difficult matter, seeing that the bridge is the only one available between Warrington and Barton. We hope the Rural Council will persevere in its effort at improving a state of things so antiquated and troublesome.</p>
        <p>There are some Warrington Conservatives who are almost as energetic as Mr. Chamberlain himself in] their advocacy of Protection. Some of them have been quite busy this week in distributing the specious pamphlets issued by the Tariff Reform League. They have invaded the meetings held •inder the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association and made a copious distribution of leaflets of the out-and-out Protectionist type, printed on what appears to us German made paper. Some of the statements made are so fallacious and at variance with true economic principles, that we do not think any hard-beaded workingman will allow himself to be led away by them. No doubt our Protectionist friends thought they had done a smart thing in sowing tares amongst the wheat,but unfortunately for them their pamphlets got into the hands of the lecturar, who made good Use of them. He had little difficulty in showing the hollowness of the Protectionist arguments, and those who heard them must have been more than ever convinced of the soundness of Free Trade as the best policy for this country.</p>
        <p>Those dauntless enthusiasts, the members of the Warrington Cycling Club, have held their annual meeting this week and showed themselves quite ready for the next season's outings. Cycle runs are generally carried out " weather permit- ting," and on many occasions last season the weather did not permit, but Secretary Pickavance has a most cheerful. report .to present. He is one of those incorrigible optimists indispensable to such an organisation as the one he so worthily represents and almost acts as foster-parent to. Nothing disturbs Mr.Pickavanc3's equanimity,and if it tains he finds solace somehow.. Toe humor- ous side of things appeals to him at once, as will be at once . apparent on perusing the Secretary's report printed in another column. We must compliment Mr. Pickavance on his increasing literary and poetic attainments. The Club provides so many other entertainments out of &amp;ors that the members can generally find some congenial employment when cycling fails. Bowls, football, and fishing all come within "the sphere of influence," as Russian and Japanese diploma- tists would say. We regret that, according to the reports some of the hardier spirits tried in vain to "lure the wary Scottish trout with Anglo-Saxon tackle." Perhaps the flavour of the bait was a bit too English also. Liying things across the border are wanderfully clannish. We heartily wish the cyclists fair weather and dry roads in the coming season. The world of wheels resounding with the whirr of wheels is what all of us are looking for- ward to.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians have seldom done a saner or more sympathetic action than in reforming the funeral arrangements of paupers. It is as little as can be expected that the Guardians should give decent burial to a pauper who has ceased from troubling them. But up to the present the badge of pauperism has been attached to ever a pauper's corpse in Warrington. The coffin has been conveyed to the Cemetery in what has been Known as the pauper's hearse—a one-horse vehicle which looks as poverty stricken as any pauper could be. Then, as if to emphasise the fact that a pauper was being conveyed to his last resting place, a seat was provided with the driver for the paupers who were acting as bearers. These:men -always wore the distinctive corduroy assigned to paupers in the Warrington Union, and they sat on the box. of the hearse like weary sentinels over the remains of. a brother pauper. What their feelings were it would be difficult to describe. An unsympathetic Guardian would probably be prepared to say that they had no feelings. But it is quite conceivable tbas these veterans, worsted in the battle of life, must have sometimes thought of the time when they too would have to make their !final journey in a similar manner from the Work- house to the grave. Their reflections cannot have been very comforting to themselves. Happily this undesirable method is to be discarded by the Guardians. In future an undertaker is to pro vide bearers and a two-horse hearse, and thus the badge of pauperism will be removed.</p>
        <p>A letter in our correspondence column from one of the secretaries to the Ragged School gives fuller particulars than we were able to furnish last week of the simple and inexpensive way hundreds of poor children may be fed and entertained. One sovereign will provide for no less than 300 neglected slum inhabitants, and we are sure that there are plenty of people in Warrington who cart spare so small a sum for so deserving a CAUSO. The Ragged School managers will be only too pleased to take all the necessary trouble if someone else will provide the funds. We hope as many of our, readers as can possibly afford will subscribe to the funds for this purpose.</p>
        <p>Warrington Rechabites will now be able to hold aloft the standard of temperance in the true and literal sense, and we congratulate them upon the acquisition of the beautiful banner which was unfurled on Saturday. A greater cause for grati- fication is that the battalion which it is earnestly hoped the banner will lead on to signal victories in the fight against the drink evil, is ever strengthening it.; ranks. Now it can boast of a membership of 1248 adults and 781 juvenile and it is evident that interest in the work of the Older in this town and neighbourhood runs high. Financially the Warrington district is in a sound position and tlie officers, over zealous for its welfare, spare no pains to safeguard tha interests of those who repose such confidence in them. The usefulness of the order is greatly en- hanced by its work %along the rising generation. To train the children in the ways of sobriety is in- deed a laudable undertaking, and those who en - gage in it are deserving of every encouragement and support. We can only re-echo the hope ex- pressed at Saturday's memorable gathering that each succeeding year will bring increased pros- perity to an or4.7,anizition which deserves well of all who have the advancement of the temperance cause at heart.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain will not be pleased to hear that the Government, despite its Protectionist leaning, had been guilty of the enormity of placing an order for 300 pairs of locomotive wheels and axles for the Indian railways in Germany. This is not the first time that a Government order of this kind has been placed with a Garman firm, over 5,600 tons of locomotive machinery having been exported from Germany to India during the course of 1903. When the London County Council placed a contract for tramway rails with a Belgian firm Mr. Chamber- lain waxed indignant; and accused the Council of taking bread out of the workmen's mouths. What will he say of the conduct of the Government which has his whole-hearted support ? But then the ugly suspicion arises that, after all, the cause of the diversion of these orders to Germany may be that British engineering firms are too busy to accept orders for prompt delivery. A good Pro- tectionist would die rather than admit that trade is ever good in Free-Trade England; but the fact that our exports of machinery last year amounted to the record total of £18,000,600 hardly suggests that our engineering shops are suffering from acute depression.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BAPTIST CONFERENCE AT • WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BAPTIST CONFERENCE AT • WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. England oh " Citizenship."</p>
        <p>The spring meetings in connection with the Hill Cliffe sub-district of the Lancashire and Cheshire Asso- ciation of Baptist Churches were held in the Baptist Church, Golborne-street, on Thursday afternoon and evening. The attendance was larger than usual. A prayer meeting and conference were held in the afternoon, presided ot er by the Rev. H. Dunnico. The position and status of the Association were dis- cussed, and a committee was formed for the purpose of endeavouring to get the Baptist Churches in Earles- town, Widnes, Lymm and other places in the district to join the Association It was also decided that the plan and constitution of the Association should be revised and definitely set forth. A paper on "Citizenship" was read by Mr. E. England. He urged that if the aims of a citizen were, bon6 solely for the advantage of his own country and. to the disadvantage of the people of other countries such citizenship was not onlya mistake but was eventul ally doomed to failure. Most of the terrible wars that the earth had witnessed had arisen, he said, from this narrow, false idea of patriotism and citizenship, and the war which had just sprung up in the Far East could also be traced to the same polluted and evil source. Selfish aims always did and always would work evil and mischief in the world. Mr. England endeavoured to show (1) that it was only by the adoption of Christian principlesthat true citizen ship could be attained and progress realised. (2) That the work of a true citizen was for the practica working of the reformation of man and thus of society and citizenship (3) That the efforts of citizens who had been co-workers with God, however faulty they might have been, had tended to the uplifting of man and of society. Thert was, however, and they were all con- scious of it much work for the citizens of the earth yet to do. To many,man appeared yet to be only in his in- fancy. Even in this country, of which at times we were proud, sometimes justly and sometimes falsely, there were hindrances in the van of progress and the House of Commons which gave some expression of the voice of the people, but very often only the voice of a. section of it by passing Acts, might do more for the uplifting of the nation. Could it be said that the ?aws relating to the drink traffic were satisfactory or that the gambling laws were really meant to check the evils attached to gambling, or could it be said that the recent Education Act had caused justice to be administered with an even balance to the people of the land? Was it surprising that no less than 75,000 respectable and really conscientious citizens, many of them the veritable cream of society, had considered it to be their duty to rebel against the law of the land and for the sake of their children and other people's children, to passively submit to grievous distraints and also to odium and scorn from their fellow men' rather than pollute their conscience, and also in the hope that they could destroy the unright- eous act and thus prove their citizenship. He thought this 75.000 would grow into 759,000 unless the Educa- tion Act was swept off the statute book. A discussion followed, taken part in by Mr. Norman Ralmford, Mr. Drewett, Rev. J. S. Hughes and the Rev. J. C. Elder, of Acton Bridge, Mr. ENGLAND was cordially thanked for his paper and the conference adjourned.</p>
        <p>rea was served in the schoolroom, at which about 70 persons sat down.</p>
        <p>A public conference wus held in the chapel at which not only members of the various associated churches assembled but also a good number of Sunday school superintendents and teachers of the various denomina- tions of the town were present. The Rev. H. Dunnico took the chair and was supported on the platform by the Revs. J. F. Matthews, J. S. Hughes, J. F. Elder and Mr. S. Fea,rnley, the secretary of the Association. During the course of the proceedings a very exhaus- tive and able paper was read by the Rev. J.F. Elder, of Milton Baptist Chapel, Acton Bridge, on the -Relation- ship of the Church to the Sunday school." The meet- ing was then thrown open and a very interesting dis- cussion took place, the following gentlemen among others taking part :—Messrs. Hough, Dale, Buckby, Clatworthy, A. Lloyd Jones, Heywood. England and the Revs. H. Dunnico, J. S. Hughes and J. F. Matthews. A vote of thanks was passed to the Rev. J. F. Elder for his paper. The day's proceedings, which throughout had been of an educational, enjoyable and interesting character and also probably the most successful ever held under the auspices of the Associa- tion, were brought to a close by the singing of the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>TEA. AND SOCIAL.—The second annual gathering of the combined forces of the Choral Class,and the Men's Bible Class, connected with St. Mary's Mission Sunday School, was held in the Sankey Sunday School on Thursday night last. This year a tea was added to the programme, and the catering was entrusted to Mrs. George Clarke, and was in every respect satisfactory. After tea the large room was adjourned to, and the evening was devoted to enjoyment the programme con- sisting of dancing, interspersed with games for the benefit of the non-dancers. During the evening the following ladies and gentlemen contributed vocal items: —Misses Belle Davies and A. Atherton, Messrs. H. INfainley, W. Upton, Dr. Batchan, A. Goulden, and the "Glee party," a humorousl sextet who caused much merriment. The Vicar, the Rev. J. R. Jones M.A., addressed the gathering in his genial and, breezy style, and hoped they would enjoy themselves. It is safe to affirm that all did, and the meeting dispersed at 12.30 after a hearty rendering of the National Anthem. Mr. T. Shoobridge had engineered the affair, and was re- warded by the success attained. Music for dancing was provided by Misses Tunstall, Foster, Jeans, and Miss M. -;tandish, who also accompanied the singers. ACCIDEN'T.—On Thursday morning an accident occurred to Ernest Dumbell, employed at the Tanning Company's works. Dumbell works at the "splitting' machine, and while adjusting tie blade it slipped, and his hand was severely cut. Medical attendance was procured and the cuts stitched.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Finances. The yearly statement of accounts is published in the current issue of the 'Warrington Parish ChurPh Maga- zine. The Churchwardens balance sheet shows that the receipts from all sources amount to 709 6s 2d, of which the offertory has produced £567 IGs 4d, an average of £lO a Sunday, which is considered very creditable.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGID-CLASS MINERA. WATERS.—Advt. Treasure Hunt.</p>
        <p>- _ The treasure hunt is at an end. There was more searching for the £25 medallion stated to be hidden in Warrington on Saturday and Sunday, and now it is announced that the treasure has been unearthed by two gentlemen from Pemberton near Wigan and Pres- ton respectively. Small comfort indeed for the War- ringtonians who have wasted hours in following the " clues " and searching nearly every inch of the waste ground off Wilson-Patten Street.</p>
        <p>UNDER. the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. P.S.A. Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly open meeting of the Buckley-street IP.S.A. Society was held in the Buckley-street f.llethodist Free Church, on Sunday, under the presid- ency of Mr. Greenleaves. The lesson was read by Mr. F. Harter, and the address, which was a very interestingone, was (riven by Mr. B. Richardson, of Liverpool. Mr. J. Hannan gave a fine rendering of "Star of Bethlehem," and Miss A. Woodland effec- tively rendered "The Reaper." The accompanists were Miss M. Plinston and Miss Miller. ASK for CMCNINGHA.3I'S HIGH-CLASs MDIERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Locker for South Africa. Mr. Tom Locker, of the firm of Thomas Locker and Company, Ltd., leaves England this afternoon for South Africa, sailing from Southampton by the Walmer Castle. He is making a business trip and will probably be away some months. Mr. Locker's many friends naturally regret his departure, and on Wednesday evening the members of the Football Club presented him with a silver cigarette case as a token of esteem and a reminder of his connection with the club. In the absence of Mr. Neil, the president, the presentation was made by Mr. Harry Ashton in a -suitable speech, and best wishes were also tendered by other sneakers.—Last evening the Subscription Room members of the Liberal Club also presented Mr. Locker with a small token of their appreciation. The present consisted of gold cuff-links and studs,and these were presented to him by Mr. Councillor Tinnion at a convivial gathering held at the Lion •</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for BES BOCT 3 in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Debate on' "Sport." _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the Fairfield Literary and Debating Society on Monday evening, under the presidency of Mr. T. Hopkins, a discussion on "Sport, and its effect upon the rising generation," was 'intro- duced by Mr. H. Rogers. He argued that sport itself was beneficial to the people, but that it was abused in many ways. He contended that such habits as gambling tended to do sport a great deal of injury. The paper proved one of the best the society has had for the purpose of debate, and many side issues which arose were thoroughly thrashed out. At the close of the meeting a special vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Rogers. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF '</p>
        <p>TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET, WarringtOß.—Advt. Window Cleaner's Death. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at the Police Station on Saturday touching the death of John Evans (29) a window cleaner who resided at 14, New-road Latchford. The evidence showed that deceased was employed by the Express Window Cleaning Company of which the owner and only member is John Leather. On Wednesday morning he cleaned a bedroom window at 178, Wilderspool-road. It was thought that in Preparing to descend he placed his foot on some iron Spouting which gave way and preeipitated him to the ground. He died at the Infirmary from a fractured .skull.—A verdict of Accidental death" was returned. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Ship Canal Appeal Dismissed. The case of the Salt Union, Limited, v. the Man- chester Ship Canal Company, on Thursday came before the Court of Appeal on the appeal of the defendants from the judgment of Mr. Justice Wright, given on a special case stated by the arbitrator. Mr. Justice 'Wright found in favour of the Salt Union's contention, and awarded them compensation in the sum of £.6,500 and an annual payment of £l,lOO on the arbitrator's -award, hence the present appeal of the Canal Com- pany. Without calling upon counsel for the respon- dents on the appeal their lordships affirmed the deci- sion of Mr. Justice Wright, and dismissed the 'appeal with costs.</p>
        <p>The Productions of Gartons Limited. We have received a copy of a catalogue of new and improved breeds of farm seeds for 1904, which has just been issued by Messrs. Gartons Ltd., the well- known Warrington firm of seed specialists. It is an attractively-arranged booklet and contains full details of this enterprising firm's productions which cannot fail to be of value to agriculturalists. Special- ities offered for the first time are the new breeds of " Colossal" oat and " Eclipse" six-rowed chevalier barley, and the testimoni- als published as to the splendid yields obtained from the seeds during past seasons are con- vincing evidence of their excellence. A surprisingly, large number of awards have been gained by cus- tomers with produce grown from Messrs. Gartons' seeds and the catalogue contains letters from customers from all parts of the country in favour of the new breeds. The purity and germination of all the seeds are guaranteed. Sequel to a Ship Canal Collision. _ _ _</p>
        <p>In. the Admiralty Court on Tuesday, before Sir F. Jeune and Mr. Justice Barnes,sitting as a divisional court, the hearing was resumed of the case of the Zillah Shipping and Carrying Company, of Warring- ton v. the owners of the steamer Millicent on appeal by the defendants from an order of the Liverpool County Court judge.—ln this case plaintiffs brought an action in respect of a collision which took place on the 6th January, in the Manchester Ship Canal between the steamers Sarah Brough, belonging to the plaintiffs, and the steamer Millicent, of the port of Newcastle. The defendants put in a plea of both to blame, and the learned County Court judge found that as a fact both vessels contributed to the collision, and directed that damage should be assessed on the footing that each part was to receive against the other the half of a sum not exceeding £OO. Upon this the defendants appealed.—Their Lordships now allowed the appeal, and ordered that the damages should be assessed on the basis of half the amount of the actual damage "sustained, the half trot to exceed the sum of £3OO. No costs were allowed on either side.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES Of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warrinaion.--Advt. Mr. Tom Boothroyd's Orchestra. •</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, a popular concert was given in the Parr Hall in aid of the District Nursing Asso- ciation by the members of Mr. Tom Boothroyd's orchestra assisted by some solo vocalists. The attendance, though not crowded, will probably be regarded as a satisfactory outcome of a deserving effort to benefit a local institution. The orchestra, it will be remembered, is of somewhat novel composi- tion, comprising as it does, a mass of plectral instru- ments, to whose weak tones support is given by a contingent of violins, a 'cello and bass, and the piano- forte. Mr. Tom Boothroyd conducted and some engagingly melodious selections and adaptations were Performed in an ensemble style which greatly pleased the audience. Miss Nolland, Miss Mabel White and :Miss Annie Warren also sustained solo mandoline and banjo parts in a manner which reflected high credit llpon themselves and their tutor. Mr. Boothroyd 'himself also contributed as • a violin solo two bracketted items. Songs, at the instance of :4iss Emma Shaw, Miss Helene Chalmers and ltr. G. H. Ditchburn were likewise agreeably interspersed in the programme, while Mr. J. Mac- clermott amused a section, at least, of the audience, with some songs. distinctly redolent of the Music Hall stage. H. M. AswroN's LIFE Tome is the Km or Toxics.-63, &amp;mar-snow, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A Ship Canal Accident. In the early hours of Sunday morning the Lamport and Holt steamer Canning collided with the embank- ment of the Ship Canal at Acton G-range, with the result that a number of forward plates were torn off. The vessel was unable to resume her journey until a diver had made a temporary repair of the damage. Whist Drive at the Academy. The members of the Warrington Society held a very enjoyable whist drive on Thursday evening at the Old Academy. About 60 ladies and gentlemen were present, and the games proceeded with an interval for refreshments, from 8 p.m. until midnight. Messrs. S. Wilson, J. A. Ransome and F. Cr. Taylor acted as secretaries and M.C.'s and to them much of the credit for a successful function is due.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Dramatic Society. A second instalment of ;UO brings the total of the proceeds Of the performances of "Lady Clancarty " to £6O, which is a credit to all concerned, and a sub- stantial subscription to the District Nursing Associa- tion. It will be of interest to know that despite adverse conditions over 1900 persons were present at the recent performances of " G-rimaldi " given in aid of the infirmary. The Hon. Secretaries ask us to state that it would be a great help to them if ticket holders would make early returns to the Infirmary. BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 35, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Women's Employment Bureau. It has been felt that there is a great need in the town for an office where women in search of work, or who wish to take lodgers, can have their names registered, and where those who wish to employ them can go to make inquiries. A special ,committee of the Town Council, consisting of Alderman Smethurst, Alderman Pierpoint, Councillor Ashcroft and Coun- cillor Arthur Peacock was appointed to consider the matter and the scheme was fully discussed by them with Mrs. Clay (guardian of the poor), Miss Broadbent and Inspector Hackett (N.S.P.C.C.). It was decided that a room in the Gymnasium should be set apart for the purpose, to be open to the women applying for work every Tuesday and Thursday afternoon from two LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SIIOES, UNDER, the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Ad?t, • The Field Club. At the seventh winter meeting in the Museum Lecture Room, Mr. May presided over a small audi- ence, and Mr. G. Carruthers read a paper on "De- generation in plants and animals." He pointed out a number of facts which afforded strong arguments for regarding such organisms as the yeast plant as degenerate; the principal evidence for this belief being the methods of reproduction,which,under certain conditions, closely resemble those of many fungi. A similar argument was adduced in respect'of many bacteria. Mr. Carruthers also held that the common mussel and the lancelet (Amphioxus) are essentially degenerate organisms,a view whicb,so far as the lance- let is concerned, was vigorously contested in the dis- cussion which followed' the paper. The concluding portion of Mr. Carruthers' stimulating address was devoted to instances of the degeneration of organs as distinct from that of organisms, especial attention being given to the modification of the wings in such birds as the penguins, and to the disappearance of the hind limbs in dolphins, whales and their allies, which are unquestionably the descenditiits of land quadrupeds modified for and adapted to a marine life.</p>
        <p>Warrington Caledonian Association. The annual business meeting of this Association was held in the Lion Hotel on Wednesday evening last, the President, Mr. James 3fonro, occupying the chair. The reports of the Treasurer aud Secretary were submitted, and showed the organisation to be in a thoroughly satisfactory position both as regards numbers and finance, although there was a slight deficit on the last concert. Both reports were unanim- ously adopted. The following office-bearers .and members of committee were then elected, viz. : President, Mr. James Monti), Fir Grove, Latchford; Vice-President, Mr. W. Skinner, Hill Top, Latchford; Hon. Treaserer, Mr. George Bowman; and Hon. Secretary, Mr. B. Williamson; and committee: Messrs. J. Carruthers, J. L. Devonport, J. Finlayson, J. Hunter, J. McDermid, and T. S. Steel, together with Messrs. McGregor, Ewart and Shearer, ex- Presidents. Several new members were elected and the Association looks forward to having a successful year. All Scotsmen in the town and neighbourhood are cordially invited to connect themselves with the Association which is doing a good work in the district.</p>
        <p>St. John's Literary Society. The St. John's Literary and Social Society held the usual weekly meeting ii St. John's Schoolroom on Tuesday evening last, the Hon. President, Rev. J. L. Milne, B.D. in the chair. The President, Mr. W. E. Rees, read a paper on the "Admirable Crichton." Crichton, said Mr. Rees, was born in 1560, his father haying been a high officer of state in Scotland and, his mother descended from the Scottish Royal Family. Young Crichton was believed to have received his early education in Perth and had for his tutors the best masters of the day in every branch of learning. Before he reached the age of 20 he was master of 12 , different languages, and also excelled in manly sports and music and singing. He visited the chief centres in Europe, entering into disputations with the most. learned professors and getting the best of all adver- saries. He finally met with a tragic death in Padua, and in spite of all his attainments left little or nothing by which he can be remembered. His life conveyed the lesson that the greatest talents unless property employed only tend to bring the possessor into disrepute and contempt, Discussion followed, and Mr. -Rees was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for his paper. The next meeting of the Society is on February 16th, when the Rev. J. H. Scott, of Man- chester, will give an address entitled " Scotland and the Scotch—some English misconceptions con- sidered."</p>
        <p>FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Presentation to Mr. W. H. Grimsdale. The employes of the Tramways and Electricity de- partments took occasion on Monday afternoon to present Mr. W. H. Grimsdale, who has resigned the position of chief electrical engineer, with a pair of Ross's prismatic binoculars and a beautifully illumin- ated address as an expression of their kindly feelings towards him on his departure from Warrington to return to the office of Messrs. Preece and Cardew, electrical engineers,lLondon, for the purpose of carry- ing out a Government contract at Portsmouth. The address was signed by 100 employes. It had been tastefully designed and bore the monogram of the , recipient and the Borough Arms. The text was as follows :—" We, the undersigned, officials .and em- , ployes, desire to heartily congratulate you on your 'appointment, and to express our keen regret at your departure from amongst us as chief engineer and manager. Your able administration of the depart- , ments has been conspicuous by its unparalleled suc- cess, and your geniality has so endeared you to all alike that we feel at your departure we are losing, not only a worthy chief but a good friend. Our heart- felt wishes go with you, and we trust you may be spared for many years to use the presentation which accompanies this address." The presentation was made• by Mr. F. V. L. Mathias, who referred to Mr. Grimsdale as a very able adminis- trator. Most of those present he said had been with Mr. Grimsdale from the first, and they knew the hard work which had been done, and the enor- mous amount of organization which had been required. It had been his (Mr. Mathias's) good fortune to serve under many chiefs in various parts of the conntry, but he could truthfully say that he had never come across one who had been pleasanter to serve under than Mr. Grimsdale.—ln accepting the presents, Mr. Grimsdale said he could assure them he should always value the presents, and they would often remind him of the men who had been under him, and the loyal manlier in which they had supported him, and the earnest way in which they had carried out their duties. The officials of the Corporation had been passing through troublous times, and the support he had men- tioned had helped them in a great degree and had almost neutralized the trouble and inconvenience which to a certain extent had been put upon them. He hoped he might be allowed to say on his retirement that he trusted they would support Mr. Mathias, their new chief, and also the new tramways m(anager, as they had supported him.—Mr. F. WlPurse mains engineer) in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Mathias, said that although that gathering was a joyful one, he felt that it was, to some extent, like a funeral service, as they were burying their official relationship with Mr. Grimsdale, but with the highest honours possible. It was a pleasure to know that Mr. Mathias was to be their future chief, and that they had such a splendid administrator at their head. The motion was carried by acclamation. Three hearty cheers were given for Mr. Grimsdale, and one for "the future Mrs. Grimsdale."—The interesting proceedings , then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 13, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 13, 1904</p>
        <p>tion and suffering, and never must we go back to them. All over the world where protection fexisted wages were low and food high, so that we were practically asked to consent to have wages reduced and the cost of living increased. (Applause). Captain G. R. CROSFIELD proposed, Councillor Peter Peacock seconded and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes supported a vote of thanks to Mr. Joseph Howes,and the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETINGS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETINGS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE FISCAL QUESTION AGAIN DISCUSSED.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING ADDRESSES.</p>
        <p>The fiscal question was further discussed at two additional meetings held under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association during the past week. A meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Winwick-street, on Tuesday evening and another meeting was conducted on Wednesday evening in St. James' Schoolroom, LatchfOrd. Both meetings were well attended, and the addresses of Mr. Joseph Howes of the National Reform Union, delighted and in- structed the audiences. His addresses were marked by lucidity and force and were illuminated with some humorous touches.</p>
        <p>At the meeting on Tuesday evening, Mr, Thomas Birchall, J.P., who may be considered one of the veterans of Liberalism in Warrington, presided. He was accompanied on the platform by Captain Crosfield Councillor James Evans, Councillor Peter Peacock, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, William Furness, A Downes, J. Elwell and W. Clegg. The CHAIRM IN said the subject they were to hear a good deal about was, one of very great importance to everyone,"and yet, singular to say, less than 12 months ago nobody dreamt of its being sprung upon them in this way. Why was it? He had his own explanation and it Was this: They had had a Tory Government in power for eight years—the most powerful Government in numbers in his remembrance, with immense majori- ties at their.back—and that Government had followed out the natural instinct of Tory Governments. They gave a sop of a few millions to the landlords • gave ditto to the parsons; the), muddled the Education that the people asked for in such a way that thousands of our best citizens declared that they would break the law for conscience sake ; and they dragged us into the war which had turned out to be the most discreditable war we had ever had. What did Mr. Chamberlain practically admit last week? He dared Sir Henry Campbell- Bannerman to speak of a conversation he had with him before the war. Sir Henry took up the challenge and said that Mr. Chamberlain told him, we were not going to fight in South Africa. we were ONLY "BLUFFING "THE BOERS.</p>
        <p>That word would stick for a long time in the ears of the people of the country. The man who played with the lives of thousands of men like pawns on a chess board—the war cost 250 millions and thousands of lives—was the man who asked them to take his word on this fiscal question. They remembered what it was said they would get as the result of this war. He (Mr. Birchall) did not believe it. He was what was called a Pro-Boer and a Little Englander. It was said that we went to war to secure the rights of the Out- landers, and to get him the franchise. But where was the Outlander to-day? He was never mentioned. The great mining magnates wanted to introduce Chinese labour on a system which was practically slavery. It meant practically introducing slavery into one of our colonies, and that was all we had got for the tremendous sacrifice of life and money we had made. Truly we were a nation easily gulled. (Applause.) As Carlyle said, this nation consisted of forty millions—mostly fools—but even fools began to see by-and-bye, and the nation was beginning to open its eyes and ask who was the instigator"of this piece of blundering. It was:Joseph Chamberlain, and Mr. Chamberlain knew it would want</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>STIFF RED HERRING DRAWING ACROSS THE TRAIL</head>
        <p>STIFF RED HERRING DRAWING ACROSS THE TRAIL</p>
        <p>to draw the minds of the electors off it. He thought the fiscal question would do it, and that was why it had been introduced. The strongest arguments against Mr. Chamberlain's scheme were found in his own speeches of two or three years ago. All the figures he had put forward had been disproved, and now he dared to say that the time before the repeal of the Corn Laws was a good time, and the people were well off. He (Mr, Birchall) knew something about that time;and he did not agree with Mr. Chamberlainat all. (Applause.) He lived 16 years in that time and could say that if the present day working man had a month of it he would soon decide to make short work of Mr. Chamberlain and his col- leagues. In the days of Protection in this country a clever joiner could earn £1 a week,and a bricklayer 18s. or £l—less than a labourer got to-day. We sometimes said that fivepence was a dear price to pay for a 41b. loaf, but in the days he was refeiring to he had paid double that price. Looking back on the old days of Protection was like looking back upon a hideous night- mare. God save this country from going back to it. It was impossible to increase the price of the necessaries of life without making the position of the workingman worse. He was glad to see so many practical hard- headed workingmen present to consider this question. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MR. HOWES' ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>MR. HOWES' ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH HOWES said before they could properly understand the present state of things they must con- sider the causes which had led up to them. As a Liberal he protested against this latest endeavour, of Mr. Chamberlain's to divert public attention entirely from a lot of other things which they as Liberals con- sidered it necessary to refresh the minds of the electors upon before going to the polls. (Hear, hear.) The way the Government had disorganised the finances was a very serious question for a great commercial country like England. When the Government came into office eight years ago the National expenditure was rapidly approaching £100,000,000. He remembered how eloquent the Tories were in speaking of the extravagance of the Liberal party, but since the Tories came into office the National expenditure had been increased to nearly M 00,000,000. The Liberals left them a handsome surplus. How did the Tories get rid of it? They commenced office with</p>
        <p>A PERIOD OF DOLES. In the first place they took off the tax on "works of art and articles of virtue. Then by means of the Agricul- tural Rates Act, one million pounds per year were put directly into the pockets of the landlords. The owners of land in Ireland were next the recipients of a dole and following them came the parsons. In this way the Tories dissipated the surplus which the Liberals had, created, chiefly through the statesmanship and Sir William Harcourt, who introduced graduated death duties. (Hear, hear.) The Tory Government got into power •by promising old age pensions. They had the money with which. to establish the nucleus of an old age pension scheme but instead of keeping their promise they gave thelmoney to those who had plenty. When they wanted to take any taxes Off they went to the mansions; and when they had to put any on it was the workers who had to suffer. That was Toryism, but it was not com- mon sense. (Applause.) Home legislation had been sadly neglected. Education had been thrown into chaotic confusion. The Education Bill originated with the late Lord Salisbury who told his followers that they were "to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CAPTURE TILE BOARD SCHOOLS."</head>
        <p>CAPTURE TILE BOARD SCHOOLS."</p>
        <p>Why? In Board schoois they had the children of tbe working classes receiving the best elementary education that this country had ever provided. When Mr. Foster's Bill was before Parliament in 1870, it was stated by the opponents-of the measure that it would be dangerous to educate the children of the working classes, because if they were educated they would not work. (Laughter.) The Tory party perhaps got that idea from the fact that many of the educated rich did not like work. (Laughter.) The work of the country continued the speaker, would be much better done by an educated working-class than by an illiterate one. (Hear, hear.) It might be that an educated workingman would ask that the wealth created should be more equally divided. (Hear, hear.) The revelations of the War Commission had showed how the Government had treated "Tommy Atkins." Just as the people were beginning to realise the blunders of the Government in the conduct of the nation's affairs, Mr. Chamberlain rushed' to Birming- ham and made the marvellous discovery that the Empire was in danger. The Empire was all right when the Government took over the conduct of it, and now, after eight years of their ruling, it was in danger. (Applause.) Mr. Chamberlain said it was, and they could • think for themselves what had caused it. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain said that the Colonies were about to tear themselves from us unless something was done—then that must be because Mr. Chamberlain was at the Colonial Office. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Cham- berlain had made two statements which were THE GROUNDWORK OF TFIE PRESENT AGITATION. These were that the only way to save the Empire was preferential treatment for the Colonies, and (2) taxing the people's food. Mr. Chamberlain appealed to the working-classes; but they might say with truth that the empire had nothing to do with them. They did not own it. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain was appealing to the wrong people. He was asking the working-classes to make sacrifices to keep id existence something they did not possess and something they would never possess. (Hear, hear.) The class Mr. Chamberlain ought to have appealed to was the landlord class—the ground rent receivers, the mining royalty owners. (Hear, hear.) If Mr. Chamberlain had called these together and said the empire was in danger, they would have had a bad half hour, because they owned it. Mr. Chamberlairf might have said "there is only one way to maintain it and that is by taxing ground rents, and mining royalties." (Hear, hear.) If he had said that his platform would have been deserted in five minutes. (Loud laughter.) Some day we should learn that the rent producer was of more importance than the rent vreceiver. (Hear, hear). The Colonies could much better afford to make a sacrifice for our workers than we could for theirs. Mr. Chamberlain asked us to go back to protection. Pr3tection was an old thing. It wasitried and discarded when the people could no longer live because of it. (Applause). Those days were the days of Englishmen's greatest degrada-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MEETING IN LATCHFORD WARD.</head>
        <p>MEETING IN LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Warriniton in Protectionist Days.</p>
        <p>Speech by Councillor Pierpoint, Mr. A. H.</p>
        <p>Crosfield, and Mr. Joseph Howes.</p>
        <p>; There was a large attendance at a meeting held in St. James' sehool under the auspices of the Liberal Association on Wednesday evening. The all-absorbing fiscal question was once more under consideration and the audience followed the speeches with deep interest. Alderman W. Pierpoint, J.P., presided, and was sup- ported on the platform by Mr. Joseph Howes, of the National Reform Union, Councillors Tinnion, Peter Peacock, and James Evans, Messrs. A. Rathbone, W. Furness, C. Jackson Holmes and J. Elwell. The Chairman said it afforded him a great amount of pleasure to preside at a meeting of Latchford Ward with which he had beenconnected for more than fifty years. Although he knew that the ward had had the reputation for a good many years of being a Tory stronghold, he hoped the time was opportune for the present meeting. (Hear, hear). The great question of fiscal reform was a matter of exceeding importance to every man, woman and child in the kingdom. It was a very singular fact that the statesman who was primarily responsible for the breaking-up and disorganizing of the Liberal party was now engaged in a similar opera- tion in connection with the Tory party with which he had been identified Csince he left the Liberals. As a member of the Liberal party he (the speaker) was</p>
        <p>VERY MUCH OBLIGED TO MR. CHAMBERLAIN for having caused that division among the Tory party which had:had the effect of sending back to the Liberal party or at any rate bringing into close touch with it, some of those great statesmen who were the life of the party prior to the introduction of Home Rule. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Chamberlain professed that he could give them exact rules whereby they would have constant employment at increased wages, It reminded him (the speaker) very much of the quack doctor who had got a universal panacea for all diseases ; but he thought it would be a great deal better if the people did not take any notice of the medicine that was offered.(Hear,hear.) Mr. Chamberlain's policy, in his opinion, had been for- mulated not so much for the future as to cover up the mistakes that had been made by the Tory party of late. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Chamberlain was a very clever elec- tioneerer, and he was prepared to say and -do anything almost in order to maintain the Conservative party in office, but he (the speaker) was afraid that if they did remain in office they would go on repeating the great blunders they had already made. It was impossible to tax food and raw materials without increasing the cost of living and restricting onr markets. it was obvious that if they taxed anything the price would be in- creased. He failed to see any advantage that might possibly accrue from the making of bread or clothing dearer. He thought it would be a great deal better to maintain the present position and have cheap food and clothing. If people would only spend less money on drink, which, he believed, did no good to a.uy man, we would not have so much of the present misery which was attributed to want of Protection instead of to its proper cause. Many businessmen jumped at the conclusion that Protection would make them rich. He was not going to deny that it would make rich some ,:of those great trusts which had the power of controlling their output and manipulating their productions. Protection might possibly be the means of making some of these men— the members of the iron trade, for instance—immensely rich, but if they made one man immensely rich•they ought to consider where the wealth would come from. (Hear, hear.) He was inclined to think that the in- creased wealth due to Protection would in all proba- bility have to come out of the pockets of the artisan population. Alderman Pierpoint proceeded to give a picture of WARRINGTON AS IT APPEARED IN 184.5</p>
        <p>—before the abolition of the Corn Laws. The popula tion then numbered about 15,000 or 16,000. There was not a plate 'glass window in any shop in the town and the principal ',industries were provided by a number of small tanneries and a very small wireworks, belonging to Messrs Rylands, in Bank-street. There were only two pair-horse • carriages in Warrington— (laughter)—and no cabs, because people could not afford to pay to ride about in cabs. Two little omni- buses plied between the hotels and the station, and there was a chaise which was used to convoy people to small parties. There were a good many small breweries most of which, with the exception of Walker's and Greenall's, had now been swept away. They had no wealthy men in the town, as far as he could recollect, but there was a great deal of misery. The highest wages paid at the tanneries ranged from 12s. to 15s, per week, and he recollected seeing bread shops which had been broken into by starving men for the purpose of obtaining food for their families. Altogether the con- dition of things was deplorable. To-day Warrington had a population of nearly 70,000 and there were evi- dences of very great progress in material wealth and in the comfort of the people. Warrington in 1845 was</p>
        <p>TYPICAT. OF ENGLAND AT THAT PERIOD, and the condition of the cOuntry was one of intense misery, dissatisfaction and poverty, from end to end. Warrington's wealth and great industries had been brought about by the abolition of the Corn Laws. It was a very striking fact that at the recent Labour Con- ference at Bradford, delegates representing 900,0'00 man voted against Mr. Chamberlain's policy, and only 30,000 in favour of it. That, to his mind, was conctlu- sive that the Trade Unionists, the pick of the artisan population, had made up their minds, and if they stuck to their position there was no doubt as to what the result of the next election should be. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ADDRESS BY MR. HOWES.</head>
        <p>ADDRESS BY MR. HOWES.</p>
        <p>Mr. HMVE3 observed that after eigat years of Tory rule, they ought to be able to come to some conclusion as to the advantage or disadvantage which had CJIII3 as the result of that administration. If they wanted to know why the flical proposals had been sprung upon the nation it was necessary to turn their attention to some of the things which had happened since the Government had been in power. (Haar, hear.) As Liberals they did not intend that the next election should be fought entirely upon the fiscal proposals. They would be criminal as a nation if they overlooked the crimes com- mitted by the present Government. The Tories were full or promises at the electiOn, but those promises had not been followed by performances. The speaker pro- ceeded to refer to these promises in detail, and in referring to the question of</p>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS said this matter ought to have been settled long ago. The Colonies had solved this problem, and if they had why not the mother country? Our poor laws were out of harmony with the spirit of theage. They were passed in the reign of Elizabeth, and althoughlthey mightihave been admirably adapted for that period, they were not adapted for this. (Hear, hear.) Quite a large sum was collected for the relief of the poor but only a small sum reached the poor. There was no branch of internal administration where there was greater leakage than in connection with our poor law administration. If they encouraged a system of old age pensions it would be a great financial saving to the nation and it would relieve the nation in many ways. But when the Tories pro- mised old age pensions they were only trifling With the question. Mr. Howes proceeded to show that in- steadof utilising the surplus w'aich the Liberals lisft, them, and which might have been used in the establish- ment of an old age pension scheme,the Tories frittered the money away in doles to the rich. He also showed how the Government had</p>
        <p>NEGLECTED HOME LF.GISLATIDN. In Education, they had created such astate of confusion that respectable citizens were being dragged into police court proceedings and once again the goods of Dissen- ters were being confiscated in order to keep up doctrines to which the Noiconfor nist conscience did not sub- scribe. T'na E Incation Bill had brought about a re-im- position of religious tests and the re-introduction of church rates. (Elear, hear.). This was not a healthy state of things and could not possibly be allowed to continue. The country wanted a scheme of education for the people—one that, lwould link the a. b. c. class right on with the University. (Hear, hear.) The wok of the nation would be better done by an educated rather than an illiterate working-class. Where there was any heavy solid work required to be done, the Britisher beat the world, but when it came to beauty of design and colour, the foreigner walked right past us. We had been developing the- muscle and neglecting the brain. (hear, hear.) Some of our competitors had been developing both, and we needed to provide the necessary educational machinery that 'would enable the people to more successfully tight the battle of life. In referring to the so-called religious education, he said creed was not religion and religion was not creed Religion was truth. (Hear, hear.) An alteration in the present system must take place. The conduct of the Government whilst prosecuting the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICAN WAR</head>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICAN WAR</p>
        <p>was scathingliCriiici;edab-y-'.t-he'leaurer, who spoke of the cruel treatment of Tommy Atkins. Discussing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CRAWFORD3'</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD3'</p>
        <p>SCOTCa SHORTBREAD The " Crawford" Quality.</p>
        <p>HIGHLAND. Thick Cakes. LOTHIAN. Tnin Sections, sugared on top AYRSHIRE. Thin oblong Cakes, sugared on top. Sold by Grocers and Bakers everywhere.</p>
        <p>the results of the war, ho asked what about the position of the white man now? The white man was to be superseded by the yellow man and this Government which talked of stopping alien immigration here was prepared to sanction the importation of Chinese labour into South Africa. If alien immigration was not de- sirable here it was not desirable in South Africa (Hear, hear). Mr. Howes, in dealing with the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FISCAL POLICY</head>
        <p>FISCAL POLICY</p>
        <p>pointed out that in every country on the continent where protection existed, the wages were much lower than here. If the Government intended to tax food, then it was reasonable to ask the Government to pass a law to enforce the payment of higher wager to meet the extra expenditure (Hear, hear). This taxing of the people's food struckat the poorest class of the com- munity. It struck at the smallest incomes. It struck at the children, and when the Government struck at his child be was prepared to strike the Government as often as possible (Hear, hear). We gave our colonies the privilege of open ports but they did not return the compliment. Mr. Chamberlain Wished the workers of this country to make further sacrifices for the colonies, but our Colonial friends ware better able to make sacrifices than we were. Mr. Chamberlain had advised us to think imperially That was all very well if we were allowed to eat imperially. (Hear, hear). At the Liberal meet- ing held on the previous night, the Tories distributed literature on the fiscal question, containing all the</p>
        <p>OLD FALL A.CIES. One leaflet went back to the oldargurnent that although the bulk of our trade was increasing in all parts of the world, yet we suffered from excessive imports. It was argued that if our imports and exports were equal we were doing well, butniflour exports were one pound and our imports five pounds, then we were in a worse posi- tion. Mr. Howes showed that this was an absurd fallacy. He demonstrated that our imports were pay- ments for our exports and that unless our imports exceeded our exports we should soon be a bankrupt nation. A shopkeeper, he said, who gave a shilling for anarticle and only got a shilling back again would not keep open very long. But if on the other hand he received five shillings for an article which only cost him a shilling he would be on the high road to prosperity. (Hear, hear). That England was increasing in wealth was shown by the income tax returns. The Blue Book that was brought out to curse Free Trade remained to bless. We were the greatest traders in the world. Our shipping indus try which had made ns the greatest carriers in the world was built up by Free Trade. When our shipping industry was protectel under the Navigation Laws it was insignificant. We had tapped all the world and mdae every country contribute to our necessities and- comforts. Comparing the position of the workers in England with those of Germany and the United States, Mr. Howes showed that under Free Trade, the British workman Wag able to enjoy greater comforts, less hours of work and greater spending power in his wages. Mr. Howes quoted from a grocer's bill of 1820, and compared it with a grocer's bill for alike quantity of goods at the present day. The prices of goods in 1820, he said, were as follows :-21bs. of lump sugar, 25.; 11b. of moist sugar, 9d. • tea, Bs. per lb.; yellow soap. 10d. per lb. ; currants, is. id. per lb. ; raisins, 10d. per lb. ; salt, 141bs. 4s. 9d.; candles, 3d. per lb. ; ground coffee, is. 81. ; starch, 11d, per lb. ; pepper, 11b. ls. He showed that whereas this bill amounted to 21 is. 7d., the goods could now be bought for ss. 81. That, con_ tinned Mr. Howes, showed the difference between Pro_ teztion and Free Trade. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion he urged his hearers to go forward and not backward, as would be the case under Protection, and bring into o ffice a saner and sounder set of politicians. (Applause.) QUESTIONS. •</p>
        <p>Replying to SOM3 questions from the audience, ffr. Howes said the question of railway rates WAS undoubt- edly one for the Government to inquire into. Ile thought steps should be taken to make the railway companies lower their rates for the transit ofraw materials and food products. Asked if he thought the Liberals believed in old age pensions,Air.Howes said it was the Lilieral party that started the agitation, and with the money raised by the death cluties,he believed the Liberals would have formu- lated an old age pension scheme if they had remained longer in power. With the increased national expendi- ture, old age pensions were further off than when the agitation was started. When a scheme was brought forward he believed it would come from the Liberal party. '(Hear, hear.) SPBECFI BY MR. A. H. CROSFIEL D.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosffeld, who had a cordial reception, said Mr. Howes had given them an address so eloquent, 'so vigorous, and covering so much ground, that any observations of his must needs be tame by comparison and contrast, because unfortunately he (Mr. Crosfield) did not possess the gift of tongues, or the wonderful gift of humour which enabled Mr. Howes to make his facts both palatable and powerful. Mr. Howes spoke of the Blue Book and the contradiction the facts in it were to those jeremiads which Mr. Chamberlain had been preaching in the coun- try. Contradiction was in the air just now, and a political farce was being played that surely beat any- thing outside "Alice in Wonderland." They had the Duke of Devon Shire exposing with a closely reasoned, and at times delightfully ironical analysis the flagrant contradictions in Mr. Chamberlain's speeches; and then what an exposure the debate now in progress in the House of Commons was' of the chaotic state of mind of this ricketty, ridiculous and discredited ministry. (Hear, hear.) Ministers contradicted themselves One after another. It rather reminded him of a story of Dean Ramsay's. It was a story of a SC3t3ll minister praying for fine weather, and he daresay they kaew that Scotch ministers used to have a somewhat easy and col- loquial style in addressing the Deity. As Dean Hole told them this was not by any means a token of disres- pect, but a proof of the mere affectionate intercourse and a closer gym.? Way than were generally bestowed upon their fellow men. And so this parson prayed as follows Noo, Lard, Yell nae send us wand and bitter blaudia' showers and cranreuch cald; bat ke'll send us the bonnie summer weather and the braes- sunshine, and the dooce. . ." aad there he stopped for at that moment there Came a raging, tear- ing laughter)—'ae begged pardon— t roaring gust of wind, which brought several tiles down off the roof with aclatter that alarmed' the caugregatioa. Tne uahaep minister beheld the catastrophe with dismay and be- wilderment, and looking upwards he exclaimetreprotch full y."Lard,Lard this is parfactly ridicule ns."(Laughter) A great many of Mr. Chamberlain's argumentsandstate- meats, to say nothing of the spectacle in the House of Commons, were equally ridiculous. (Hear, hear.) This Scotch story 'reminded him of a letter he had just re- ceived from another Scotchnun, who wrote some two hundred miles south of Khartoum. It really was extra- ordinary how these Scotchmea managed to invade all parts of the world ; one might alms t say" They in- herit the earth." (Laughter.) He hoped they would not accuse him of levity in quoting from this letter because he noticed during theGateshead election it was claimed, that all the sportsmen were on the side of Chamberlain, and he (Mr. Crostield) wanted to prove that that was not so, because these were the remarks of a snertsmam vary well-known to the golfing world, Mr. John Lowe who was Captain of the English team that went to play America a short time ago. "I warn you," wrote Mr. Lowe "against this fellow Joe. Have no truck with him. (Laughter.) He isout of the bottom drawer and should on be put back in his own place." (Renewed laughter Fkid cheers). A writer in the loaal press had been complaining very much of the way in which he (Mr. Crostleld) had criticised Mr. Chamber- lain's reckless and culpable diplomacy during the fatal summer of '99, front which he held that</p>
        <p>M. CHANIBERLA.IN'S BLUFF AND POLICY at that time was chiefly responsible for the terrible mischief and evil which had followed. He was not going to cover the old ground he had already traversed in letters to the Warrington Press. All he would say to his unknown friend, "Mr. H. F. F.,"was that he gave him entire credit for good intentions, but if he would just come down from the clouds—lnd good intentions did not always carry them in that direction—if he would come down and attend to facts he would find that all he (Mr. Crosfleld) had written in reference to the Raid and this diplomacy—or ratheinegation of all decent diplomatic methods—(hear, hear,)—in 1899 and its con- seluences was absolutely correct. Perhaps the writer he was referring to would even remember that though we hat( to deal with a Tory in President Kruger in his old age,—and under the circumstances,no wonder—(hear, hear)—still the progressives among the Boers had made great headway, and their Government offered us great concessions. (Hear, hoar.) While the English working- men were then earning a pound a day wages in the mines—(hear, hear)—what were they earning now? What chance was to be given to them? It was pro- posed to supersede them, not even with black labour, in. South Africa. 'but to import yellow labour from the East to take the place of the very Uitlanders on whose account chiefly we were induced to spend two hundred and fifty millions of money, and lose in killed wounded and disabled' nearly a hundred thousand men. This was his answer to "Mr. H. F. P." (Cheers). The Birmingham missionary told them that we had made a fetish of Free Trade. Within the last six or eight months he (the speaker) had had the advantage of talking the question over with a great number of manufacturers as well as a great many people outside and inside Parliament, but only once had he met with a man who said that under no circumstances did he think it was conce!vable that it woad be to the interests of this country to round off any rough corners in our Free Trade system. That was another matter he would not investigate that night because it was thoroughly threshed out in the correspondence he had with Mr. W. P. Rvlands not long since. They did not make a fetish of Free Trade: It was possible to conceive circum- stances under which exceptions to the general rule, the great principle of Free Trade, would be expedient and necessary. But at the present time they were not even going to look at the possible exceptions. That was not their business. They were faced with a monstrous and pernicious proposal tending.—let Mr. Chamberlain deny it if he liked—to make a few of the rich richer, but certainly to make the poor poorer and if it was going to do nothing but that it stood absolutely condemned, not only by every Liberal. but he hoped he might say by every Liberal Unionist in the country as well as by every man with a right sense of justice or with any love or real progr ss in his heart whatever. (Cheers.) Chatuberlainism had certainly had a very big slump and for the present it was beaten. But this hydra monster of Protection was scotched,not killed. The deathblow</p>
        <p>'must come at the next election. (Cheers.) The missionary who had preached the gospel of protection with such energy was off for a holiday. But he would come back; he would find friends waiting for him. Many of them who lately called themselves "Whole hoggers" were now masquerading as Free Traders, but they were only waiting till they thought it safe to take the Protectionist plunge. Therefore Liberals must be on the alert. The enemy was adroit and subtle and stuck at nothing when it came to a pinch. The attack might be sprung upon them by other methods, and I therefore wherever, he would not say this missionary, but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>THIS POLITIC AL INC EN D lAR Y</head>
        <p>THIS POLITIC AL INC EN D lAR Y</p>
        <p>went about the countryspreading the conflagration of Protection in the midst of our commerce, they must have their Free Trade Fire Engines ready to put it out. They owed it as one of the first ditties of citizenship to see that the life work of Mr. Gladstone and other great English statesmen was not undone. Speaking of Mr. Gladstone, Mr. Crosfield afterwards gave a brief description of Lord Renciell's beautiful • residence at Cannes, where Mr. Gladstone paid many visits during the later years of his life. Alluding to the matchless beauties of the garden and park at this lovely spot, Mr. Crosfield said such surroundings must have gladdened the close of Mr. Gladstone's life, and even soothed the pain of its calamities. Perhaps hearing so much of late about Mr. Gladstone had made one pender on the change thatlhad taken place in our political and national life since he left us, and since for so many years Toryism had run rampant. One heard a good deal of self-styled statesmen from Birmingham, and one could hardly help contrasting the sham with the real article. Not only had they the savings effected during thirty years of cautious statesmanship dissipated and squandered, but surely in many of the methods adopted in recent years there were signs of a vulgarity—(hear, hear)—, a lower- ing of the tone of English life, confined fortunately to a very few individuals, bat which, he thought, they s!aould never tolerate even in them: Let them remem- ber how Mr. Gladstone always a,poelled to the noblest instincts in human nature, and the highest ideal of public life, striving, all his days to make a much preached but.—in bractised Christianity,a reality instead of a mockery and a sham. As regards</p>
        <p>THE IMMEDIATE PROSPECT-. Mr. Howes had told them was before them.. Thera were many many questions on which they were only too willing to fight the election; questions of deep importance and value to the nation at large. Still, as he had painted out, two questions were in the fore- ground, and these certainly had hai a fine effect in welding together all branches of the ProTressive Party. These two questions were of course Free Trade and Education, both of them concerning so vitally every household in the country, that ono would almost hope they might have something like unanimity amongst the working class in the fight for objects of such priceless value. That in itself was undoubtedly the main motive that would prompt all of them to do their public duty, and their very best to make their victory in the big fight decisive and complete. But in addition to that primary and all-important motive he thought thatall of them might feel proud in knowing that at the same time they were fighting, to defend what had been achieved for them by the''life-long work of their greatest statesmen in the last century, Sir Robert Peel and Mr. Gladstone—(Cheers)---backed up by those splendid tribunes of the people, Richard Cobden and John Bright. Let them preserve their work and the standard of life so far won for the people, and instead of listen- ing to wretched reactionary proposals let this be the starting point for further progress towards a higberand nobler civilisation. (Cheers.) Mr. Crosfield concluded by proposing a vote to the Chairman and Mr. Howes. Councillor Tcs-Yfos seconded. He said it seemed like "old times', when they had the healthy:robust Liberal- ism of Alderman Pierpoint helpilg them. Warrington had laboured in the darkness of Toryism since 1880 and he thought that if they could have the benefit of Mr. Howes' services fora few days before the election, he would make such an impression that the result, of the contest would be as gratifying as when Mr. J. G. 3rc Minnies was returned. The resolution was heartily carried and Mr. Hawes responded.</p>
        <p>The expressed the hope that the profound and stirring address of Mr. Howes would have its effect upon the waverers and would strengthen those who, like hinnelf, had never wavered on the question of Free Trade. He hoped all present would hold fast to Free Trade and Liberal principles and they would never regret doing so. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>" King Olaf."</p>
        <p>An intimate knowledge of Dr. El,gar's earliest im- portant work—the "Scenes from the Saga of King Olaf"--leads to the conclusion that it will often he judged unfairly by an audience hearing it for the first time. The detached way in which the scenes are necessarily presented mast always tend to minimise the impression made upon an unprepared listener, richly expressive though much of the music portraying them admittedly is. It is particularly pleasing therefore to record the fact that Wednesday's audience assembled in the Parr Hall proved somewhat of an exception in this respect. T 9 the credit of oar reputation as a musical townspeople let it be said that the magnificent proportions and the dignity of Elgar's work were early recognised by the audience, and that its fascinating intricacies were followed by them with a manifest intelligence and a clearly expressed appreciation. It is doubly pleasurable therefore to associate one's-self with the feeding that arises on the unfolding of the tone-picture of " ging Olaf" that the composition is one of no common order, and also to testify to the , fact that one rises from the revelation of its beauties with an unsatiated sense which, taking the form of a strong desire for a deeper acquaint- ance, becomes in itself high praise of the work. What it is of supreme interest to note is the fact that the music of" King Olaf" bears the unmistakable imprint of modern--:let us also say English—ideas and methods. Dramatic force and vivid colour are its pervading characteristics. What perhaps one does feel to be a little fatiguing is the ceaseless movement and the rest- less tonality of the. 111USiC, an objection largely disarmed by the very exigencies of the text, yet applicable in a general way to much that Dr. Eiger has written. Great energy and a ready flow crf ideas in both the vocal and the instrumental parts are ever-present feataces in the score. And combined with these is a weu th of melodic resource which gives a fi.nr.) luminosity to the whole work. The choral part-writing is by no means easy, and owing to its being often involved with the parts for the principal voices, it calls for extreme care in interpretation. Over these obstacles the Musical Society's members triumphed signally on Wednesday evening, notwithstanding the fact that the rank and file seemed thinner than usual in paint of numbers; the exacting chorus for the ladies "Sigrid sits inher high abode " deserving special mention by reason of its freedom from serious blemish. Indeed it is remarkable that, in such a work as this, the only noticable slip arose in an altogether unlooked-for place in the part for the principal bass. The challenge of the god Thor is a peculiarly effective and forceful number, and in point of massiveness marked perhaps the culmination of choral effort. The two strongly con- trasted choral ballads. "The Wraith of Odin" and that embodying the delicious bit of gossip about Thyri, the fair " may be cited as instances both of the composer's versatility of method, his prodigality of , tone-colour, and of most praiseworthy ensemble singing. On the other hand, for complexity of effect which, while no doubt largely appropriate to the text, ap- proaches dangerously near to depicting also a literal babel, commend us to the chorus in which the death of Olaf is described. But with a touch of real genius this tumult is used as just the necessary foil for enhancing the beauties of the unaccompanied part-singing in the Epilogue, the weird melodies and harmonies of which fitly close, as they also open, the work. Here the society's chorus members did some really conscientious work and, while preserving throughout a well-balanced volume of tone, they admirably kept up the pitch The voice parts, like the instrumentation, embody the free use of representative phrases and themes, which, if not exactly used in the Wagnerian manner, serve to define easily traceable lines of thought which go to make up the warp and weft of the music. In dealing with these LcitarAire and interpreting the rich orchestration overlaying the vocal parts, the society's instrumentalisth (augmented as usual) acquitted themselves admirably. The principal Parts were in the hands of Miss Agnes Nicholls, Mr. Lloyd Chandos, and Mr. Robert Radford, and they proved a thoroughly capable trio, the last-named making a most favourable impression at this, his first appear- ance at these concerts. The trying duet between Miss Nicholls and Mr. Chandos "The gray land breaks to lively green," proved a most important and enjoyable number.and its magnificent vocalisation made it difficult to pacify the audience with the mere bowing of acknowledgments of long-continued acclamation. The society's staff officials, Mr. Crossley, Mr. Pearson and Mr. Payton, were at their accustomed posts as con- ductor, leader, and organist, and thereat each mid all did yeomen service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>B kRLEST3Wg DRAM %TIC PARTY.</head>
        <p>B kRLEST3Wg DRAM %TIC PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the members of this psrty gave an entsrtainment in the Town Hall, Earlestown, before a large and appreciative audience. The items sub- mitted were the p Ith 3ticl drama "Sunshine through the Clouds," and the farce "Slasher and Crasher." Both pieces were excellently represented, and it would be invidious to „single out any individual for special men- tion. The dramatis persons in "Sunshine through the clouds" was as follows :—Lientenant Frank Cleveland, Mr. S. D. Own: Esteourt, Mr. Frtnk Owen; Old Sandford. Mr. R. King; Mrs. Cleveland, Miss Mather; Emily Cleveland, Miss K. Holdsworth 'Harriett Treveleryan, Miss Ba.ybutt. "Slasher and Crasher" was supported by Mr. T. D. Owen as Mr. Benjamin Blowhard; Mr. A. Fairchild (Mr. Sampson Slasher) ; Mr. Tom Kenny (Mr. Christopher Crasher), Mr. J. Astill (Lieutenant Brown), Miss Ambrose (Miss Dinah Blowhard), Miss N. Mather (Miss Rosa). During the evening selections were given by the Earlestown Orchestral Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>A Wet Season Reviewed.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The numerous events of the past cycling season—one of the worst on record as far as the weather conditions were concerned—were interestingly reviewed at the annual meeting of the Warrington Cycling Club held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday evening. Mr. W. Brown presided over a small attendance and at the out- set expressed regret that the bulk of the members did not take a more active interest in the business meetings. THE SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>•rln his annual report, the hon. Secretary (Mr. E. PICKAVANCE, jun.,) congratulated the members upon the fact that a fairly successful season had been manu- factured out of what at first sight appearedto be excep- tionally poor syclin,g material. The weather was un- doubtedly the worst oxperienced since the formation of the club. Run after run to the most popular and beautiful resorts had to be cancelled owing to the per- sistent downpours of rain, but the regular attenders of the runs rose to the occasion and the cycling season of 1903 was saved.</p>
        <p>A. STUDY IN CONTRASTS. The report continued :—lnstead of exhilarating spins over the rolling moorland, gay with golden gorse and purple heather, it became a series of mad rushes through the drenching rain to the nearest shelter. Instead of sweet, indolent loiterings through shady avenues, &amp;c., it was a question of "cussed" waits at draughty railway stations for trains which always seemed to be late. Instead of peaceful strolls in dear old Cheshire, with the air laden with the scent of wild roses and honey- suckle, it was a case, as Shakespeare observes,of " the water free curls through the twisted roots." Laughter.) However, the season provided many pleasant memories and happy recollections of glorious little outings. THE SEASON REVIEWED. Mr. PICKAVANCE then reviewed the events of the season,commencing with the run to Warburton on March 28th. Special reference was made to the visit to Beeston Tower at the invitation of the respected president (Mr. John Naylor) on July 11th. Those who made the journey by road had to return through a "veritable deluge of rain" but nevertheless,the afternoon would long be remembered with pleasure. On August Bth the old-time fixture known as 'President's Day" was honoured and despite the wet weather Me, members journeyed to Chester and welcomed the President. The annual fishing match at Acton Bridge resulted as follows :-1, J. Podmore ; 2, J. Waywell ; 3, A. Knowles. The ancient game of bowls had been well to the fore this season,and proved as attractive as ever to the members. The first Tuesday night main was started at Winwick, and after an exciting contest was won by V. Barber, J. Podinore being second, S. H. Antrobus third, and H. Whittle fourth. The second Tuesday main was cancelled owing to rain. An: innovation tried this year had met with great success. The Committee took: the "Chapel House " green for the season on Wednes- day nights, for the use of members and friends exclu- sively, and it was gratifying to note that the green had been very well patronised. Two matches were played ' with the Clock • Face C.C., the Warrington C.C. winning the first and the visitors the second. Arrangements had practically been made to have the green on Wednesdays during the comity* season. In that case all the bowling mains would be played on that main and Members would have no cause to complain of Tuesday evening runs being spoiled by bowls occupying so much time. The holiday tours, though now mostly carried out by motor-cars or trains, had been greatly enjoyed. At Easter, Barmouth, Llangollen, and the North \Vales coast claimed a large number of "our boys "—and girls also. The weather varied in the extreme, and on Easter Sunday one party had the unique experience in the Berrwyns, of gathering primroses one hour, and the next snowballing one another. At Whitsuntide the weather was lovely and Llangollen, Ellesmere and Derbyshire were visited by various sections of the club. The August Bank Holiday tour was marred by the ever-present wet" but a few of the hardier spirits visited North Wales and Mid. Cheshire whilst others crossed the bortliq tut+ tried in vain to "lure the wary Scottish trout with Anglo-Saxon tackle." The attendance at club runs had avetaged 12 for Saturdays and 14 for Tuesday nights, and although there was considerable room for improvement, it was a very good average for such a wet season. The attendance prizes had been won by F, Garnett and W. Taylor for Saturday and Tuesday runs combined, whilst J, G. Chappell took the prize for Tuesday night ruas only. An allusion wag made to the Lifeboat Saturday demonstratipn hi which the club took an active part, and to the sucdFssful series of social functions. Hearty l thanks were accorded to the artistes and the aceompanists(Messrs. F. Booth, J Grounds, and W. H. Kendrick). The club membership now stood at 73 active and 11 honorary members—an increase of 10 upon last year. Thanks were due to the donors of prizes and to Miss Kievnan for the free use of the room at the ".Patten Arms" hotel.</p>
        <p>-64 OBITUA.RY. It was with feelings of the deepest sorrow, ccaltinued Mr. Pickavance, that he referred to the loss that the club had sustained during the year by the death of two of the quietest and most respected members. On May 19th Mr. George Whitehouse was taken from their midst after a brief illness. His genial and unassuming manner had made him many friends and his presence had been greatly missed at their various functions. On Saturday, December 26th, Mr. Joseph Faulkner passed away after only three for four days' illness. His memory would long be cherished by the members, as he was one of as decent a set of boys as ever belonged to the club.</p>
        <p>LOOKING FORWARD. Tn conclusion they could confldently assert that the club was still meeting with a good amount of success. To show improved membership, better finances, and more sincere good fellowship, after such a damning season, proved that the club had still as much grit in it as ever, and if the members generally would take an active interest in all that appertained to the harmonious and successful working of the club, they would have a club second to none in the north, and a credit to the whole cycling community. (Applause.) 4Mr. LITTON proposed the adoption of the report. It did not matter under what adverse circumstances the club had met during the season, the Secretary always came up smi;infrb with his report. (Hear, hear.) It appeared to be absolutely perfect, and chronicled th&amp; doings of the club in a manner which reflected very great credit upon Mr. Pickavance. Mr. G. H. SMITH seconded. The CHAIRMAN remarked that each succeeding report wai better than its predecessors. The proposition was heartily agreed to. • THE FINANCES. T.ll TrI3N. TREASURE:I. (llr. F. Garnett) presented the balance sheet, which showed that after meeting an estpeaditure of £2 183. 51., there was a credit balance on th 3 year's working of £1 3. 3d. In addition there was a oalance i the bank of L',3 143. 91, making a total to the credit of the club of £ll 18s. Mr. PICK..A.VANCF. moved the adoption of the balance sheet which, he said, was one of the best they had had for some years, and reflected great credit upon the treasurer for his able management of the finances. Mr. liNo,vt,ss seconded the motion, which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>THANKS. On the rttltion of Mr. J.IL. DAVENPORT ssconded by Mr. J. SA.MPSON, jun., a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the secretary and treasurer. In moving a similar compliment to the Captain (Mr. W. Taylor), sub-captain (Mr. J. Poimore), and com- mittee, Mr. Pickavance said that despite the wretched weather the captain and sub-captain had attended a splendid percentage of runs. There had never been fewer than eight out of tha eleven members of the committee prasent at the meetings and everything had been carried out harmoniously. Mr. H. HoPE seconied the prlpo3itiOtl which was heartily carried and Mr. W. Taylor responded. Thanks were also voted to Miss Kiernan for the use Af the meeting roo-n, and to the auditors (Messrs. J. G. Chappell and J.Grounds).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CONDOLENCE.</head>
        <p>CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>The members pissed a vote of condolence with Mr. Arthur Booth, and Messrs. Robert and Joseph Daintith in recent family bereavements.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr..Tohn Naylor wis tinani-nonsly re-ele3ted presi- dent, the SEC RETARY remarking that there was not a more enthusiastic cyclist in Great Britain. The honorary vice-presidents were re-elected and it was decided to invite the Mayor to accept a similar office. The acting vice-presidents were re-elected with the exception of Councillor T. J. Hulse. rathough Mr. PICK A vANC F: stated that in view of his long servtces in that capacity he would be willing to resign the office of hon.secretary in favour of a younger And more enthusiastic member, it was the unanimous Opinion thathe should continue the duties which he has discharged so admirably. He was therefore re-elected With enthusiasm. ,</p>
        <p>The other elections were RS follows --Hon. treasurer, Mr. F. Garnett; captain, Mr. D. Hodge ; sub-captain. Mr. E. H. Clare; auditors, Messrs. J. G. Chappell and J. Grounds; N.C.U. delegates. Messrs. E. Pickavance kud A. Booth; committee, Messrs. W. Brown, G. H. mith, W. Taylor, J. L. Davenport, J. Sampson, jun., H. Bellian. and J. Podmore. vote of 'thinks to th 3 Chairma.n concluded .the meeting.</p>
        <p>THE MODEL HOUSE OF FURNITURE.—Those of our readers who contemplate furnishing cannot do better than send for the illustrated booklet just issued by Mr. Samuel Pitts, of London-road, Manchester. A glance through its pages convinces one of this well known and success- ful firm's ability to cater for all classes, but the special object of the booklet is to call attention to the model 200 guineas house of furniture. All the rooms are superbly equipped and cannot fail to satisfy the taste of the most fastidious. As instancing the extent of the firm's operations, it may be mentioned that they have had the honour of executing orders for the Sultan of Morroco, the Duke of Devonshire, and numerous mein- bers of the nobility.</p>
