      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>I MID-CHESHIRE TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>I MID-CHESHIRE TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>I To incorporate the Mid-Cheshire Tramway Company and to empower that Company ,to make and maintain Tramways and other works in Mid-Cheshire, is the title for a Bill now deposited in Parliament. The promoters are Messrs. Leonard Cooper, George Pear- son, and Thomas Carter Beeley. By Clause 6 of the Bill," power issought to purchase from the Stockport and Hazel Grove Carriage and Tramways Company, Limited the present system of horse tramways, running from St. Petersgate to Hazel Grove and it is intended to electrify this portion of the tramway and continue the system through Poynton, Adlington, and into the Borough of Macclesfield where a complete system of Borough Tramways is to be organized and ultimately it is intended to connect up the districts of Sandbach, Crewe and Nantwich, with a branch line from Broken Cross near Macclesfield, to gnutsford and Warrington. A generating station is to he erected at Poynton on the land belonging to Lord Vernon in close proximity to the Colliery and from where power-gas will be used. Powers are also sought in the Bill for supplying elec- trical energy to all the authorities on the route includ- ing MaccleSfield,Poynton,Adlington and Prestbury.These districts will therefore be able to obtain a supply of electrical energy for distribution at a very cheap rate, having the advantages of generation on a very large scale. The time for completion of the work is fixed at five years. The gauge of the tramway, in order to meet the approval of the Council, has been fixed at 4ft. 81in. Cheap fares are to be given to the labouring class arid power is taken also to carry small parcels. The capital or the company is to be £200,000 in 200,000 shares of £1 each. The Company however will not be permitted to go to allotment until one fifth the amount of such share capital has been subscribed. The Company seeks power to borrow on Mortgage or otherwise a sum not exceeding in the whole £30,000; interest is to be paid out of capital during construction. The number of directors is 4 and the qualification is the holding of not less than P5OO shares. The first directors are Leonard Cooper, George Pearson and T. C. Beet ey The estimated cost of the undertaking is £197,000. A syndicate has been formed of local gentlemen interested in the area with a registered capital of £lO,OOO. The Bill comes up before the Chairman of the Standing Orders Committee on the 15th and the necessary pro- portion of the consents of the Local Authorities have been obtained and the Bill will be reported for first reading. The Bill for the construction of the Tram- ways within the Borough of Macclesfield deposited last Session, to which however the County Council objected on the ground of the narrow guage has been withdrawn. The promoters of this Bill are in no way associated with the former scheme. Messrs. Lacey and Siller of Manchester and Queen Anne's Gate Westminster are the Enzineers and the Solicitors for the Bill are Messrs. Rowcliffe and C0.,0f Cross street, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WET WEATHER DANGERS Bila BeallS a Safeguard.</head>
        <p>WET WEATHER DANGERS Bila BeallS a Safeguard.</p>
        <p>In London alone, and on one day only last year, over thirty million tons of rain fell ! What became of all this water, and that which fell during the scores a other wet days? Much of it remained in the 'ground,. making it damp, giving rise to rheumatism and other ailrnents. This month, throughout the country,rheuma- tism, chills, influenza, are ptinfully common. Wet weather means wet feet. Wet feet often mean influenza liver chill, or rheumatism. As soon as you feel you've ctught a chill, take a cap of hot meat extract, followed by two or three of Chas. Forle's Bile Beans, and get between warm blankets. The hot drink °perm the pores, and Bile Beans operate gently on the bowels and the liver, thus preventing the chill from doing further mischief. If you haven't taken the chill in hand soon enough and it's got .741tu in it's grip, don't despair. Same remedy! Mr. J. Atherton, of Ormskirk-road, Pemberton, says: "I neglected a chill, and it turned to influenza. "This broka;me down . campletely—des- troyed appetite power of digestion, brought on attacks of dizziness, he tdache and depression— played havoc, with my entire system! Liver be- came disordered, giving ma pain in the side and between shouldens. Hed constipation, and then piles. Doctor failed. Friend said Bile Beans wouldn't fail. Tried them. They didn't. They restored both my appetite and my po ver to digest nourishing food. After that it was plain sailing and I soon got back my strength." Nluch mare evidence could be quoted to SAM?, effect. Best proof is self-trial. If you've caught sold, suffer from indigestion, constipation, piles, rheumatism, or any liver or digestive trouble, try them. Bought from any chemist for or 2s. 91., per box (family size). Same prices direct from Bile Been Co., Red Cross- street, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>N)RTHERI LEAGUE MEETING.</head>
        <p>N)RTHERI LEAGUE MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this league was held on Tuesday at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, Mr. J. Platt, Oldham, pre- siding. 'The- following rearrangid dttes were agreed to : York "A " v. ‘Vo..kefield " A," April 30. Replayed match: St.. Helens v. Rochdale Hornets, MondAy, February 22.</p>
        <p>Barker, of Bradford,-reportel for kicking in the match Oldhan v. Bradford, January 23, suspended up to and including February 27. Mr. Compton, Bradford, brought a charge of stone throwino• against the Normanton spectators after the match Islormanton. v. St. Helens on January 30th, and after careful consideration the committee decided that notice of warning be posted on the Norrnanton ground and that Normanton pay the expenses of the officia Is attending this inquiry. Kelly, Oldham, for delib3rately kicking, suspended to February 20th. Wolstenholme. Batley, and Stacey. Keighley, for striking each other, the former to Februi ary 29th, and Stacey to February 13th; Pickard, York, and Burnett, Hunslet, for wrestling, to February 20th; D. Peeston, Salford, for striking, to February 13th; Crabbie, Rochdale Hornets, deliberately striking, to February 20th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MEETING OF NORTHERN UNION COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>MEETING OF NORTHERN UNION COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held on Tuesday night at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, Mr. It. G'ollinge (Rochdale Hornets) in the chair. The following rulings, asked for by the Lancashire and Cheshire Referees' Society,were given :-1. Suppose a full back runs back over his own goal line and then kicks into the field of play, but is then deliberately ob- structed, what is the correct rulingl?—Aaswer, A touch- down should be allowed, as per byelaw 20. .2. A neutral touch judge fails to put in an appear- ance. The home committee arrange for one of their members to take half the game, and the visitors the other half, although knowing at the time that there were neutral • touch judges on the ground. Is it fair for such a policy to be adopted under the circumstances? The matter was thoroughly discussed (by the Referees' Society), and it was decided to place the question before the Northern Union Con-nittee,—with a recom- mendation that where possible neutral touch judges should be requisitioned in preference to club men.—A. The Northern Union Committee decided that where possible neutral touch judges should be requisitioned in pref rence to club men. The minutes of the last Professional Sub-Committee meeting and Cup Committee mosting were confirmed, with the exception of the ruling given by the last- named committee on the 20th ult., with respect tii qualification of players for cup-ties. Messrs. W. D. Lyon, Hull, and J. E. Howarth, Millom were elected members of the committee- in place of Messrs. Hildreth (resigned) and Fawcett (deceased) respectively. The explanation of the Batley Club with respect to their agreement with D. Thole was considered satis- factory. A grant of £5O was made to the Cumberland County Union. The Secretary was instructed to make inquiries into the case of E. Sleep Hull Kingston Rovers, who is at present an inmate of Mr.• Allison's establishment at Manchester. It was resolved to put the order for the Challenge Cup medals up for competition, and that Messrs. Elk- ington, Lloyd. Payne, and Amiel, and Fattorini and Sons be invited to furnish designs. The committee decided that they could not interfer with the sentence of " sini die " passed on J. Garrety</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>.„.„„ - , -</p>
        <p>NOW IS THE TIME.</p>
        <p>Do you enjoy perfect health? If not,why not? The simplest way for man to secure immunity from disease is to follow, as far as practicable, the laws of nature, and if illness overtakes him, to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The only natural remedies are vegetables and the chief vegetable remedy of the present age is vegetables, Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from debility, nervousness, and general ex- haustion, and if any value be attached to human testi- mony the efficacy of this medicine is established. Its claims have been tested and proved by. the medical profession and others, and car oborated by the written testimonials of eminent men. OUGHT TO BE IN EVERY HOUSE. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contains not only a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose. but the active principles of the following well-known herbs—sarsapar- illa, saffron, gentian, lavender, burdock, and dandelion. At this season of the year no one should be without Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Best Remedy of the Age for various ailments. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is recommended for indigestion in its different forms, such as sick head- aches, heartburn, cramo, flatulency, sense of fulness and oppression after eating, drowsiness, and pains in the region of the heart. It is, without exception, the best remedy for depression or spirits and melancholia. Being at vegetable "Pick-me-up," it is strongly recommended for nervous diseases, such as undue anxiety. despond- ency, fainting' fits, neuralgia, and nerve pains generally STRONGLY RECOMMENDED. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has successfully treated (after all other known preparations had failed) severe cases of affections of the chest, such as common colds, bronchitis,asthmatic colds,shortness of breath,influenza, etc. It purifies and enriches the blood. It is a healthy and safe tonic. Should be taken by all who are deli- cate,a:: it strengthens the system and promotes good health. People suffering great weakness, either after fill illness, or through long confinement in ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause, find Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters most valuable as a great strength reviver. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE IhtrEßs. Gwilym Evans' Quiniae Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d.and 4s. 6d. each or will be sent,on receipt o stamps, carriage free, direct from the Sole Pro- a*ietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. BEWARE OF IMITATIONS. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>When you ask for Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, see hat you get it, with filename "Gwilym Evans" on the abel, on the stamp, and on the bottle, without which one is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BEFORE THE XAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE XAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>VIOLENT ASSAULT ON A TRANI CONDUCTOR. 'At the Warrington Police Court yesterday, Samuel Barlow, Folly-lane, was summoned for assaulting Joseph Greenwood, tramcar conductor, on January 30th.—Mr. Steel represented the defendant and on his behalf pleaded gnilty.—Mr. Stanley Wilson. depaty town clerk, Prosecuted and said the defendant boarded a cir at the Cemetery and went inside. The conductor noticed that be stretched hi i legs across the car in such a way as to interfere with the comfort, of passengers, and requested him to alter his position. Defendant took no notice and at Butterrnarket-street two old ladies were inconvenienced in getting out of the car. On arrival at the Central Station terminus the conductor went on. the top of the car to change the trolley pole. When ha came down the steps he found the inside of the car was full and defendant was sitting sideways so as to take up the room of three passengers. Greenwood asked the defendant to sit straight but he refused, and when asked where-he was gtlinp, to told Greenwood to mind his own business. With the assistance of the driver defendant was got off the car and he immediately struck Greenwood a heavy blow on the back of the head with a stick. Greenwood told the driver to start the car and he would jump on at the back, but as soon as the driver went away defeedant again struck the con- ductor three or four violent blows with his fist, and kicked him on the knee cap. As a result of the ..assault Greenwood had suffered considerable pain in the head and had been unable to work for a week. The case was a serious one, and called for a heavy penalty.—Mr. Steel said that in regard to , the ole matter Mr. Barlow's mind vas a blank. He had questioned as to what took place, but could give n•-) account of the affair. Their Worships would know , his peculiarities and on this occasion he had had some drink. Defendant was vary much upset and could not, appear. He Wt s willing to pay the conductor any com- pensation the magistrates might suggest and he (Mr. Steel) thought the ease would be met by the:payment ,of compensation and the costs.—The magistrates fined defendant 5:1-and cost, and assessed the compensation t 305., making a total of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Warri.l,gton B3raugh Pa!lca Cyart.</head>
        <p>Warri.l,gton B3raugh Pa!lca Cyart.</p>
        <p>IVlinar Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. Birchall (presiding), Hay- ward, Wright, Alderman Smethurst, and Councillor Thornton. ANNOYING WORSHIPPERS.—SiN. boys named. Frank Ford, 16. Watkin-street; Herbert Dutton,6, Hale-street; -John Jarvis, 33. Foster-street; Joseph Ward, 50, Forster-street; John Rose, 35, Forster-street; and Robert Leigh, 43, Forster-street, were summoned for havine made loud and unseemly noises in West-street, P.C. Hewitt stated that at 7.10 p.m. on the 31st of last month he heard a loud noise in West-street and, upon proceeding down the street,. he found the defendants •shouting and kicking at the door of the Salvation Army Barracks. The Chief Constable pointed out that such conduct ',visa frequent occurrence in that neighbour- hood, and it, had been eemolained about by the Army -captain. In fining each of the defendants 55., Mr. Birchall said they deserved a good birching. It was too had that people who were trying to do good -should be annoyed by voting rascals like these. SCENR NEAR THE CENTRAL STA.TlON.—Patrick and Peter Carey, 42, -Back Dallanl-lane, and Patrick and Josephine Owen, 49, Foundry-street were summoned for having made aloud and unseemly noise in Winwick- street, the previous Monday. P.C. Stewart deposed to having Seen the defendants near the Central Station quarrelling, and using bad language. The magistrates said they. were determined to stop this sort of -thing, and imposed a fine of 53. and costs each. MINoa CASE3.—William GemaK, hawker, 4, Haydock- ,street, and John 8e11,37' Scotland-road, were summoned for having committed a breach of the peace by fighting in Winwick-street on the 23th ult. P.C. Wise proved the case, and each of the defendants was bound over to keep the peace, and ordered to pay the costs.—For hav- ing used obscene language the previous day. Annie .Johnson, of no settled abode, was fined Sc., with the option of seven days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>• .TUF.3DA:Y.—Befora Dr. Fox (in the chair), Alderman Hutchinson, and Mr, J. Rymer Young. D '-`,7K IN Ca IRGE.—Tohn Beck, 3, Mahnesbury- Tplai—. Wilrlerspool-roal, w.ts charged with having been</p>
        <p>.M. E.. 1111.411.</p>
        <p>drunk in chargc. of a horse andjurry at 10.7 the previous night, and further with not having a lighted lamp attached to the vehicle.—Sergeant Smith said that he noticed a horse 'attached to a lurry going in the direc- tion of Wilderspool without any light attached to it, and also observed prisoner, who was sitting on the lurry fell backwards and roll on his side. Upon going to him he found he was drunk. Prisoner was fined 7s. (Id. and costs.</p>
        <p>THURSDA.Y.—B3fore Messrs. John Fairclough (presid- ing), F. W. Monks, C. White, and J. R. Young. SIX VIETII APPEAR ANCE.—Joha Baguley, who made his 69th appearance, was, on the evidence of P.C. Sweeney, fined 20s. and costs, with the option of a month's imprisonment, for having been drunk and dis- orderly in Buttermarket-street on Wednesday evening. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Roberts and Thornton,Kessrs. I H. Roberts, Hepherd and Young. A DISGRACEFUL CASE.—Mary Ann Davenport NAAS charged with keeping a disorderly house inWilliamson's Square and Jane Sprigg was charged with aiding and abetting her. Davenport was further charged with neg- lecting her children.—The Chief Constable said that for horror and squalor the case was almost the worst he had Ihad to bring before the court.—ln stating the facts of the case he said the children were in a filthy, ill-fed con- dition, and were often left in the house alone for hours. Sometimes they were in the care of an old, deaf woman who was unable even to take care of herself.—The magistrates decided to send Davenport to gaol for months and Sprigg for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 13 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 13 1904</p>
        <p>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETINGS AT NEWCASTLE- ON-TYNE.</p>
        <p>INTERVIEW WITH THE REV. THOMAS LAW.</p>
        <p>Undoubtedly the event par excellence in the Free Church world is the National Council of the Evangelical Free Churches, which this year —from March 7 to 10—holds its Sessions at Newcastle-on-Tyne. From Brighton in the "Sunny South" to Tyneside is a far cry, but the Council is indeed national in its operations, and, as is well known, the Free Churches hold a very, strong position in the Northern Counties. A representative of this paper was able to secure an interview with the Rev. Thomas Law, the Secretary of the National Free Church Council, in reference to the meetings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NATIONAL REPRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>I asked Mr. Law, writes our reporter, if the position of Newcastle, in the extreme north of the country, would not affect the representa- tion.</p>
        <p>"By no meats," replied Mr. Law. "Our staff at the Memorial Hall are working at the greatest pressure receiving and entering the names of the delegates going to the meetings. Councils from the extreme parts of Wales and Cornwall are appointing representa- tives, and in the Midlands and North we are especially strong; but, in view of the excep- tional programme, our local Free Church Councils are realising that the question of dis- tance must not he allowed to stand in the way. I anticipate from the returns coming in that the representation will be completely national."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>AN EDUCATION PRONOUNCEMENT.</head>
        <p>AN EDUCATION PRONOUNCEMENT.</p>
        <p>"What will be the most important feature t the gatherings ?" "Undoubtedly, the Education Session. Your readers will remember the historic meeting at Brig Von last year, when the Council was abso- lutely united in its opposition to the Education Act. On that occasion the Executive were given a mandate to prepare a policy for the Free Churches. This has been done, and a scheme for a settlement of the question on the lines of a national scheme of education will be presented to the Assembly for confirmation. This scheme has already received the endorse- ment of practically all the leaders of the move- ment, and will be spoken to by the Right Hon. James Bryce, M.P., Sir George Kekewich, Dr. Clifford, Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell, Mr. D. Lloyd- Georae, M.P., Rev. J. Scott Lidgett, M.A., Dr. Bank's (ex-presiolent of the Wesleyan Con- ference). Rev. J. H. Shakespeare, 141. A., and Dr. Robertson Nicoll.</p>
        <p>WORKING CLASSES, TEMPERANCE, AND MACEDONIA.</p>
        <p>"What other features of interest will there be ?" I asked.</p>
        <p>_ "That is difficult to say, because of the pro- fusion of subjects, all of first-rate importance. To Isention only a few, considerable interest is anticipated in the address which the Rev. J. G. Greenhoiugh, M.A., is giving on Modern Un- settlements in the Christian Faith.' Then there is likely to be an important discussion on The Attitude of the Working Classes to Religion.' Mr. Will Crooks, M.P., Rev. R. J. Campbell, M.A., and Rev. S. F. Collier are to open the subject, which, in view of Match- fordism ' should be of timely interest. Mr. George Cadbury's paper on The Adult School Movement' should be well received. Mr. Cadbury is an authority on the subject, as he rises early every Sunday morning to take part in the Adult School in Birmingham. The Temperance Question will be discussed. Doubtless by the time the proposals of the Government on the Licensing Question will be known and you may be sure the National Council will emphatically repudiate any attempt to restrict the 'discretion of the Magistrates in rrfusing licenses. Macedonia and other press- 41k subjects will also be dealt with."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>NOTABLE PREACFIERS.</head>
        <p>NOTABLE PREACFIERS.</p>
        <p>"You anticipate an important pronounca- ment from your new President, I presume ?" "The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., who will be in- ducted to the Presidentship is known the world over, and his address will certainly be of the greatest interest. He intends to deal with the most practical questions of the hour. We shall have some of our best preachers, too. The Rev. Dr. Fairbairn, Principal of Mansfield College, Oxford, is to give what is known as the Council sermon. This will take place on Tues- day evening. Other preachers are to be the Rev. James Travis, the retiring President, Rev. J. H. Jowett, M.A., Rev. W. J. Dawson, and Rev. John Thomas. The Devotional Session on Thursday should be very helpful and inspiring. Dr. R. F. Horton and Dr. T. Bowman Stephen- son will speak, and Rev. E. Johnstone Ross will deliver the Communion Address." EXCELLENT SOCIAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>"What of the social side of the meetings ?" "The President of the Nexcastle Council, Mr. T. H. Bainbridge, J.P., has entered very heartily into the arrangements for the reception on March 7. The Olympia, which has been engaged for the Session and the principal demonstrations, will be entirely re-decorated and adapted. For instance, one of the gal- leries will be fitted up as a drawing-room, with easy chairs and everything to make the place like home,' as our energetic local secretary, the Rev. William Glover, expresses it. Councillor Hugh Morton is the host for the Secretaries' Conference, and I understand that the Social Gathering of Women Workers will be carried out on a scale never before attempted. The arrangements for the accommodation of dele- gates is very extensive; the Hospitality Com- mittee can at present see its way to providing for at least a thousand. But early appointment is essential in order that no confusion may arise. ThAv I'ave been compelled to fix Feb- ruary 15 as the latest day for receiving the names of delegates requiring hospitality."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EVENINJ MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>EVENINJ MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>"I understand, Mr. Law, that the Midnight Meeting is to be repeated ?" "That is so. It will be held in the Olympia on Wednesday night, and Gipsy Smith (who is to be accorded a 'send-off' on the occasion of his visit to South Africa), Mr. W. R. Lane, and the Rev. 3. Tolefree Parr will speak. The even- ing meetings are likely to be a special feature this year. On Wednesday, the Young People's Meeting will be held, presided over by Mr. A. E. Hutton, M.P., and addressed by Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A., Rev. Ensor Walters, and Rev. Charles Brown. On Thursday, a great Mass Meeting for Men will be held in the Olympia, to be addressed by Mr. R. W. Perks M.P., Rev. Charles H. Kelly, Rev. Bernard J. Snell, M.A., B.Sc., and Mr. D. Lloyd George, M..P. and at the same time the women will hold a large meeting at the Brunswick Chapel, to be addressed by Marianne Farningham ' (in the chair), Mrs. George Cadbury, Mrs. Price Hughes, and Mrs. Charlotte Benham. Still On the Thursday there is to be a Missionary De- monstration at the Gateshead Town Hall, to be addressed by W. P. Hartley, J.P.' Rev. J. Baldwin Brindley, Dr. Clifford, and Rev. F. Lamb, whilst in all the chief towns of Northum- berland and Durham' district meetings will be held, the speakers including practically all our Free Church leaders."</p>
        <p>"You are expecting the meetings to be a success, then ?" "Most decidedly. Indications point to the Newcastle meetings eclipsing all that have gone everbefore,and they should prove a great stimulus to our Free Church life. There is one point you might mention. In view of the large number of representatives appointed by the local Coun- cils the number of personal members is neces- sarily limited. Members of Evangelical Free Churches who desire to become personal mem- bers may do PO, if they apply to me at the Memorial Hall. Farringdon-street, London, E.,C., enclosing 55.. As the work of preparation is enormous, it is most important that appoint- ments should be made immediately."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SECONDARY EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>SECONDARY EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR SADLER LECTURES AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Present-Day Requirements.</p>
        <p>Higher education is such an important question at the present j unet ire that those who are engaged in the development of Warrington's educational scheme are only too willing to avail themselves of expert advice. There was a representative gathering of educationists at the Co-operative Hall. Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening when, at the invitation of the Literary and Philosophical Society, Professor M. E. Sadler, M.A., L.L.D., professor of history and administration of education in the Victoria University of Manchester, delivered a lecture entitled "What are our chief needs in England in regard to secondary education?" Mr. F . G. Ruddock, F. I. C. (president of the society) presided and read a letter from Mr. William Long re- gretting his in ability to attend,owing to an engagement in Loudon. Continuing, Mr. Ruddick said that during the past few years the subject of education had been force' upon them with greater and greater intensity. They were told by the president!of the British Associa- tion that they wanted a large number of extra univer- sities, and, on the other hand, they were told that if they had more universities, the curriculum in the secondary schools did not fit boys very often to take up higher education in the universities or higher kind of technical schools. It was therefore specially implrtant that they should have all the light that could possibly be thrown upon the important question of secondary education. Among the men who were doing such an excellent work in the development of education in this country there was no one who took a more prominent position than Professor Sadler, whom they welcomed that night. It was a subject of congratulation to all of them that he had been able to give them an evening, as his time was so fully occupied. (Hear, hear and applause). Professor: SADLER said [we were living at a time of educational opportunity to which it would be hard to find at the most more than three parallels in our natural history. The re-adjustment of parts of our educational system to the new intellectual and social conditions which prevailed in the world had become a matter of pressing national need. Every great nation was making unextunpledefforts to improve its system of Edncation All felt more and more strongly the force of the great German reformer's maxim "That the safety and strength of a city and of a state reside above all in a good education which furnishes well-instructed,honour- able, and well-trained citizens." (Applause.) In the world might they not say that Great Britain stood for certain ideas and ideals, for fairness to opponents, for toleration, for Empire. combined with local freedom, for national unity, combined with local differences, for steadfast adherence to old ways, combined with a readi- ness in due time to try new experiments? (Hear, hear.) It was in order that England might, be able to give expression to these ideal under modern conditions of international affairs, that: we needed a deepening and a strengthening which would have a beneficial effect upon our industrial and commercial efficiency, upon our social well-being, and as well also upon our political readiness to grapple with the menacing problems which sprang up in a time of profound international unrest. The educational system of a country WAS inter- dependent in all its parts. The welfare of each part depended on the welfare of the rest. He had the honour that evening of speaking with special reference to a portion of the problem, nanaely,that of secondary educa- tion. The CHIEF AIM OF A SECONDARY SCHOOL</p>
        <p>seemed to him to be to train boys and girls to be useful patriotic citizeos,ready and able in the discharge of their professional duties, or in their industrial and commercial careers, to bear the burden of social service and by their industry, by their training and enlightened sympathy, and -by their self-control,to promote social peace, and to further the common and collective well-being, that thus there might be, commensurate with the greatness of our need, and with the urgency of the task which lay before us,an ever-growing number of strenuous workers for the common good, keenly alive to the claims of local patriotism, but none the less sensitive to the larger duties imposed upon us by our citizenship of a great nation and a great Empire, and yet never forget- ful of that wider fellowship which linked together, in high aims ani common work, man and women of like purpose throughout the whole world, or forgetful of that other citizenship for which all earthly ties and duties were bet a preparation. (Applause.). We were passing through</p>
        <p>A PERIOD OF EXTREME DANGER AND DIFFICULTY in international affairs. Who was not conscious of grave fears for the 'future pressing on his mind, and yet conscious, too, of a strong hope that through it all the greatness and, strength of England might become greater and stronger still? In order that we might compass the great work that lay before us as a nation,in order that we might address ourselves with earnestness and thoroughness and with scientific system to the task of social reform in which all, rich and poor must be perfectly interested, did we not naad two great qualities, qualities which could be promoted by a good education —he meant the qualities of steadfastness and openness of mind to new ideas? (Applause). All true education was a combination of opposite qualities, and he would venture mostearnestly to submit that it was not enough by a national system of education to prodince agility of mind which delighted in novelties alone or, on the other hand, to be content with a system of education which persisted too often, in the American phrase, to "stay put." What was wanted was a combination of moral kind intellectual steadfastness, of faithfulness to what had been found to be of tested excellence in the past, with an openness of mind which welcomed and shrewdly tested new schemes and new ideas. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>TA SK OF EDUCATIONAL REFORM</head>
        <p>TA SK OF EDUCATIONAL REFORM</p>
        <p>whether they regarded it from the social. administrative or more strictly instructional point of view, was an exceedingly difficult one. In all countries it was diffi- cult and complex, but in no country in the world was it so difficult and so complex as it was here. He pleaded that, while we should devote to these problems the best of our thoughts, we should refrain from hurried or ill- considered action, and endeavour in all our reforms to preserve as much as might be of what we had found to serve us and the country well in our old methods. The problem was complex because of our combination in Great Britain of, industrial and Imperial tasks. Was there any nation in the world which contained within its borders so many varied types of life and conse- quently so many varied types of social need and social duty? No narrowed stereotyped form of education would suffice for our purpose. We needed a system which included many types of training, all of them rooted in the conviction of national unity and all inspired with a sense of national duty. First, he submitted that we ought not mechanically to copy the educational system of any other country. (Applause.) Education was</p>
        <p>BUT ONE ASPECT OP THE WIIOLE SOCIAL PROBLEM, and, therefore, as the problem for each nation was in a large measure, though not wholly,different,its method of dealing with the problem ought to be different too. On the other hand,in the words of one of the wisest of Englishmen—Bacon—"we should light our can Ile at every man's torch." For instance we might well seek to imitate America, in her willingness to make sacri- fices for education, in a confident belief in the social and economic value of a course of intellectual training which stimulated a man with new ideas and opened windows in every direction. Those who were acquainted with the'great work which had gone forward in national education in Germany would, he felt sure, agree with him in thinking thatiwe could learn much from studying the benefit of a persistent aim in educational policy, as illustrated by what had been done in Prussia or other German States—that we could learn from them the advantage of methodical investigation into methods of teaching, and the great advantage of having a well- organized body_ of secondary schoolmasters, highly cultivated and intending to devote their whole life to the practice of their profession. The second principle he would submit was that we must value and do our utmost to preserve those</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>GREAT TRADITIONS OF CORPORATE LIFE</head>
        <p>GREAT TRADITIONS OF CORPORATE LIFE</p>
        <p>which had been the glory of our higher English secon- dary education. Trt regard to certain sides of their work, it was not too much to say that our great English public schools were the envy of the civilized world. No other schools seemed to have the same power of training men who could govern others. The third principle was to keel) open the way to the very highest places in education for boys and girls of promise and of industry, however humbly born. (Applause.) It had been one of the glories of England that so many of her great men had risen from low station to high office or great influence. The means by which those .men had riseri in the past were the small local Grammar schools from which they passed to the Universities. We needed to translate the old order of things into a new form adopted to modern needs, but there was a great danger lest, in the modern organization of educa- tion, we should, without meaning it, close avenues which once were open. There was need for a wise and well-considered</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SYSTEM OF SCHOLARSHIPS</head>
        <p>SYSTEM OF SCHOLARSHIPS</p>
        <p>skilfully adapted to local needs in eveiyarea,and which VAIN, AND AGAIN, AND AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Again we ask you; to drink Vi-Cocoa. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is neither a medicine nor a mere thirst-assuager. It is a food at the same time that it is a beverage, and thus answers a double purpose in the building up of the human constitution. You can try it free of expense. Merit alone is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and the Proprie- tors are prepared to send to any reader who names this Journal a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa free and post paid, upon receipt of a post card to the Head Office. 80, Bunhill Row, London, E.C.; or you can - purchase a 8d: packet l or 9d.or 15.63. tin from any grocer or-s,tores. yi-cocoa is the cheapest and best food beverage in the world.</p>
        <p>provided not only immunity from school fees but,where such was needed, help towards maintenance in order that parents might be able to dispense with what would otherwise have been an assistance rendered to them by their children. (Applause) It would, however, be an unnecessary expenditure and sociably unwise to multiply those scholarships beyond the necessities of the case. All education must prepare for life, and it was possible to be given an education which unfitted one for the life he should lead. Let us net make the access to higher education too easy, lest we tempted the wrong people to ruin themselves by 4 entering it. When we had enabled those who deserved" such an opportunity to avail themselves of it, let us not make their lives a burden to them with competitive examinations. The fourth principle he wished to call attention to was that the chief force in all education was the personality and example of the teacher. The aim steadily to be kept in view was that we should have in our schools men and women of the highest ability and of the highest character, whose example was an inspiration to those who came under its influence. Without t'aat the most beautiful buildings and the most modern equipments were of small value. His fifth principle was that we should not be wise in England if we set before us too purely an intellectual standard of excellence in secondary education. We did not want to train</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MEN WHO WERE ALL TONGUE.</head>
        <p>MEN WHO WERE ALL TONGUE.</p>
        <p>(Ls,ughter.) Surely deep down in our English hearts was the conviction that we honoured men and women more who did things than people who merely talked about them. Let us not set up a merely bookish educa- tion. We wanted to sea the school turn out boys and girls who were handy, practictl, resourceful, and not above the common tasks of life, knowing, as Herbert said, that it was "a great thing even to sweep a room if they did it for God's praise." (Applause.) They did not want a nurnher of boys and 1.7,:r1s above their work and spoilt for the real tasks of life. For many genera tions we had done too little to make manual work - a conspicuous part of the curricula of the secondary schools. Happily the influence was rising in favour of manual work, and it was a change wholly confirmed by medical advice and physio- logical consideration. (Hear, hear.) A sixth principle was that it should be the chief aim to produce not only intellectual agility but firmness of character, the know- ledge, touched by sympathy, and sustained by faith in unseen things. (Hear, hear.) Proceeding to deal with the chief needs in our system of secondary education, Professor Sadler pointed ont that oar first noel was a stronger parental interest in the intellectnal side of education. More interest should be taken at home in what the boys and girls were actually learning at school. (Hear, hear.) The students ought not to be taken away before they had got the full benefit of the education they needed. Many of our schools were weakened by the fact that boys and girls were taken away too soon.We had</p>
        <p>A GREA.T DEA.G TO LEAIiN in this regard from Scotland where the intellectual side. of education was more highly developed than in this country. We had also much to learn from Germany. whose greatness was largely due to her faith in spiritual and intellectual things. Secondly we needed relief from the conflicting claims of various examinations. (Hear, hear.) The work of many schools was injured by the number of slparate and varied examinations. Our schools needed a clearer aim to work for. They could not gather what wls best and ny)st precious in edustaion by written an3w3r; to printed questions. (Hear, hear.) He hoped nothing would be done to set up a highly centralised state system of leading examina- tions which would be detrimental to local freedom and intellectual initiative. He hoped the State would see its way to work hand in hand with the Universities. (Hear, hear.) This work of instruction and examination ought to be well done, and it was necessary to have men and women of leisure and experience who could give a large amount of their time to this particular duty. The third duty was an improvement in some of</p>
        <p>OUR METHODS OF TEACHING. There was going forward in England at the present time a striking educational movement. He doubted' whether at any previous epoch there had been so widespread or genuine or spontaneous a movement fo, the improvement of methods of teaching in our English schools. Far morel remained to be done. We needed to study how to train boys and girls to express them- selves in their mother tongue. Then more leisure was needed for teachers to study, to travel and to read. (Hear. hear.. We could not fairly expect teachers to assimilate all the new knowledge which wai accumu- lating so rapidly unless we gave them the leisure the means, and the opportun'ty of doing so. We must bring within the sphere of school teaching this wonderful new store of knowledge. It could not be done unless we made the positions of masters and mistresses financially attractive to the best people. More</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL HELP FROM THE -STATE was needed for the proper and tim•?,ly development of secondary education. This was a matter which affected directly and indirectly, every citizen in the country. We could not have good secondary education unless we were prepared to pay for it. (Hear' hear.) He hoped to see town violag with town in the excellence of its educational institutions</p>
        <p>WARRINGToN WAS FAMOUS in the history of English education for having been the home for six years of a man who wrote one of the. finest books on education—he referred to Joseph Priest- ley.(Hear,hea.r.)" Education," said Priestley,"is properly : that which makes the man." (Hear, hear.) Professor Sadler quoted with approval other remarks by Priestley on education, and concluded by referring to the need ' of an improved system of elementary education as a basis for secondary education, and as a moans towards this end he advocated smaller classes in the elementary schools. The Cost would be greater, he said, but it would be worth it. (Applause.) The Rev. E. .T. WILLCOCKS, in proposing a vote of I thanks to the lecturer said 'Professor Sadler was. a ! world-wide authority on all matters appertaining to education. He believed he was responsible for • the Sheffield scheme, and was also to be called in to settle affairs in Liverpool. They had all a great deal to learn. not only as to organisation and eurrieuizt but also as to methods of teaching. They would carry away with them very many valuable lessons from what they had heard frona. Professor Sadler. (Hear, hear.) . The Rey. MORLEY STEYENSONT seconded 'the motion. He said that, although the audience was ricir a large one, it was of a very representative charactif, and he hoped the Professor would feel that he had been sock- . ing to an audience which thoroughly appreciated all he had said and which had been in full sympathy with the sentiments he had uttered. (Applause.) Mr. F. MoNks J.P., expressed the hope that'Professor Sadler would allow the Education Committee to print in full the address he had given. (Applause.) Mr. J. Mooae MURRAY said he wastlad to have the privilege of taking part in the meeting, as it gave him an opportunity of personally expressing thanks to Pro- fessor Sadler, not only for his admirable address, but for the valuable assistance he had given to all those who had a real interest in education. There were . few : men who, in recent years, had done more to influence public opinion in educational 'hatters thae Professor • Sadler. They were indebted' to him for editing that ! fine series of special reports on educational subjects i when Director of Special Inquiries at the Board of Education. Many of them regretted his leaving the Board',but their regret. was turned to pleasure when they found that net only was he still to continue to help , them by pert and voice, but that he was to become a Professor of the Victoria University, Manchester. Then quite recently some of them hadhad the pleasure 1 of gaining much advice and knowledge from a course of lectures which he delivered in the Whitworth Hall. (Applause.) It often happened that the theorist was very much at fault when his theories come to be applied, ! but Professor Sadler had adopted scientific methods, ! which had resulted in his giving to those concerned. advice which was capable of adoption because he had recognized the existing conditions and did not attempt a revolution, but, rather a reformation The attitude he took was exemplified in the paper they had been listening to. He inquired first what they nee led, and finding what was needed he gave them suggestions how 1 to fill that need. It was only by such a method they would be able to learn how to reform their system of higher education. But that reformation would be long in corning unlesa, as Professor Sadler said, there was awakened a strong and real public interest in educational matters, and, he would add, not only in the politics of education, but in the necessity of every boy and girl receiving an education w'iich would fit him or her for their respective walks in life. When would it be said of England," There is a universal belief in the value of education"? That had been said of America,and whilst he did not agree with those extremists who believed that the American or German education was perfect he did think that until there was a universal belief' in the value of education in England. our progress. towards supplying those needs which Professor Sadler hadmen- tioned would be slow. That was not the time to give reasons for saying that,nor for showinghow that interest could be aroused, but he (the speaker) could not resist theitemptation• of pointing out a method of stimulating people to realize the value of education. The standard of education in a town was fixed to a very largo extent by the employers. If the latter were con- tent with a low standard of attainments, allowing their employes to become mere machines, without any further mental development than was to be obtained by passing through a few of the standards of an elementary snhool, then the parents, not having themselves been made to feel their need, Iv:mid take their children from school at the earliest age. If. however, the employers refused to take boys who had not had that education which the Position they wished to occupy demanded, they would find that the children would not be withdrawn from school so early, and they would attend the evening schools, and not as at present consider they had finished learning on their thirteeath birthday. That could net be done at once, it would require due notice to be given and he should almost like to suggest that the Chamber of Commerce and other associations of emnloyers might be asked their *pinion as to the possibility of such a suggestion being carried out. He was encouraged in that because he knew there were in that town many employers who thoroughly recognised the defects of our present system. It might not be generally known that Messrs. Cros- field's, Pearson mid Knowles, Walker's and, he believed, ethers paid the fees in order that their lads might attend the evening classes ; moreover Messrs. Crosfield had recently intimated that they were prepared to pay their boys according to their scholastic attainments. (Applause). He feared he had wandered somewhat from the resolution, but those who endeavoured to adminis-</p>
        <p>SPECIAL TICKSTS, 115113RAulNii LOUAL IOURS IN uunNwALL, ARM 13sCsu :Ens PRINCIPAL sTATIONS. ROOKLRT4 41VINTI- ALL PARTUMLAR4 Now itvc4nY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>ter our education laws were daily reminded that, per- haps, our greatest need WAS a healthy strong belief by the people in the value and neessity of educating their children. (Applause.) Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD, in supporting the motion,said t'heir great need WAS want of interest on the part of all in education, and especially parents. They, as s3hool- Masters, liked to feel that parents took a real interest in education. They ought, he believed, to experiment in new ideas, but they should do so wisely. (flear,hear) Mr. Gow advocated greater freedom in the curricula. If they were going to have suceessful secondary schools thronghout the country and AIM the high hopes and ideals which the Professor had placed before there, they must not be content to place in front of their classes, men fresh from the University in professional teaching Speaking of the inquence of parents he said they seemed to take little or no interest at home in edacatioaal matters, and very rarely visited the schools or studied the reports. If they could put into, operation one tenth of the high aims the Professor had pat before them that evening their educational system would be most rapidly improved. (Hear, hear). Mr. F. W. Moxics expressed the. opinion that the want of interest on the part of ,the parents might arise from the want of encouragement on the part of the teaching profession. Waea p treats went to the school they were not looked upon with very great welcome by the teachers. In Germanyand Switzerland parents were encouraged to visit the schools and were made welcome by the teachers. The Rev. HARVEY Cool( said what we needed was a larger understanding of the application of education to the work of commcn life and social service. If a larger and more liberal education did not make a mason a better mason then it was not of so, very much use cramming his head with knowledge. He was vary pleased with the wty Profeagor S viler had treated what aopearedto him to be spaeial points in the great ques- tion of education.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLY said h 3 could assure his fellow townsmen that WArrington was n4t behind in the ques- tion, as a scheme of higher eincition hid already been submitted by the Elacution. Cammittee, approved by the Town Council, and had also.received such a measure of approval as could be given: by the inspector to the Board of Education. _ _</p>
        <p>Miss BRDADBENC thought that books upon education ought to be kept in the leading library as they would help to cultivate an interest in educational questions on the part of parents. The CHAIRMAN observed that as a scientific man and one who had been trained in a university,he looked with a certain amount of jealousy upon introducing science very early in the school course. It appeared to him to be it very risky thin.g to introduce physical science too early into a boy's trainia.g. The vote of thanks was then cordially passed and Professor Sadler briefly acknowledged the compliment..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>"Friendship" was the subject of the Pastor's monthly sermon to young people on Sunday evening last. The Preacher first spoke of the grandeur and joy of friend- ship and went on to emphasize the tremendous power. of association and the necessity fOr all true friendship, being based upon moral principle. He urged young men and women not to give away' their heart's affec- tions unthinkingly and make friendships hastily, but to be wise in the choice of their companions. In closing the Preacher spoke of the unfailing friendship of Jesus Christ, which was more real and intimate and more satisfying than any earthly friendship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The usual mee-ting was held ou S iada-y, when there was again a good attendance. Mr. S. Porter Walley of Bolton (secretary of the William Mather Ambu- lance Challenge Shield Competition, of the P.S.A. Lancashire Federation), presidel and spoke upon the. pleasant character of the P.S.A. meetings. Referring to the remarks passed occasionally, that the meetings were simply entertainments, he said, no matter who passed those remarks, whether Bishop or layman,they were in total ignorance of the conduct and character of those meetings and were persons who never came to see and hear for themselves. He had great pleasure in giving to them the hearty greetings of. their P.S.A. brethren at Bolton.—Mr. E. Johnson,, 8.A., M anchester, gave a most able and insbructive address upon the realities of life, and based his remarks upon "As it were." He referred to the ideals of monarchs, emnerom, and man of varied stations in life, in the past history of the world, showing that the mind' and ambitions of these persons were simply upon self. Where the ideal came from purity, the person sought not his own good simply, but the good of others-; working, striving, waiting, enduring, and looking for ward to the final, when the Crown would be received' After faithfully serving the groat ideal pattern—Christ, we should thee. receive the real welcome " Well done," from the Great Examplar.—The ,hon. secretary (Mr. Farrar) replied to the Chairman, and returned the com- pliment to Bolton P.S.A. brethren. Although they at Bolton were a younger society. yet they had:now become a bigger one than Wycliffe. He hoped the members of the Wycliffe Society would so labour and serve the best interests of the brotherhood that they would soon be as big as their younger brother at Bolton.—Mr. C. Cotton also took part in the meeting.—Mr. Barnes was the soleist,and sang two,pieces very effectively,namely "Dream of Paradise," and" Sun of my Soul."—Master Pearce presided at the organ. Prize lists were dis- tributed, which contain a splendidj assortment of books.</p>
        <p>FROZEN EGGS.</p>
        <p>We are threatened with another invasion in the form of frozen eggs, this being the shape in which a Canadian proposes to ship them to England. The eggs will be packed as tightly as possible in barrels, but with no straw or any other material between them, and then the whole will be frozen solid and kept so till landed. The eggs must all be quite fresh when packed, and it is held that on arrival, when thawed out and usel at once, they will be in first-class order, and much superior to, the eggs one generally gets. Canada is a great egg-prodßc- ine country, and would be able to send overan enormous quautity.—" Co untry Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LOCAL FAILURE. At the Warrington County Court a receiving order has been made against Joseph Littler, carrying on busi- ness in Market-street, Earlestown, as a grocer and pro- vision dealer and wine and spirit merchant. THE BURTONWOOD LIGHTING QUESTION. On Monday evening there was a large gathering of the ratepayers of Burtonwood at the Board schools to decide whether or not the Lighting Act should be adopted for the district. Mr: R. Forshaw presided, and was supported by Mr. William Fairclough, clerk to the Parish Council. It was unanimously .;ecided to take a poll of the district for the purpose of ascertaining the views of the ratepayers on the question. POVERTY LEADS TO ROBBERY. At the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, before Mr. T. Halton, Thomas Callaghan, Boundary road. St. Helens, was committed to Liverpool Quarter Sessions on the charge of stealing a purse containing 15s. in money and eleven pawntickets, the property of Charles Fryer, 5, Legh-street, Golborne, with whom he had lodged for eight weeks. When the theft was dis- covered prisoner had disappeared, and when he Wa:g arrested the purse and pawntickets were found in his possession.—prisoner now said he was very sorry. 110 was unable to work and poverty drove him to. commit, the robbery.</p>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION IN TETE NEWTON DIVISION. A private meeting of the Labour party of the Newtog Division was held on Saturday afternoon at Newton- le-Willows, under the presidency of Mr. William Speak- man, to consider the question of a candidate for the general election to take the place of Mr. Sam Woods, who, through ill-health, has been compelled to withdraw.—A resolutson expressing the party's regret al' Air. Woods's enforced withdrawal, and thanking him for the services he had rendered on behalf of the Labour interests in the division • a second one to the effect, that,, in view of the attacks upon trade unions with all idea of wrecking them, and on account of the fiscal proposals before the country, the Labour party were convinced ,hat the divission -should be contested ; third, appionting a committee of ways and means as to the best methods of raising sufficient money to meet the expenses of a contest ; a fourth, that a committee should wait upon the Miners' Federation to ascertain if that body did not bring forward a candidate, would it contribute towards the election expenses,were passed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CURE FOR SCIATICA.</head>
        <p>A CURE FOR SCIATICA.</p>
        <p>Sciatica has at last mot a medicine that can and does conuer it. Many eases have been reported of a seriong character, where this remedy has effected a complete and permanent cure. Among these is that of Mr. ItarrY Armitage, Green Side Farm. Clayton, near Bradford? Yorks, who says: "Last November I had a very severe attack of Sciatica, and I did not know what to do for the best. The pain was at times intense, and it WO only with the greatest difficulty that I could get about at all,, in fact, I could not attend to my business pre' perly. One leg was so bad that I could hardly put nIY foot to the ground, and it was impossible for me te walk. After repeated failures with other medicines.' commenced taking Dodd's Kidney Pills,and soon found they were doing me good. I, therefore, used tho:u regularly until the Sciatica completely left me, and I am quite cured. T. can go about my business, which re" quires.me to go out a great deal, with ease and com- fort; this is entirely due to Dodd's Kidney Pills."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>DEATif OF AN OCTOGENARIAN. Oa Thesday Mr. Timothy Simister, of Runcorn, die" in his 83:rd year. He was A native of Worsley, and hack art uninterrupted service with the Bridgewater canals ol seventy years. RUNCORN LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>These annual sessions were held on Monday, Mr. A. Thomas presiding. in his report Superintendent Ennioo said there was an average of 208 of the population to each licence. One hundred and forty-four persons had been proceeded against for drunkenness. 134 of whorl; .were convicted, as compared with 141 persons proceedea against and 135 anvicted in the preceding year. Thit° showed a decrease of one person proceeded against no: a decrease of one convicted as compared with the yea' 1902. He was directed by the Chief Constable to drag attention to the Millstone Inn, Mill-street, Runcorn, kept by Thomas Leyland. The back entrance to the premises opened into a yard common to five cottage°, and proper police supervision was thereby frustrated' Plans of proposed alterations which should have :been placed before the Bench had not yet bee° snumitted. Mr. F. A. Lake, on behalf of Messrs' Greenall, Whitley and Company, said negotiation° were in progress to acquire the ownership of the cot- tages and it was hoped that plans would soon be soil" raitted—A petition for the reduction of licences, partJ; cularly in the neighbourhood between High street alr the river, where one house in 20 was it was stated ah" censed house, was presented by representatives of the,, Free. Churches,the Salvation Army,the "Runcorn Pridei Lodge of Oddfellows and the order of the Sons of Ton,: perance.—The Chairman said the justices were mile': obliged for the memorial, but in view of • the fact the" legislation upon the justices' duty and power in the matter was pending they did not think it was a time t° go into the question of the reduction of public-house' When they knew what the Bill was They would be veil ,glad to do something.—George Partington, grocer nit 'provision dealer, Balfour street, Runcorn, again mnoy application for an off-licence. The Magistrates' Clel said he received notice of the application too late no •the magistrates had no power to grant the licence!, ,Applicant said he would make an application at the nu- ,fourned sessions. The Chairman: Have you any fresebii evidence to bring before the bench ?—Applicant repli l ;in the negative. The Chairman: If you have not we shel :not giaut it ;so you need not bother about it. Serve. ;the notices and come if you like, but I don't supposo. you will get it. Applicant : I will apply in any case' welokemiw..kekioar.o4postmezitee‘woiwoomaze,,tc..</p>
