      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>--- The King and Queen will shortly be going abroad. I. *Royal yacht Victoria and Albert is to be ready for "ds Majesty's use early in March, when the King will !oar.a for a cruise in the Mediterranean. A visit will be to i) 4 the King and Queen of the Hellenes at Athens, joil„",,ilis Majesty will probably return overland after the lag at Venice, possibly staying at Copenhagen on In; irlyttY. Queen Alexandra will then be in Denmark Path lag Christian's birthday on April Bth, when "the Later Europe" will be eighty-six years of age. the eg there have been disquieting reports concerning hokuealth of the King of Denmark, but though feeble, tk "as all his faculties, and still takes an active part in Laiaff rs and government of his kingdom. !.0 " Castle of Elsinore."—lt is reported that the eo"a4ish Castle of Kroaborg, like the East Anglian is threatened by the inroads of the sea. The e"Ve, which was built by Frederick 11. towards the tlii" of the 15th century, is chiefly interesting to us as sne_Elsinore of "Hamlet," and as the birth-place of tO-Grarumaticus. from whose history Shakespeare's jagedy is said to have been derived. It was upon the ,-traee at the Castle that the ghost of Hamlet's father ZP,Peared, and fertile imagination has placed in the "einity the grave of Hamlet. But apart from these assocons the Castle has a fascinating history as the Beene of the imprisonment of the unfortunate Matilda, reen of Christian VII., and sister of George 111. of ugland, the lady whose biography has recently been tUblished anew in a volume entitled " The Queen of earS." ,h2etting up Housekeeping.—lt is generally understood t Queen Victoria provided under her will for the of Albany's children, or else some other provision dbe necessary for Prince Alexander of Teck and tifLneess Alice. The Duchess of Albany receives from foe country £6,000 a year, but this is barely sufficient lateeeping up so large a place as Claremont, which the th Queen gave her for life. It is probable that, after eir return from Cannes, where the latter part of the Are3lll°°n will be spent, Prince Alexander and Princess cline will chiefly reside for a time with the Duchess at th:2retnont. All the wedding presents are being sent p?• Amongst them are some valuable diamond and ic'nee ,t 1 ornaments given by the King and Queen, and the to and Princess of Wales, but they also include ohTIY presents of the useful kind such as furniture, I,na, carriages, linen, glassware and so on. 1,7:,,-Te new Duchess of Norfolk—Next to the Royal -"cling, that of the Duke of Norfolk has attracted Zieral interest, especially in Roe-an Catholic circles,in ch the Berries, like the Howards, occupy a foremost tr. There is some element of romance in the Nor- *, Wedding, for which Lord and La-ly Berries have ,4ell entertaining a family party at Everingham Park. ?_ Present Lord Berries married a Howard, and the h 0 of Norfolk became a constant visitor at Evering- „ani. House. Being fond of children he used to play and a:InP with his little cousins, Lord and Lady Herries two .01Ighters,both of whom were born in the same year,the in January and the other in December of 1877. But Wilas the elder of the two—the Hon. Gwendolen Max- am' who attached herself to her grown up ducal cousin, thohw'as always his especial favorite. The Duke was thou Only newly married to his first wife and little tawaght that he would some day lead his little play- es the altar.' -e rope's zeal for Reform.—The Pope is exercising hi authoritv over many things with which apparently to: Ptedestes"sor Leo XIII. was content. Tawdry orna- paper flowers, and cheap statuary are to be :Il‘ ished from the churches, together with operatic o*p and women singers. Presumably this order would xi've applied to Miss Maxwell, the new Duchess of orfolk, who for many years presided at the organ, and -lied the choir in the beautiful chapel attached to Ever- tllgllain House. Pius X. is also turning his attention to social matters, and has ordered that women are not to. atPpear in decollete dresses, at functions at which diaa°Ps and Cardinals are expected to be present. Evf- ently the Pope intends to make his authority felt with raore austerity than was the case with Leo XIII. Parliament and Gowns —Few new toilettes appear (.0 have been ordered for the opening of Parliament, the teaaon being that very little can be seen of gowns worn upon this occasion. Draughts abound, and the fair wearers were, as usual, glad to retain their wraps, Which were generally speaking, exquisite creations. These wraps, together with a brave show of jewels, are What the Peeresses rely upon to make a sensation. Gowns for the early Courts, which are in course of preparation, would appear to suggest a return to the rich brocades which for so long have been thrown into the shade by lighter and more airy-fairy materials. We !ihall all do well to see to it that only brocades of nome manufacture are purchased by us, than which none ate more beautiful, or well-wearing. Wedding Fabrics.—The favourite fabric for guests ing gowns is chiffon velvet, and crepe de chine 4'Bo crepon de soie, which is very like crêpe de chine v.it.la the faintest suggestion of a tiny rib. The most :novid colours are being chosen, and provided they are too crude or gaudy, these unquestionably afford an Zided brightness to the scene. But after the long fterl°d af soft pastel shades, that has fallen to our lot, will take some time to accustom ourselves to the viZi,d greens, reds, and purples, now deemed so modish. th'ding toilettes are all built long, that is, they sweep e, floor all round. WI. Pretty Trimming.—Chenille is in great favour. 4,°rnetlmes this is mixed with jet, when it makes an -,`n,.°.eal trimming for a black toilette, or it is introduced :th laees of the heavier make. The long straight stoles, !tall tolerated by Dame Fashion, are often threaded in 'And out with a ropily,' of chenille, whilst the ends are 5eeP1.17 fringed with the same trimming. Not a few beciond or third rate dressmakers make the mistake of isetting in these stole ends at the waist, but this idea istinctly wrong, so they are obviously intended to 'Lig straight. thime New "Fall."—The veil.or more properly speaking ° "fall," without which no hat or toque may be called jo()lnPlete, is a formidable item in the dress allowance 'l3-:,t now. It plays no part in protecting the face, ng draped about the crown, and brim of the hat, L..et.„which it falls much or little, as the wearer decides. ta"ing milliners are shewing models with the veil thZ'ag over the left side, and at the back only, and Use veilings—mostly hailing from the centre of the telerick lace industry. are eiguisite creations. Indeed 44° hat or toque so trimmed needs little or nothing T,lll the way of ornamentation. pir, PiP Machine.—Few people have ever heard of a glrnachine, or would be able to guess what it is. A was recently admitted to a London hospital with tnnrY to her hand.which she said was caused by such gr-lachine. This it appears. is used for the manufacture t:„nl: wood, of imitation seeds, which are put into jam Re s've it the appearance of being the product of chne fruit. Much has been done recent years to Reeek adulteration of food, but the adulterators have joranerally been more clever than the legislators, and the cillalPow commonly sold at the grocery stores under S o olacations such as" improved with fruit jell v" and obi 3 is a very different article Ito that which was warn totit able before adulteration was carried to the fine veeA,..,l°,w is. But though jam might consist largely of l and gelatine products. few imagined that the aarrracture of wood seeds for putting into it, was " on as a regular trade or business. 3IADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET.</head>
        <p>FISH AS AN ARTICLE OF DIET.</p>
        <p>Its Great Importance. asAll doctors agree as to the great importance of fish nahsari article of diet, but, unfortunately, there is a vQeness in the ways of serving fish that prevents its er use. We must have our palates tickled, or we do :4_?t.relish what we take. What is wanted is a greater ety in serving the commoner sorts of fish. To 3,ettlin this fish should be served with a variety of simple ,a-qY sauces. Such sauces are now within the reach aft4l, Find in Brown and Polson'sbooklet, "Simple Fish extlees," written for ordinary households, by a leading r timber of excellent recipes for such sauces ;IT given. These are quickly and easily made. and give thr, flavour. and richness to the commonest fish. The l"pening for such sauces is Brown mid Poison's Shoulda erlt," Corn flour. Application for this booklet .. be made at once to Brown and Poison, Dept. jfly Paisley, enclosing id. stamp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>That most useful article of dress, the blouse, shows no signs of losing any of its long lived popularity, and is very much in evidence for morning, afternoon, and evening wear. Its usefulness is unbounded and has become so generally recognised that the tight-fitting lined bodice is rarely to be seen except, per- haps, in the evening, when it appears in the form of the Princess gown exploited in some rich looking material such as velvet or a thick soft make of satin. Velvet is, indeed, alto- gether fashionable, but is much softer and drapes in beautiful soft folds which were un- known to the stiffer makes of former years. For dinner gowns it is fashionable, the most popular colours being black, crimson, or a deep violet which might almost be termed purple. Good lace and sometimes a little fur are used as trimmings, and a woman with a good figure never looks better than when gowned in this rich yet soft and most becoming of all fabrics.</p>
        <p>I • f - 4 N s I• •i 4k 41 a r ,ffrigt IL \-liiko„ 1 w .ro / A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AN EVENING BLOUSE:</head>
        <p>AN EVENING BLOUSE:</p>
        <p>There are also some very charming velvet blouses which are delightfully cosy for winter wear. These are generally made with deep box pleats all round, pouching slightly over a deep swathed waist band of silk, while a turn down collar of finely embroidered lawn of delicate lace, together with a smartly tied cravat, give a finish to the throat. Others again are made with insertions or motifs of labc.</p>
        <p>The pretty evening blouse depicted is of lace over a satin slip, with tiny gaugings of chiffon on the sleeves and round the decolletage, while the front between the two smartly arranged satin bows is of accordion pleated chiffon, the same material forming the frills at the elbow sleeves. A band of the sa4in ribbon passes under the arms and forms a V at the back. A blouse in this style of cream lace and chiffon td match would look exceed- ingly charming with the bow and bands of black velvet ribbon caught, perhaps, in the centre by a dainty brilliant buckle. Black velvet and cream on coffee-coloured lace con- trast most pleasingly, and can be worn with colour of skirt, although for preference I</p>
        <p>Should advise cream or black as the bodice and skirt of • different colours are not now con- sidered so modish as those 'which match or at least harmonise. Indeed it is seldom that one sees any one who lays claim to good taste wearing a contrasting blouse and skirt unless, of course, from economical motives, for nowa- days when selecting one's coat and skirt the blouse is always chosen to follow as closely as possible the scheme of colour and to avoid any unnecessary jarring note of colour. The blouse effect is noticeable in some of the smartest coats, and in the neat costume illus trted a specimen will be found of this style. The costume is of brown cloth, and is tri,mmed with black astrakan cloth and small gold buttons. The astrakan cloth is introduced in the form of a partial yoke and forms the fronts, and a trimming round the basque. The cuffs are also of the same material, and a finish is given by it to the edge pf the skirt. Brown is a colour which was not at all fashion- able a short time ago, but has again crept into favour and is much beloved by the Parisiennes. It is a tone of colour which requires careful consideration and deliberation, for there are so many varying shades and some are vastly more becoming than others. A neat brown cloth or serge tailor-made with a simple</p>
        <p>A SMART HAT.</p>
        <p>marquise hat of roughish surfaced felt to match looks an ideal toilette for immediate wear with a sable necklet and muff. A blouse of very soft dull satin simply made and worn with a deep belt of the same material and a touch of creamy lace at the throat and wrists would be the correct indoor completion of a brown cloth skirt, a bunch of scented violets tucked in the band giving a finish to the toilette. A little bunch of these delightfully fragrant blossoms gives a touch of refinement which any one can arrive at, at a trifling cost of one or two pence. Millinery is always an interesting subject, and the smart hat sketched is sufficiently original in design to merit imitation. Ostrich feathers, especially the very long ones, have been quite a feature in the more elaborate hats of this season, and those who possessed good specimens must have found them invaluable as they can be cleaned and curled and even dyed to look as good as new and to match any costume. The inarquise hat is certainly the most popular style at present, and owes its success generally speaking less to its trim- ming than to its trim smartness of shape. One striking innovation which we must all have observed in our headgear is in 'regard to the " fall" or veil nets worn with the plain little trimmed hats. These are arranged so as to fall straight from the brim over the face in the early Victorian fashion, but are mostly worn thrown back and arranged in soft folds round the brim, the ends falling down the coiffure at the back.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE.</p>
