      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIAN S The Nursing Arrangements at the = lllWorkhouse.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIAN S The Nursing Arrangements at the = lllWorkhouse.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held at Dut- ton Workhouse on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr. Joseph Stokes. There were also present :—Messrs. S. Woodward, J. L. Jones, G. Pilling, C. Roylance, S. Taylor, J. Mounfield, J. Spruce, T. Wood, A.R. Norman J. Edwards, E. Clarke, T. Darlington, T. Lewis, T. Wright, H. Mawdsley, T. Lea, G. F. Ashton (clerk),W.E. Hough (deputy clerk) T. Cook, T. Riley and W. R. De Prez (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE CENTRAL POOR LAW CONFERENCE. MR. EDWARDS OBJECTS TO.THE.SENDING7OF DELEGATES</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that he had received notice of the Central Poor Law Conference to be held in London on the 23rd and 24th inst. _ _ .</p>
        <p>.04.• Mr. Woodward moved that Messrs. Wood and Antwis be the Board's delegates. Mr. Edwards'rnoved an amendment that no delegates be sent. Mr. Pilling: Do you want to go yourself? Mr. Edwards said his opinion of these conferences was that there was "much cry and little wool." In re- gard to the vaccination question for instance, they had done nothing to convince the Local Government Board. There was no seconder to the amendment which therefore fell to the ground. Mr. Pilling said he hoped that in future when Mr. Edwards rose to make a speech he would know what he was talking about. (Laughter.) The Central Poor Law Conference .had not to deal with vaccination. It was left in the bands of the Poor Law Associations which had direct communication with the Local Government Board. The resolution was then carried, Mr. Edwards dissent- ing.</p>
        <p>THE PASTA SAPH ORPHANAGE. The Clerk read a letter from the superioress of Pantasaph Orphanage asking the Board to grant an increase in the weekly payment for the maintenance and clothing of inmates sent by the Board. The amount at present allowed was 4s. which was quite inadequate as things generally were dearer than they formerly were. Educationally, there were constant demands to keep up to date and this entailed more expense. It was re- quested that any increase granted might be added to the contribution for the past quarter. The Clerk said the superioress had previously asked for an increase to 4s. 6d. At Buckley Hall the rate was Cs. per week. Mr. Wright gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that an increased payment be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NURSING ARRANGEMENTS.-A COMPLAINT.</head>
        <p>THE NURSING ARRANGEMENTS.-A COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Northern Workhouse Nursing Association vrote that owing to complaints received that the hours of duty were too long, that the staff was inadequate and that there was not sufficient attention paid to the comfort of the nurses, his committee had on Thursday granted Nurse Muir permission to send in Ler resi gnati on. The Clerk said that when the resignation expired on March Ist there would be no nurses at the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman asked if there had been any previous communication pointing out defects. The Clerk: I don't think there has been any official communication. 'The Master said verbal complaints had been made to Mr. Norman said it ought to be clearly understoo d by the public that this was the first time the matter had been properly brought before the Board and steps would be taken to plait right. (Hear, bear.) At a later stage Mr. Wood submitted the minutes of the House Committee which included a resolution that Nurse Muir's resignation be accepted. The committee also expressed the opinion that additional accommoda- tion was required in the hospital for the numes, and also for the lying-in cases. They recommendeJ that a report be obtained from an architect as to how an extension could be made in the Workhouse generally to meet the increased requirements, and also desired to place on record that this important matter bad been under care- ful consideration for a long time, and lined been await- ing developments for a complete scheme to be considered. Complaints had, added Mr. Wood, been brought'4, before the committeel from time to time, but they had never been brought before the Board. Something would have to be done to improve the accommodation. The committee recom- mended that the Secretary of the Nursing Association be interviewed by the Chairman of the committee, the Medical Officer, and the Master, to see if the association would recommend nurses until such alterations could be made as to satisfy them; also that two nurses be advertised for at a salary of £3O rer annum each, with rations, uniform &amp;c. The Master was authorized to en- gage temporary nurses in the hospital if found necessary, before the appointment of permanent officers. Mr. Roylance seconded the confirmation of the minutes. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the minutes contained £erious business which would have to be taken in hand. The minutes were approved. Mr. Wood then gave notice that at the next meeting he would propose that Yr. William Owen, of Warring- ton, he asked to prepirre a report on the extensions required. Mr. Jones: Why shnuld we employ Mr. Owen, of Warrington, when we have local medical officers who pay rates to this Union.? I should suggest Mr. Jones, of Frodsham. Mr. Pilling: Mr. William Owen and Mr. Segar Owen are ratepayers of the Union and both reside in it. Mr. Wood pointed out that Mr. Owen was well acquainted with the arrangements at the Workhouse and had plans of all the departments.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS TO WORKHOUSE LAVATORIES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported the receipt of the following tenders for the carrying out of alterations to the Work- house lavatories, &amp;c.:—James Mort, Runcorn, £243; John Littler, Frodsham, £209; Joseph Dolan' Warring- ton, £268 10s.; J. Gerrard and Sons, !Swinton, £240; F. W. Wright, Warrington, £230 155.: Harry Fair- clougii, Warrington, £231; Samuel Appleton, Winning- ton, £207; Robert Gleave and Son, Frodsham, £225; Beckett and Company, Hartford, £249. Mr. Woodward proposed the acceptance of Mr. Littler's tender. Mr. Littler, h 3 said, was a large rate- payer, and his tender was only £2 above the lowest. Mr. Lewis seconded. Mr. Clarke moved that Mr. Appleton's tender be tccepted, but the amendment fell to the ground and Mr. Woodward's proposal was agreed to.</p>
        <p>FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, n behalf oTil-le Finance Committee. reported payments for out-relief as follows :—Mr. Cook, £80; Mr. Riley, £24 ; Mr. DePrez, £34. The account for the maintenance of orphans in the Pantasaph Orphan- age for the past quarter was £26. There were also payments in respect of children in other institutions. The receipts forithe montb.including the balance carried forward,were £1,942 4s. 9d. Afteepayment of accounts there was a credit balance of £1,527 4s. 111.</p>
        <p>MR, EDWARDS STILL DISSATISFIED</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards-a-sked whether at the last meeting uotice was given of the intention to appoint delegates to the Poor Law Conference.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Wright: notice appeared on the agenda paper. The Clerk : It was unnecessary to give notice at the Board meeting. Mr. Edwards; 'The whole thing is informal. (Laugh- ter.) Mr. Clarke: I think the mistake is that we did not appoint Mr. Edwards. (Laughter.) Mr. Edwards: No. I would not go. I would not waste my time. I think it a waste of ratepayers' money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>INSPECTION OF THE WORKHOUSE,</head>
        <p>INSPECTION OF THE WORKHOUSE,</p>
        <p>Mr. Wood reported that Mr. Danzy, Local Gu,ern- meat Board Inspector, visited the house on the 19th ult. He reported that he found the wards clean and the inmates seemed contented with their treatment. If the Guardians could see their way to putting in some new maternity wards on modern principles they would he a great addition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EDUCATION OF EPILEPTIC CHILDREN,</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION OF EPILEPTIC CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Westminster Diocesan Educa- tion Fund wrote stating that, St. Elizabeth's School, Much Hadham. had been certified for GO epileptic boys and girls under the Elementary Education (Defective and Epileptic Children) Act, 1899 by the Board of Edu- cation. The charge would be 12s. Gd. a week. The cost of the school was over £12,000. The Clerk remarked that it would not be necessary to subscribe to the institution. Childrencould be sent on the terms named in the letter.</p>
        <p>A primeval forest containing 120,000 camphor trees, measuring from seven feet to 18 feet in circumference, has recently been discovered in Formosa. It is esti- mated that the yield of camphor gum will amount to 13,000,000 pounds.</p>
