      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Mrs. Richard Daintith.</head>
        <p>Mrs. Richard Daintith.</p>
        <p>The death occurred at her residence in Sankey-street at noon on Wednesday of Mrs. Ellen Daintith, wife of Mr. Richard Daintith. The deceased lady was seized with illness on Friday last and became unconscious on Monday. Congestion of the brain was the cause of death. Mrs. Daintith had carried on business as a milliner in this town for forty-five years and had occupied the premises in Sankey-street for twenty- three years, removing there from Friars' Gate. She was the daughter of the late Mr. Joseph Hughes, who rPsided in Cairo-street. Much sympathy is expressed with the widower and family (four sons and three daughters) in their bereavement. The funeral will take place at the Cemetery this afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Defeat was again Warrington's portion on Saturday, and having tasted the same dish in three successive matches they should be just about ready for a change. No other result was expected from the Hunslet match. Goldthorpe and his men took a point from Wilders- Pool, and with their great forward strength they were expected to hold the upper hand at Parkside. Still Warrington were not by any means ten points the inferior side. Their line was only crossed twice, and the flist occasion was very fluky. Indeed it is ques- tionable whether the try should have been allowed. However, this did not affect the result, for Warring- ton never had a chance of winning.</p>
        <p>A Yorkshire writer has some very fair comments on the match. He says The game at Parkside was really a duel between the Hunslet forwards and the Warrington backs, and as is usual in cases of this sort, the forwards had the best of it. Warrington found the Hunslet eight in a mood for hard work. The home front rank pushed through, rushed through, and generally played" ducks and drakes" with their opponents, and it is to the credit of the Warring- tonians that they should have been able to keep their lines from being crossed on more than two occasions. The Wiredravrers' rear division played excellent foot- ball against overwhelming odds. Fish, Dickenson, Hallam, Isherwood, and Kenyon—the last-named had been taken from the second team through the absence of Hockenhall—being, perhaps, the most prominent."</p>
        <p>"Fish, who usually shines as a sprinter, had to resort to defenlive kicking, which was admirably judged, but the few opportunities he had of showing his running powers were invariably put to good use. On the Hunslet side Albert Goldthorpe' as usual, played well, though he was rather inclined to favour attempts at running and passing, when kicking might have been more serviceable to his side. The turf, with its thick coating of mud and slime, was not corklucive to an accurate exhibition of the handling code. As we have said, it was the forwards who won the game for Hunslet, and the Parkside authorities have every right to be proud of their splendid front rank."</p>
        <p>This bears out just what I anticipated in my notes of last Saturday when I said Warrington had nothing to fear in the Hanslet back division, but they would have to mind they were not pushed off the earth by the forwards. No blame could be attached to the Warrington scrummagers. They were overweighed and outclassed by a pack second to none in the Northern League. The most comforting reflection is that the backs put up such a good display against big odds. Had Warrington been represented by the Parkside forwards they must have put on a big score, for the men in the rear were all in good form, with th 3 possible exception of Isherwood, whose play just now is reflecting the strain a continuous football. A. rest would probably he of great bene4 to the War- rington captain. llahoney was also lacking in dash, and only on the defence could a word be said in his praise.</p>
        <p>Kenyon played splendidly and his football future seems particularly bright if he sticks to the game and takes care that his ideas do not get too big for his body. That has been the besetting fault of more than one good player I could mention off hand were I in- clined. He bad hard luck in not scoring on one occasion, while Fish also made a brilliant ,run through the Hunslet team.</p>
        <p>'Warrington are fairly well off for wing men at the present moment, as Harris has once more donned his jersey. He turned out for the" A" team on Sat- urday and received a hearty welcome from the crowd. Rochdale Rangers famished the opposition and very soon put five points to their credit. Harris scored a couple of tries for Warrinc:ton and won the match by a single point. Without him the homesters would have lost, for the second try was the outcome of a char- acteristically strong eff lrt which would have been too much for any other man on the tnam. The Rochdale lads played good football, though the weather was atrocious, and they had hard luck to lose. Hockenhall filled the centre position, bat played a poor game.</p>
        <p>Bradford reached the head of affairs in the Northern League an Saturday by a big win at Huddersfield, who now repose unhappily at the very bottom. They seem doomed to second division foot- ball, and as to who shall accompany them is an open question. Keighley have lately won three matches in succession, and stand an even chance of escape with Batley and Leigh. The championship is still at the mercy of half-a-dozen clubs, with Leeds first favourites on paper. They have two matches inland, and are only a point behind the leaders. Both teams have yet to come to Wilderspool, however, and they may find their visit here big with fate. keVould-be cham- pions have frequently met with a rebuff on the War- rington ground.</p>
        <p>Runcorn accomplished a performance on Saturday which indicates that the team is coming back into form. They went to Hull and actually defeated the Rovers at Craven-street. Though beaten forward the Cheshire men's superior combination 'behind nulled them through. Jolley and Butterworth were heroes. The defeat came as a shock to the lovers, who won at Runcorn earlier in the season, and doubt- less expected a sweet couple of points. Like War- rington, they must by now have given up all idea of the championship.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. - Against.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Bradford 23...16... 6... 1...28 48-200...11 18-76...33 Salford 23...15... 6... 2...40 55-245..11 20-82...32 Oldham 24...15... 7... 2...26 37-163... 8 14-59...32 Br'ghton R..23...14... 6... 3...16 54-191...21 18-96...31 Leeds 21...14... 4... 3...16 34-134...10 17-71...31 Hunslet 23..15. 7. 1..30 32-156...15 20-90...31 Hull K't'n R 23...12...10... 1...18 27-117...12 25-99...25 Warrington 21...11... 8... 2...20 32-136...14 21-91...24 Halifax 92 9..10. 3...15 15— 75...18 16-84...21 Swinton Runcorn Wigan - 3...13 17— 77...13 24-98...17 Widnes 24 6..14. 4..14 15— 73...24 49-195..16 Hull Batley 24 6..15. 3..15 18— 84...33 44-203.35 Keighley 5...12... 4...21 13— 81...28 43-185...14 Leigh - 2...20 21-112...26 37-163..14 Huddersnd22... 6...16... 0...10 28-104-27_44-186-12</p>
        <p>Widnes and Leigh were antagonised on Saturday, and the Mather Laners gave further evidence of their improved form by capturing a point. This match also countsd in the South-West Lancashire Competi- tion the table for which stands as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. 'W. L. D: for agst Pts Wigan Warrington 4 ... 3 ... 0 ... 1 37-14 ... 7 Widnes 7 ... 1 ... , 2 ... 3 18-26 ... 5 *St. Helens 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 0 8-14 ... 4 Runcorn Leigh • *The St. Helens matches, not being home and awair engagements, each count 4 points.</p>
        <p>Warrington should at any rate secure this cup for it must be remembered that they have yet to receive ' a visit from St. Helens, which match counts four points in the League. The fact that Wigan have already beaten St. Helens puts them at the head of affairs. By the way, Warrington will not play St. Helens on Easter Monday after all. The Saints are engaged with Runcorn on that date. Warrington have yet to visit Leigh, Runcorn and Wigan, so that they have their work cut out to secure the South-West Lancashire Cup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>– • – • • • • • • -7-7.7-77.,' • , _ • . – _ . •</head>
        <p>- • - • • • • • • -7-7.7-77.,' • , _ • . - _ . •</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>- Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>Repairing Daglish Engines at Winwick. Sir,—ln Saturday's papers there appears at consider- able length a report of a discussion in the Council ChamberresPecting the above. As some of the re- marks made by certain memhers of the Council are cal- culated to affect us detrimentally in business we feel bound to protest against them and to correct the wrong impression they are likely to make on the public. We have no objection to these repairs being given to Messrs. Pearson and Itnowles. What we do object to. is the statement that this is the only firm in the town capable of carrying out work of this description. This is grossly untrue, for as a matter of fact, we are not only in a position to deal, in the ordinary course of our business, as expeditiously and efficiently with such a repair as any firm in the trade, but, if necessary, we could have built the engine's at our works, We not infrequently carry out more extensive contracts for private firms. It was stated that the work was put in hand under circumstances of great emergency, there being danger of the supply of the town's water running short. If this be so, we should like to ask what is the use of the recently- erected Delph-lane Pumping Station when a stoppage for a few days of a portion of the Winwick plant for repairs become,wcompulsory ? There must be occasional stoppages such as this to enable repairs to be carried out. That is,we understood the very reason why the additional pumping station was required. How is it that, in the few days in which a stoppage could be safely made, local firms competent to cope with emergency repairs are not invited to tender for the work ?—We are, sir, yours respectfully (for John Melbourne, Ltd.) T. MELBOURNE. ' Wilderspool Iron Works, Warrington. February 11th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Poor Children's Treats at the Ragged</head>
        <p>Poor Children's Treats at the Ragged</p>
        <p>School.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Executive Committee of the above Mission desire to express their'thanks to the local press for giving publicity to the efforts they are making on behalf of the poor children of Warrington. There are many people of means in the town who sympathise with the miserable lot of these poor wretched children, and who desire to help them in some effectual way. Please note'that a sovereign will pro- vide a substantial meal, and an excellent entertainment (of music, singing, limelight lantern. conjuring, etc., given by willing workers) for 300 slum children. We propose to designate each treat given, by the donor's name. We do not wish to restrict these events to the children who are under the direct care of the Mission; we are willing to entertain all the poor children of the town periodically in our large and commodious build- ing. We suggest that each ward be taken in turn, and leave the work of selecting the most deserving cases to clergymen and others interested in this good work, who are resident in the respective wards. It only re- mains for those who have more than a sufficiency for themselves, to pay the cost. If they will only do this, and come and see how the children enjoy these meals and entertainments,they will be more than repaid for their outlay. We plead for help for those who cannot help themselves.—l remain, sir, yours faithfully, 12th February.</p>
        <p>JOHN W. HOWARTH (Financial Secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LOCAL OBITUARY.</head>
        <p>LOCAL OBITUARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Pemberton, Winwick-street.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. John Pemberton, manufacturing hosier, 35, Winwick-street, Warrington who passed away early on Tuesday morning. Decaased was aged 55 and was born at Blackburn. About 20 years ago he commenced attending the Warrington market, and having secured a good connection as a stocking, cardigan jacket and general hosiery manufacturer and dealer, he about 15 years ago removed to this town, and made it his centre. He continued to attend various neighbouring markets, and by attention and energy built up a good business. In his younger days Mr. Pemberton was a good tenor vocalist and a fair athlete,taking special interest in association foot- ball.He was a Liberal in politics,and when opportunity offered took an active part in connection with the I.L.P. and other movements which he believed to be for the social benefit of the masses. On one occasion he con- tested the Latchford Ward in opposition to Mr. A. Jolley, but was unsuccessful. In early years he was identified with the Swedenborgians, but ultimately became an earnest advocate of Spiritualism. He was instrumental in bringing several prominent speakers to Warrington, and after the formation of the War- rington society was elected a vice-president. This office he held up to the time of his death, which was due to heart trouble. He leaves a widow,:a son, and three daughters.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Cemetery yesterday afternoon in the presence of a large gathering of relatives and friends. The Rev. C. Harvey Cook, minister at Cairo-street Chapel,conducted the service at the chapel, assisted by Mr. W. Phillips, editor of "The Two Worlds," Manchester, Mr. Coupe, of Blackpool, and Councillor J. T. Ward, of Blackburn. At the graveside addresses were delivered by Mr. W. Phillips and Mr. S. S. Chiswell, of Liverpool. The chief mourners were :—Mrs. Pemberton (widow), Mr. Alfred Pemberton (son), Misses Gertrude and Florrie Pemberton (daughters), Mr. and Mrs. Booth (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. Herbert Pember- ton (nephew), and Mr. and Mrs. Foxcroft. The following representatives of Spiritualist organizations, in addition to the gentlemen already mentioned, attended :—Mr. W. E. Stout (preside* of the Warring- ton Spiritualist Society), Mr. John Hall (Kingsley), Mr. Jones (Ashton-in-Makerfield), Mrs. Newton (South- port Spiritualist Society), Mr. F. Tomlinson (chairman of "The Two Worlds" Company), Mr. and Mrs. Howard and Mr. Morton (Darwen), Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Tetlow and Mrs. Porter (Manchester.) The Court Liverpool 2007 of the Independent Order of Foresters (of, which , the late Mr. Pemberton was a member) WWI represented by Mr. Fred Dodd, C.R. and Mr. S. g. Chiswell, P.C.R. Mr. E. Placa (chief market inspector), Mr. W. H. Wallington (assistant market inspector), a large number of market tenants, and Councillor Arthur Peacock were also present. A number of market tenants acted as bearers. Floral tributes were forwarded by the Blackburn Spiritualist Society, the Blackburn Lyceum, the officers of Court Liverpool 2007 of the Independent Order of Foresters, Darwen Spiritualist Society, Warrington Spiritualist Society and Lyceum, the directors of "The Two Worlds" Company (of which the deceased was a 'Erector), and the committee of the Market Tenants' Association. Mr. Charles H. Piga. 1</p>
        <p>Another well-known and respected townsman passed away on Tuesday in the person of Mr. Charles H. Pigot, who carried on business as a music dealer in Bridge- street, and resided at 23, Stafford-road, Stockton Heath. The deceased gentleman, who was in his sixtieth year, had been ailing for a long time, but the end came unexpectedly. He came to Warrington from Manches- ter twenty-two years ago, and commenced business as a music dealer in Rylands-street. subsequently removing to the shop in Bridge-street. Mr. Pigot was a member of Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and was much esteemed by the congregation and by the townspeople generally. He leaves a widow and six daughters. The funeral took place at the Cemetery yesterday afternoon, the Rev. John Gunnell officiating. The mourners were Dr. Gvton, London (brother-in-law), Mr. Sydney Agate, of Manchester (cousin), Mr. Bert Pigot (nephew), Mr. George Gyton, of Blackburn, and Mr. A. Holpine, of Widnes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Albinson. We regret to announce the death of Mr. Joseph Albinson, who passed away at his residence, 13, Old, London-road. Stockton Heath, early on Tuesday morn- ing. Mr. Albinson, who was sixty-six years of age, had been unwell for some time and since Christmas had been unable to go out of doors. Cerebral hemorrhage was the cause of death. The deceased gentleman, who was held in general respect, was a native of Macclesfield and came to Warrington about forty-five years ago. He soon identified himself with the Liberal Association, and for many years was an active worker, serving as a member of the committee He had been a member of the committee of the Co- operative Society for nineteen years and took a great pride in the rogress of the organization. He was a regular attender at Latchford Baptist Church and prior to opening of that building had worshipped in the old barn used as a Baptist Church at Stockton Heath. He held the office of treasurer of the church and had also discharged the duties of superintendent of the Sunday School. For about forty years he had been associated with the Loyal Stonehenge Lodge he. , Heath) of the Order of Druids. He had passed through I all thechairs and was a trustee. Mr. Albinson had made I many friends who will deeply regret his death, Theinterment took place at Hill Cliffe graveyard on 'Thursday, the Revs. J. F. Matthews and J. S. Hughes conducting the service. The• chief mourners were Messrs Joseph, Samuel, Thomas and Alfred Albinson (hong). Mr. P. Wilkinson (son-in-law). Mr. Wilbraham and Mr. A. Few (brothers-in-law), Mr. B. Few, Mr. James Hall, Messrs. S. and T. Drinkwater (nephews), Masters James, Joseph and Fred Albinson. and A. Wilkinson (grandsons). The officers of Latchford Baptist church were represented by Messrs. W. T. and</p>
        <p>Isaac Wilkinson, George Wells, William Pairhnrst,Peter Cliffe, and William Adams. On behalf of the Co-opera- tivesociety there were present:—Messrs.T.BirchallJ.P. (president), W. Jeffrey,R. Cook, Win. Jones,T. Critchley, W.Ford, S. Dunbobbin and E. Williams(members of the committee). Others who attended included Messrs, James Strange, W. Strange, G. Heald, T. Healing, T. Hibbert, W. Knight, W. Wilkinson, J. Fairhurst and Thomas Woods. Mr. William Bibby, Mr. Roughley and others represented the Order of Druids.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 13, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 13, 1904</p>
        <p>Widnes are the visitors to Warrington to-day and a hard game should result seeing that points in both Leagues are at stake. Warrington have made a couple of important changes in the team. Harris once more resumes on the right wing, and at half-back Evans is promoted vice Mahoney from the " A " team. I have seen the Pendlebury youth play several times and he fully deserves a trial with the seniors. He can run and pass well, and I hope he will give the three-quarters plenty to do to-day. Fielding is miss- ing from the forward ranks owing to being out of work.</p>
        <p>The fall team is Hallam,fall back, Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Harris, threequarters ; Bate and Evans, half backs; Morrison,Boardman, Thomas,dook, Swift, Jolley, Naylor, and Edmondsonf orwards.</p>
        <p>The "A " team will be : Fillingham, full back ; Short, Mahoney, and Kenyon, threequarters ; Duck- worth and Hockenhall, half backs ; Forster, Fairhurst, Coleman, Harmer, Lunt, and Jolley, forwards.</p>
        <p>Most of my readers will be surprised to learn that genial "Tom "Locker leaves for South Africa to-day. He is going away on business connected with Thomas Locker and Company Limited, and will probably be absent some months. We may confidently expect him back for next season's football, however, and if he finds any good Rugby . scrummagers out there he might as well bring them .back with him. On Wed- nesday evening a nice little present of a silver cigarette case was made to him from the members of the football club,who will all,together with " Ovalist," wish him " good *luck !"</p>
        <p>On Tuesday next, Shrove Tuesday, Warrington journey to Halifax, and a rare good tussle should result. Last season,when Halifax were both champions. and cap-holders, Warrington took three points out of them, and they will have an even chance of success on Tuesday. The forwards must play the same game they did a year ago and Warrington's superior- ity behind will pull them through. lam in hopes of a gratifying result from the Halifax visit. So far Warrington's performances on Yorkshire soil have been far from creditable, the Hull match being the only one which resulted in favour of the Wirepullers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>INFIRMARY BALL</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY BALL</p>
        <p>LIST OF GUESTS.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary Ball, as it has long been designated, has always been considered the ball of the season in. Warrington. Many of the leading residents in the dis- trict combine to make the function a success, and the long list of distinguished guests shows how well they succeed. As a rule the funds of the Infirmary and Dis- pensary benefit to the extent of £5O per annum as a result of the ball, but this year a much larger sum will be realised. No fewer than 247 half-guinea tickets were issued and as a numb3r of subscriptions were also received, the Ball Committee are hoping to be able to hand over to the treasurer of the institution, a more substantial sum than usual.</p>
        <p>The ball on Thursday evening was held, as hitherto, in the Assembly Room of the Lion Hotel. There were 221 guests-10 more than in any previous year— and the accommodation was taxed to the utmost. Dancing commenced at 9 and continued until 2 o'clock. Vetter's bandi was in attendance. Supper was served in the billiard room from 11 to 1 o'clock. The catering was undertaken by Mr. John Lee, proprietor of the Lion Hotel, and gave entire satisfaction. The table decorations were in the best taste. At the centre table was a boar's head artistically treated, and bearing a flag with a red cross, worked in confectionery. The ball room had been specially decorated for the occasion. Plants and flowers were kindly sent by Mrs. Frost, Mrs. H. Greenall, Mrs. J. C. Parr, and the Warrington Corporation. The following gentlemen were the appointed stewards: Colonel A. F. G. Richardson, Lieut. Colonel J. D. Buckton, Lieut. Colonel J. A. Edelsten, Major R. L. Hartley (The King's) Major .T. D. Fairclough, Messrs. John Coxon, Henry Greenall, James Hepherd, F. W. Monks, Roger C. Parr, Dr. Reginald Smith, and Mr. W. Peter Rylands. The onerous duties of hon.secretary to the Ball Com- mittee were discharged by Mr. W. Peter Rylands, who undertookthe duties for the first time and entered into the work with zeal. The following ladies were mem- bers of the Ball Committee:Mrs.Pickmere (president), the IVlayoresss (Mrs. Bolton), Lady Greenatl,Mrs.Airey, Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. John Fairclough, Mrs. Fox, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs. Litton, Miss Litton, Mrs. Long, Miss Massy, Mrs. Monro, Mrs, J. Chsrlton Parr, Mrs. H. V. Pigot, Mrs. Rome, Mrs. W. P. Rylands, Mrs. John White. and Mrs. Willis.</p>
        <p>The appended list of guests is taken from the ball tickets, but as a few did not sign their names, it is not quite a complete list : The Mayor (Aid. Bolton) Lewis, Mr. W. R. The Mayoress Lloyd, Mr. F. J. (The 1 The Misses Bolton King's) ' Mr. W. A. Bolton Lord, Miss Lockett, Mr. J. ' Ainsworth. Miss. Lockett, Miss Airey, Mrs. ' Lockett, Miss J. Airey, Miss Airey, Mr. T. Macgillycuddy Mr. (Royal Appleton, Captain Field Artillery) Atkin, Mr. and Mrs. Mortimer. Mr. W. Agnew, Miss D. ' Massey, Miss Miller, Mr. C. J. Brocklehurst, Lady Miller, Mrs. Brocklehurst, Miss Miller, Miss C. ,Broadbent Mrs. H. Miller, Miss E. Broadbent, Miss Milling, Mr. and Mrs. H. Broadbent, Miss Sylvia Milling, Mr. F. A. Broadbent, Miss 1). Milling, Mr. T. W. Broadbent, Miss M. Marshall, Miss V. (Rat- Boumphey,Mr. J. U. ford). Blair, Mr. and Mrs. A. M. Blair, Miss Nelson, Mr. and Mrs. W. Blair, Miss T. Norris, Miss D. S. Bleckly, Mrs. H. P. Norris, Mr. T. H. Bleckly, Miss Buckton,Lieut.-Cal.&amp;Mrs. Owen, Mr. and Mrs. Seger Bottomley,,Miss Owen, Mrs. Birley, Mr. Owen, Mr. H. Bardswell, Mr. H. Owen, Miss Ruth Braddyll, Mrs. Osborne, Cap. Willoughby Braddyll, Miss Brown, Mr. W. Parr, Mrs.J. C. Brown, Miss Parr, Miss E. Bowden, Dr. Parr, Mr. Roger C. Pigot, Rev. H. V. &amp; Mrs. Crosfield, Miss Pigot, Miss F. E. Comer-Mein, Miss E. Payne, Mr. J. J. 1 Cunningham, Mr. Pickmere, Mrs. . Coon, Ir. and Mrs. Pickmere, Mr. and Mrs. E. Callender, Miss . R. (Liverpool) Callender, Miss E. Pickmere, Mr. and Mrs. Chandley, Mr. Travers 1 Curyet, iVtr. H. (Birkdale), Pierpoint, Miss Margaret , Charles, Mr. James Clynch, Captain. Roberts,Mr.and Mrs. H. 1 Roberts, Mr. R. McMinnies Dakin, Mr. A. V. Roberts, Miss E. C. 1 Dakin, Miss R. M. Richardson, Colonel Dawson, Mr. A. • Rathbone, Captain Duncan, Mr. A. Richmond, Dr. &amp; Mrs. C. E. Duncan, Miss Robertson, Mr. J. M. Dugdale, Mr. Rylands, Mr. and Mrs. W. Denham Captain Peter Davies, ?'Mrs. J. E. Rawlins, Mr. Davies, Miss D. Rowson, Mr. L. R. Dyson, Miss Rowson, Mr. R. W. Daunt, Miss . Rowson, Miss Ina I</p>
        <p>Elliot, Mrs. H. Secker, Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Elliott, Miss Shore, Mr. J. Eckersly, Miss Smith, Dr. R. and Mrs. Smith, Mr. and Mrs. A. Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. Smith, Mrs. Sidney John Stevenson, Major and Mrs. Fairclough, Miss Stewart, Mr. H. Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. Simpson, Miss It. (B'kdale) Henry Symonds, Miss Fairclough, Mr. Harry Sloane, Miss. Falcolough, Major J. D. Selby, Miss. Fairclough, Mr. Lionel Fairclough, Miss J. Tathan, Mr. C. A. (London) Fairclough, Miss Molly Theobald, Mrs. Fairclough, Miss Norah Thorburn, Miss (Peebles) Fairclough, Miss T. Thompson, Mr. Fairolough, Mr. R. Tillotson, Mr. E. (Bolton, Fairclough, Mr. E. Tillotson, Miss E. Fisher, Mr. J. H. ' Tinsley, Miss Fox; Dr. and Mrs.E. J. Tinsley, Miss M. H. Fox, Mr. Arthur Thornycroft, Mr. J. Fox, Miss Thorneycroft, Miss F'. Field, Mr. Farmer, Mr. (Hereford) Verdin, Mr. (Northwich) _ _ _ Verdin, Miss _ _</p>
