      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVE D. I</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVE D. I</p>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING ME N,</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of " Search Lights on Lantl, Labour, and Social Problems."</p>
        <p>RETALIATION.</p>
        <p>"A great many people seern to think that we are the worse-treated country in the world. That is not so. Other nations are just as much kept out of differ- ent markets as our 0w.,, and what right have we to suppose that we should succeed where foreign coun- tries have failed. If we adopted retaliatory tariffs, and we failed to break down hostile tariff walls, we should be worse off than we are at present."—Lord Hugh Cecil. The word "retaliation" has a very ugly sound, and it appeals to all that is basest and worst in human nature. If any man has done you any real, or fancied wrong, it says you must inflict a similar injury on him. If he has knocked one of your teeth out, whether by accident or design, it says you must knock one of his teeth out,and then you will be even with him. This doctriue of "an eye for an eye," and "a tooth for a tooth," and a "life for a life," was very popular when the world was young,and our hangmen and execu- tioners did a roaring trade, in avenging old quarrels, and manufacturing new ones, But I 1111170 my doubts about the wisdom of this policy and I am fully persuaded that there is a more excellent way. The policy of bludgeoning and skull tracking, and retaliation, is a relic of barbarism, and it may do very well for cannibals, and savages, and soldiers on the battle field, but it will work endless mischief if it is introduced into commercial life. I.—RUSSIA HAS GIVEN US A DELIGHTFUL OBJECT LESSON ON RETALIATION. She believes lathe policy of Protection. Her motto is "Russia for the Russians," and "the devil take the foreigner." She not merely taxes nearly every- thing that is imported, but she gives bounties to her own manefacturers, and if any nation taxes what she sends to them, she practically closes her ports against that nation's goods, by imposing heavy, retaliatory duties in return. Now let us see how this gospel of selfishness works, and who has to pay for all this folly, and wickedness? For many years she has been manufacturing bounty-fed sugar from beet root, and charging her own people twice as much for it as she would accept in our markets, and she has been buying large quantities of tea from our Indian and Ceylonese plantations. By this arrangement our con- fectionery and jam, and chocolate manufacturers in England, were able to hold their own against the world, with cheap sugar, and the Russians got some excellent tea at a reasonable price. But our muddle-headed Government signed the Sugar Convention and closed our markets against Russian sugar. And Russia to spite us, and pay us back in our own coin," retaliated" on our tea. She put a heavier duty on tea that came from India, and Ceylon, than she put on tea that came from China. Now let us look at this deal more closely, and try to find out who gained anything by it. What did we get? Well we got some dear sugar, that has already raised the price of our jam, and marmalade, and confectionery, and it will make its influence felt in every industry in which sugar is largely used. And what did Russia get?. She got some inferior tea, for which she had to pay a higher price. So you see both parties to this retaliation scheme were losers in the end, while the unlucky tea planters of India and Ceyliin, who had nothing whatever to do with Ale quarrel, lost some of their best customers. The only parties who gained anything at all were the Chinese tea merchants, who sat on the fence, and watched our quarrel, and then came in at the finish, and captured the spoils. Now I ask any sane and honest man if this game is worth the candle? I can imaKine children, or lunatics, or criminals, playing it, but I see no earthly reason why clear-headed, honest, business-men should adopt it. IL—RETALIATION HURTS THE MASSES AND IT BENEFITS ONLY A FEW INDIVIDUALS. The beauty of this game of Retaliation is that you can rob everybody all round, and yet you can make your unlucky victims believe that you are conferring favours on them. Benjamin Franklin brought out this truth veryclearly more than a century ago, and nobody has answered him from that day to this, because he is unanswerable. He asks us to suppose that Englancl is manufacturing and exporting'silk, iron, and woollen goods to Germany, France, and Belgium. Our silk trade declines, and we fly to Protection as our remedy. So we put a duty on French silks in the hope that it will increase the sale. and raise the price of our home manufactured silks. Perhaps it does confer some little benefit on the silk trade for a short time, but France retaliates. She says we have injured her silk trade with us, and to spite us, and punish us, she will damage our iron trade with her, so she increases the import duty onEnglish iron. Then ourliron work ers complain that their trade is suffering, so we put a duty on iron castingscoming from Belgium. But the Belgians will not submit to this quietly, so they retaliate by putting a duty on English woollen goods. Then our woollen manufacturers raise a row, and to put money into their pockets, we levy duties on all woollen goods coming from Germany;and in retaliation the Germans tax our silk goods. Now these four countries have retaliated all round, and they have done their best to destroy each other's industries by Protective tariffs, and what have they got as their reward? They are every one of them worse off than they were before they began thoir silly games. They have damaged each other's trades'all round, and increased the cost of living tweverybody. All four countries find their common stockof enjoyments,and the conveniences of life diminished, and they have provided plenty of materials for future quarrels and misunderstandings. Some of the apostles of Protection and Retaliation have been misquoting Adam Smith, and claiming him as an advocate of this suicidal policy. In justice to this great teacher, and clear thinker, I will give you his exact words. In his "Wealth of Nations," he says: "It may sometimes be a matter of deliberation how far it is proper to continue the free importation of certain foreign goods, when some foreign nations restrain, by high duties, the importation of some of our manufac- tures into this country. Revenge in this case naturally dictates retaliation, and that we should impose light duties on some, or all, of their manufactures coming into this country. There may be good policy in retali- ations of this kind, when there is a probability that they will procure the repeal of the high duty of which we complain. When there is no probability of such repeal, it seems a bad method of compensating the injury done to certain classes of our people, to do another injury to ourselves. Every such law imposes a real tax upon the whole community, not in favour of that particular class of workmen who are injured by our neighbours' high duties, but of some other class." It is perfectly clear from these facts and arguments that if hostile tariffs hurt us, they do infinitely more harm to the nations that impose them. lII.—FREE TRADE IS AN ENORMOUS ADVAN- TAGE TO US rN EVERY OPEN MARKET.. It is a very good thing for us that so many of our competitors are willing to handicap themselves with tariffs, and burdens that never trouble us. If they ever learn more sense, and become Free Traders themselves, we shall feel their competition very keenly in neutral markets. Our advantage as Free Traders is, that we can obtain an abundance of cheap. untaxed, raw materials, while our Protectionist competitors are handicapped with taxation from first to last. Sir John Gorst in his speech at Preston, recently pointed to India as a good example of what we can do with an open market. He said: "Our exports of manufactured cotton goods in 1902 amounted to nearly thirty-seven millions, and the largest customer we had in the world was British India, which took nearly forty per cent, of our total exports. Our next largest customers were China. Turkey, Egypt, Africa and Central and South America. British India was of enormous importance to our manufacturers. It was a Ftee Trade market, and it was open to the whole world upon the same terms as it was open to Lancashire manufacturers. There was no country which did not send goods, the manufacture of which competed with our own goods in the Indian market. The result of that open and free competition was that the British manufacturer monopolised almost the whole market, and the manufacturers of other coun- tries were practically not in it." Sir John Gorst is a convinced Free Trader, and he has a clear grasp of his subject. The textile workers of Lancashire should take to heart his warning that "if Protection were re-established in this country it would seriously endanger the cotton trade. The effect of a general protective tariff upon the cotton industry would be to raise the prices of all goods, which were used in its manufacture." It is evident from these words of warning that Sir John can see some of the pitfalls in our path if we set out on a policy of Protection. Cotton has always com- peted with woollen, and linen, successfully in our market on account of its comparative cheapness. You have only to pass laws to tax it in every conceivable form, and you will make it dearer, Then it will not hold its own in competition with its rivals, and our merchants and consumers will buy less. And if you cut off the demand you will have to lessen your supplies, and re- strict your output. Sir John says" Protection would disturb trade generally, it would inflict hardships upon the poor, whereas the benefit, if there was any, would redound to the rich and lastly, a tax on food in particu- lar. which was the essential part of the scheme, would press most harshly upon the poorest of the poor, and most heavily of all upon the children of the poor." Let me ask you to remember that there can be no retaliation on our Protective rivals. unless we are willing to submit to Protective tariffs and indirect taxation. I have never yet been able to see the logic or the honesty of retaliation. It reminds me of a story recently told by the chaplain of a convict prison. He says that when convicts are discharged from prison he gives them some good advice, and urges them to turn over a new leaf. and live honestly in future. But he is often told by these men that " they will take it out of somebody yet." They have an idea that society has treated them harshly, and they will make some indi- vidual pay for it. This is bad logic, and foolish policy, but it is about as honest as those politicians who are asking us to cut off our noses, to spite our faces, by embarking on a policy of :retaliation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PRIESTLEY CENTENARY.</head>
        <p>THE PRIESTLEY CENTENARY.</p>
        <p>UNVEILING OF A TABLET AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Paper by Dr. T. E. Thorpe, of London.</p>
        <p>Saturday marked the centenary of the death of Dr. Joseph Priestley, and Warrington did fitting honour to the memory of the great naturul philosopher and divine. Priestley spent six quiet and " singularly happy" years as tutor at the old Warrington Academy, and during that period resided in Academy- street. The members of the Warrington Societk, which has its home at the Old Academy, commemor- ated the centenary by placing a bronze tablet on the doctor's former residence which is now used for office purposes by the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company Limited. Dr. T. E. Thorpe, C. 8., F.R.S., head of the Government laboratories in London, and author of a new life of Priestley, performed the unveiling ceremony on Saturday morning in the pres- ence of a large gathering. 'The tablet, which was artistically designed by Mr. W. H. Sherleurn and manufactured by Messrs. Richmond, bears the follow- ing inscription : —" Joseph Priestley, natural philosopher, resided here from 1761 to 1767 as a tutor in the Warrington Academy. This tablet was erected by the Warrington Society, on the centenary of his death, 6th February, 1904." Mr. WILLIAM LONG (president of the Warrington Society) presided at the unveiling. He said they were nI aware that one of the greatest objects of the War- rington Society was to place on record the services of eminent men of past times who had honoured Warring- ton by their lives, or portions of their lives,so that the " man in the street," and everyone should see that such great men had lived in Warrington. He would called uponDr.Thorpe,of London,who had done them the honour to consent to unveil the tablet,to perform that interesting ceremony. Dr. THORPE said Warrington had an historical association of which those who lived in the town were justly proud. During tho last third of the 18th century there existed in thatplace a school of learning, the like of which was not to be found in any other part of the kingdom. He referred, as they no doubt apprehended, to the existence of the Warrington Academy. That academy was wealthy, not in its endowments, or in its external appointments, but in the character and in the scholarship of the men who guided its destinies. One of the principal men, whose memory. they desired to honour that day, was Joseph Priestley. He cams to Warrington in 1761, and remained one of the ornaments of the town for something like six years. During that period Priestley initiated that course of scientific dis- covery which had rendered its name immortal. It was in that adjoining house that he began'the compilation, under the direction and at the instigation of a friend of his, the ever-memorable Benjamin Franklin, a man with whom he was on terms of the most intimate friendship, and as the:outcome of that instigation Priestley turned his attention to the pursuit of natural philosophy. What he became, what he eventually did, he (the speaker) could not under the circumstances explain to them, but he hoped to have in a few minutes an oppor- tunity of dealing with the question at greater length. Dr. Thorpe unveiled the tablet, and the company then adjourned to one of Messrs. Richmond's show rooms, to hear a paper on Priestley's life and work. Mr. WILLIAM LoNG:occupied the chair and'allthied to the objects of the Warrington Society which had its home in the Old Academy, established in 1757. Of the Academy and Dr. Priestley's work they would hear a great deal more, but the Warrington Society was formed for the preservation of the old buildings, and, as far as possible in this utilitarian age,they wished to have some reminders and mementos of the times when learning was a greater desideratum in the Lancashire towns than it had bean for a long time. It was pleasant to think that the wish to know more of those times seemed to be growing amongst their people, and they would feign hope from the spread of education, which was being taken up more vigorously than ever within their time, that one good effect would be the high regard in which the men who laid the foundation of their knowledge, and who made Warrington in its day famous as a centre of thought would be held. (Hear, hear.) With the growth of manufactories little thought was taken at the time about the preservation of historical memories. The ruthless hand of trade and manufacture had been laid upon them, and the natural decay of their old buildings in Warrington made their tenure of life very uncertain, so that the Warrington Society could not do better than devote some portion of their attention to putting up inscriptions and memorializing old buildings where eminent men had lived. (Hear, hear andapplause.) The first one who was memorialize d 4 was Dr. Aikin, and the first tablet was put on the Old Academy; the second to be remembered was Joseph Priestley. Long , might the tablet which had that morning been unveiled remain to show the feeling the present generation had towards Priestley. (Applause.) Dr. THORPE said that by the death of Joseph Priest- ley there passed away from the world a hundred years ago one who played an ever memorable part in the intellectual and political development of the race. As measured by the intellectual giants of the eighteenth century he was perhaps a man of no very extraordinary mental parts. A clear and vigorous but withal! not very deep thinker, whose talents,_ many and eminent though they were, had little in them of the attributes or quality of genius, this son of a Yorkshire cloth dresser yet exercised a profound influence in the inter- lectual movement of his own age; a movement which had widened and deepened with the growth of time, reacting on the national life, and permeating and vitalising the national character. Dr. Thorpe proceeded to trace Priestley's career from boyhood onwards. The serious, sickly boy, reared in an atmosphere of Calvinism, left motherless at six, grew into a pensive, studious youth, possessed of an astounding power of mental acquisitiveness which dismayed and humbled his school masters. Before he was nineteen he had a pretty good knowledge of Latin, Greek, and Hebrew, had learnt French, Italian and High Dutch, was well grounded in algebra, the elements of natural philosophy &amp;c., and had taught himself a system of shorthand. "Even when he lisped he was a student,and he remained a student to the end of his 70 years. There was no period in his history when he was not a diligent and earnest seeker after truth in its most comprehensive sense." Priestleydwas, said DriThorpe, bybrofession a theologian, and theology was to him the highßst and noblest of earthly pursuits. In fact, theology was tis science, and he studied it in a scientific spirit. What other called "science" he regarded as his relaxation.So much was this the case.that Dr. Thorpe doubted whether he brought the same powers of his mind to the one as to the other. There could be little question that if he had concentrated those powers on the study of natural phenomena, bringing the same acumen and insight to them as he manifested in metaphysical and theological inquiry, it would not have been left to others to point out the real significance of his discoveries. Dr. Thorpe dwelt briefly upon Priestley's life at Needham and at Nantwich. Of his coming to Warrington in 1761 as a tutor in classical languages and "polite litorature," Priestley found colleagues at the Academy who were as effluent a set of thinkers as any place of learning at that time could boast of. The Warrington Academy rand the men who were its tutors played a notable part in the history of Nonconformity in England. It was there that the free thought of English Presbyteranism first began to , crystallise into the Unitarian theology; for a time it was the centre of literary taste and of Liberal politics in the district in which it was placed. at was the Arionagus in the" Athens of Lancashire." If the living at Warrington was plain and the thinking high, there was a degree of decorus gaiety and refinement, of social , charm—easy, blithe, and debonair—pervading the little community,which, as might be gleaned from the memoirs and reminiscences of the period,impressed and delighted everyone who was a witness of them. Priestley's en- trance into this circle profoundly modified the char- acter of his work. His life was now wholly engrossed in the business of teaching, and there was practically I no department of it in which at some time or other during his six years' stay in Warrington he was not called upon or did not offer to teach. As a man of letters, Priestley was one of the most industri- ous bookmakers of his age. From the Warrington period onwards, he was constantly occupied with proof sheets—indeed the closing act of his life, before he put his hand to his face to hide the last flicker of the vital spark, was to make a correction in a proof sheet. If Priestley was not himself a poet he was the cause of poetry in another. Mrs. Barbauld once told him that it was some verses of his that first induced her to write anything in verse. Her first volume of poems was composed whilst Priestley was at Warrington and was printed here. It was whilst at Warrington, that Priestley married a daughter of an ironnutster at Wrexham named Wilkinson, and it was I believed that she had most to do with his leaving the town for Leeds, which he did reluctantly. Priestley had a remarkable power of adapting himself to his environment. He was one of the most even-tempered and contented of men, and had a capacity of being cheerful under conditions that would have extorted admiration from Socrates or Mr. Tabley. (Laughter.) Dr. Thorpe alluded to Priestley's career at Leeds and Birmingham, the various stages in his religions thought, and his scientific discoveries. The Birmingham riots, he said, were a disgrace to our• common humanity. In conclusion he touchingly alluded to Priestlev's departure for America, his closing days there, and his death. Dr. ALEX kNDER GORDON, principal of the Manches- ter Home Missionary College, proposed a vote of thanks to Dr. Thorpe and gave an interesting character sketch of Mrs. Priestley. Mr. F. MONKS seconded the motion which was Iheartily carried. Alderman BENNETT proposed a vote of thanks to the I President for his services in the chair, to Mr. Sher- burn, the designer of the tablet, and to the Richmond , Gas Stove and Meter Co.; Ltd., for manufacturing it,</p>
        <p>almost on the very site of Priestley's laboratory, and for the use of the room that morning.- The alderman added that they were disappointed at the absence of several gentlemen. One was Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., who was not able to attend owing to his Parlia- mentary duties, while other absentees were the Mayor, whom he hoped to see at the luncheon,Prof. Drummond, of the Manchester College, Oxford; Prof J. Campbell- Brown, of the Liverpool University; Prof. Harold, B. Dickson, of the Manchester University, and the Rev, Morley Stevenson, principal of the Warrington Train- ing College. Mr. E. J. HALL saconded the motion, which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>-Mr. SHERBURN, in acknowledging the compliment, said that as far as he was concerned it had been a "labour of love." It was impossi6le to eliminate sentiment even from business life when working where Dr. Priestley once lived. Messrs. Richmonds, he pointed out,were now engaged in turning Dr. Priestley's discoveries to practical use. Mr. RANSOME responded on behalf of the firm and said it gave them great pleasure to contribute their small quota to the memorial to such a great man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LUNCH AT THE OLD ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>LUNCH AT THE OLD ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>A small company afterwards assembled at the Old Academy for luncheon. Mr. William Long, presided, and there were also present the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Dr. Thorpe, Rev. Alexander Gordon (Principal of Manchester Home Missionary College), Mr. C. B. Roylance Kent, Mr. F. Monks,J.P.lMr. T. Birchall,J.P., Alderman Bennett and Mr. E. J. Hall (the two hon. secretaries of the society), Mr. J. A. Ransom°, Mr. F. G. Ruddock,Mr.A. Crompton,Mr. W. H. Sherburn,Mr. J. Charles and others. Letters of apology for non-attend- ance were read from Principal Drummond, of Manches- ter College, Oxford, Professor B. Dickson, Manchester University, Professor J. Campbell Browne, Liverpool University and Rev. Morley Stevenson. A toast list was submitted. After the loyal toast of " The King" had been drunk with enthusiasm, the company, at the suggestion of the Chairman, drank in silence to the memory of Dr. Priestley. ALDERMAN BENNETT AND THE GREAT NA.111,:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OF PRIESTLEY.</head>
        <p>OF PRIESTLEY.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT, in proposing the toast of "Our Guests," said they had been anxious to honour the illus- trious memory of the great man whose health they had just drunk in silence, and while everything they had heard in connection with their local centenary celebra- tion had tended to inJrease the tenderness they had long felt for his memory, the more they learnt about him the more illustrious that memory became. He was quite sure that all over the English Empire and throughout the world the increase of years would add to the lustre associated with the great name of Priestley, and enable them to feel in Warrington some small portion of the reflected lustre of that faniefrom the fact that the townhad the honour of having had his presence amongst them for six years. They ,were indeed fortunate in securing Dr Thorpe, not only for the very eminent position he held in his own profession as a chemist, but for his (Treat knowledge of Priestley. (Applause.) As they knew, Dr. Thorpe was at present engaged on a "Life of Priestley, and what that would be like they were able to form some idea by the treat he had given them in his lecture earlier that day. Personally, he would look forward to its publication with the great- est pleasure. They were also fortunate, thanks to their President, Mr. Long, in having had the presence for a short time of Principal Gordon, who had imbibed the Priestley spirit, and was imbued with those qualities which made Priestley so excellent. They were also fortunate in the presence of other visitors. Owing to circumstances the Mayor had not had the privilege of lunching with them, but they were more than pleased to have him there for the latter part of the proceed- ings. (Hear, hear.) They knew how interested the Mayor was in everything relating to the town of War- rington, and the particular interest he took in antiquities of various kinds, and although he had said unkind things about the Academy, they knew that in his heart he was just as proud of it as they all were. (Hear, hear.) Then there was Mr. Roylance Kent, who gave a most inter- esting lecture upon Priestley before the Literary and Philosophical Society. There was also one whom he must not fail to mention—Mr. Ruddock—one of their most eminent local chemists, and a great admirer of Priestley. He coupled with the toast the names of Dr. Thorpe, Mr. Kent and Mr. Ruddock.</p>
        <p>DR'e.til'OßPE AND THE DISCOVERER OF OXYGEN I. THORPE, in responding, acknowledged the kind- ness and courtesy with which he had been received, and , the kind manner in which the arrangements of the celebration had been made to meet his convenience. He felt it a signal honour to have been asked to dis- charge the duty he had done that day. In some measure it was, perhaps, one of the greatest compliments that had been paid to him throughout the whole of his pro- fessional life. He thought the terms of his letter to Mr. Bennett must have convinced him how deeply he felt the honour, and,thanks to their very great kindness, he had been enabled to come down that morning and offer to the memory of the great man whom they all revered the little tribute of reverence and esteem which was the subject of their gathering. The next point to which he wishcd to refer was with reference to the , inscription on the tablet. He had had the temerity to ' suggest to Mr. Bennett a slight alteration to what was originally proposed to be placed on the tablet. In sug- gesting it he was really actuated by the spirit which he knew would have actuated Priestley at such a juncture. (Hear, hear.) The original wording, he believed, was "Joseph Priestley, the discoverer of oxygen." Now Joseph Priestley was a discoverer of oxygen—an abso- lutely independent discoverer of it. He owed to no man the ideas which led him to make that fundamental experiment which resulted I in the isolation of the gas, and thereby, as they all knew, found what thousands of his contemporaries had been groping for,and which was in reality, necessary to demolish that system of phil- osophy which Priestley himself thought he was, by preordination, the recognized champion. (Hear, hear). As an historical fact which had only recently come to light,'it seemed that although Priestley was an inde- pendent discoverer of oxygen, and for many years sup- posed to be the sole discoverer,the actual merit of being the first person to isolate that gas—the very corner- stone of modern chemical doctrine—was an obscure Swedish apothecary named Scheele. He was the man who first isolated oxygen—not by only one method, but by three or four methods—and had it not been .for cer- tain accidental and perfectly fortuitious difficulties in the way—chiefly financial difficulties—it was he who would have been universally credited as being the true and first discoverer of oxygen. He knew that Priestley had he been acquainted with the facts of the discovery would unhesitatingly have given tl-e palm to the man who was entitled to it. (Hear hear).</p>
        <p>Mr. ROYLANCE KENT said he consfilered it an honour and a privilege to be present.. Some years ago he as led to take in hand and write a book on the history of the English Radicals and it was in that connection that he became acquainted with Priestley's life and character. It was an extraordinary testimony to what he might call the universality and versatility of Dr. Priestley's genius and abilities that it should have been in that connectionhe (Mr. Kent) should have become interested in Priestley's career.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROD-DOCK also returned thanks for the oppor- tunity he had been given of joining in the tribute t,) the remarkable man who had made the town—certainly among scientific men—famous. The address of Dr. Thorpe had shewn them the remarkable man Priestley was, and had shown them also that Dr. Thorpe was ft remarkable man. (Hear, hear.) He only wished Alder- man Bennett had lived 50 years ago. (Laughter.) If he had they would have had Joseph Priestley's house in the same form as it was when he lived in it. Unfortun- ately, at that time they did not take the same interest as they did now in the historical associations connected with science. During the last few years there had been a great attempt to treat scientific subjects historically and to go back to the originators of many of those principal discoveries of which we were now so familiar. TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON. Mr. F. MONKS proposed "The Town and Trade of Warrington." Like themselves, he said, h. had a con- siderable interest in the good old town. He sometimes imagined what it must have been liked 2,000 years ago when the Romans were rambling about in the neigh- bourhood. (Laughter.) Still there was no doubt they had left their mark on the town. With reference to the trade of Warrington, they had it on the authority of Mr. Maythat the iron trade existed about two thousand years ago. He was inclined to think that was an archwo- rogical statement—(laughter)---and he was not much inclined to attach very much importance to the mere act of findingh bit of slag or something of that sort at Wilderspool, and it was no evidence that the iron tr de, as they understood it, existed in Warrington so early as that. He doubted very much whether there were any Monks in those days—(laughter)—although there were plenty at a later date, and no doubt they existed on the very site on which they were now met. It was pretty clear that tha Monastery that existed on Friars'-green extended to the river, so that they mialit fairly claim that monks existed and spent some of their time on that spot. They were all interested in trade, seeing that they had to get their living by it. They had heard complaints about smoke. Well, the time was some years ago when there was no smoke to complain about, hut that was a time when they complained about want of employment, and it was better *0 have plenty of employment, even if they had the smoke. (Hear. hear.) Still hemight say that they (Monks, Hall and Co.) would do the best they could to abate the smoke. (Annlanse.)</p>
        <p>Mr. BIRCHALL : Youhave donea great deal already. Mr. MONKS : But it does not pay us. (Laughter.) Continuing, Mr. Monks said they were doing their best and he believed the time would come when they would do more, and it would pay them for the expenses they were undergoing. He thought in a few months from now they would have solved the problem of whether puddling could he done without smoke. Still, some- thingi must comel out as a result, andlif it was not smoke it was something else, and a lthough he did no know what " oxide or other" it was, perhaps Mr. Mid- dock could tell them, seeing that he was an authority on the matter. Therefore, they saw that if they did not have the smoke they would have to put up with some- thing else. He coupled with the toast the name of the Mayor and Mr. Ransome. Mr. Ransome. he might say was not a Warrington man, but he was interested in the town, seeing that he was the managing director of the concern which helped them to provide the employment for the neople.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in responding, said if he had to answer</p>
        <p>for the town he would have a lot on his shoulders, (Laughter). Sometimes he almost wished Alderman Bennett had been born 50 years ago when he was making some of those long speeches. (Loud laughter.) So far as Warrington was concerned he (the Mayor) had always had the interests of the town at heart, and whatever he could do for it he would always be willing to do. He was born in Mersey-street, so he had not far to go to his native place. (Laughter.) Alderman Bennett said he had given the Academy a bad name. The statue of Cromwell, presented by Mr. Monks, had been placed in front of the Academy, and so long as the Academy was ruled and kept in order as ,Cromwell kept in order the Long Parliament, they would be all right.He hoped at some future time they would have:the lives of other eminent chemists to celebrate,and it would be all the better if they were Warrington ones. So far as olden times were concerned, he lived on the very site of one of the Roman encampments, and he often saw Mr. May knocking about. Mr. May once found a stone near his residence, and, of course, said it was a Roman I.well. Mr. May knew more about it than he (the • speaker) did, so he supposed it was all right. (Laughter.) He (the speaker) had a large collection of curiosities,. and he was afraid if he showed them all his antiquities they might wish his house was the Academy—(laughter) —and some day he might enrich the Museum with those things if he were assured that they would be appreci- ated and preserved. (Hear, hear, and applause.) But he had not yet made up his mind. (Hear, hear.) Mr. RANSOME said his knowledge of Priestley, until recently, was very small, but it had been greatly en- larged by the able address of Dr. Thorpe. When he was spoken to on the subject of the tablet, and dis— cussed the matter with Mr. Sherburn, he was very dubious as to whether they could carry 'the work out themselves. It was a very far cry from bronze tablets to ordinary domestic gas cookers, but they had a try at it. and he was gratified to see how pleased they were 'kith it. (Hear, hear.) He hoped his stay in Warring- ton would be a long one, and that he would learn a good deal more of the town and its history. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN. Mr. E. J. HALL proposed "The Health of the Chair-- man," and in doing so spoke of the objects and aims of the Warrington Society. He was sure he was express- ing the wishes of all of them when he said how pleased they were to sec!, Mr. Charles, the well-known Warring-- ton artist with them that afternoon, and he hoped the' • time would come when Mr. Charles would rival Mr. Henry Woods, Mr. Luke Fildes, and other eminent members of his profession. (Hear, hear.) Mr. LONG briefly acknowledged the compliment and. the interesting proceedings terminated,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE GARDEN SOCIRM—The usual monthly meeting of this society was held in the Church Room on Tues- day evening last Mr. Charles Plinston being voted to the chair. The draft -prize schedule prepared by the committee was read and adopted, there being several new classes and special prizes offered. The date of the show to be held in the Church Room was fixed for the second Saturday in August. After some further routine business, Mr. NV. H. Woodcock read a short paper on the potato which was very instructive and listened to with much attention. The meeting closed with the usual vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>The Manurial Value of Sewage. The Native Guano Company, Limited. Blackfriars, London, have favoured us with a copy of their pub- lished results of the practical application of native guano in the farm and garden. This manure is extracted from town sewage, and its effects show it to be the natural manure for the soil, returning to it what has been taken from it. The sewage from which this guano can be obtained is, in many places, and especially in this district, neglected, wasted, and allowed to become a source of pollution to rivers and streams. The directors of the Native Guano Company invite the assistance and co-operation of all who are interested in the subject. It is contended that the multiplication of works for the treatment of sewage by their system would not only improve the sanitary conditions of towns, but what is now a nuisance to toivns would beCome a source of profit to the country generally, p,nd especially to farmers, who would have a valuable manure produced almost at their own doors. till four. It was also arranged that Inspector Hackett should call once a week and write in the register what he knew of the applicants, and whether they were clean and respectable. It is earnestly hoped that ladies will take up the scheme and try to find work, washing, charin,ff &amp;c., for those who need it, for unless there is a demand as well as a supply,of course nothing can be done to help the many who require a start in life. Ladies can call at the office at any time, and Mr. Hayes, of the Gymnasium, who will keep the book from Tuesday, March 16th, will give them all information. If when suited they will send is. to Miss Broadbent, the treasurer, it will make the office self-supporting, as the expenses will only come to about S:3 a year.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. W. Variey a Passive Resister. ; The Rev. J. W. Varley, Baptist minister, St. Annes, was summoned at Lytham on Thursday for non- payment of a portion of the education rate. Owing to the court being occupied with the licensing ses- sions, the cases were heard in the solicitors' room. Mr. Varley protested against such" hole-and-corner" work, and said the cases should either be taken in open court or adjourned. Warrants were issued.</p>
